HT_DATA.load("detail", {"ORzNZUeUHAM":{"wy":"HN links it constantly as the standard introduction to stroads and Strong Towns-style arguments about car-centric design.","q":{"x":"This video from the Not Just Bikes channel shows the stark difference between a street and a road","i":29505394,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":46135587,"author":"oblio","month":"2025-12","story":"Accepting US car standards would risk European lives","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Solid points despite the snarky tone.","quote":"They're very solid.","text":"Watch his video. Pick 2-3 at random, be a grown up and ignore the tone of voice and the snarkiness, and see what his points are. They're very solid. Try something like this: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27727210,"author":"benrbray","month":"2021-07","story":"The Growth Ponzi Scheme","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent series on Strong Towns ideas.","quote":"also has an excellent series of videos based on work from Strong Towns","text":"The Not Just Bikes [1,2,3] YouTube channel also has an excellent series of videos based on work from Strong Towns. I link to these videos whenever a friend asks why I chose to leave the United States. :) [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":36922288,"author":"snovymgodym","month":"2023-07","story":"If we want a shift to walking we need to prioritize dignity","stance":"rec","why":"Good take on dangerous and inconvenient US walking.","quote":"this take on the topic is pretty good","text":"I agree that the \"US is a rural country\" thing isn't true, but the way most US cities are built make walking either very inconvenient or outright dangerous. Posting this channel in this sort of thread is basically a meme, but this take on the topic is pretty good: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32188450,"author":"xputer","month":"2022-07","story":"Why we ignore thousands of daily car crashes","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the US not creating safe road infrastructure.","quote":"Watch some videos from this channel and you'll be able to see how the US definitely does not do enough","text":"Watch some videos from this channel and you'll be able to see how the US definitely does not do enough in terms of creating safe road infrastructure for all: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":30906857,"author":"dashundchen","month":"2022-04","story":"A smartphone app that allows public to submit evidence of speeding drivers","stance":"rec","why":"Great coverage of the terrible stroad.","quote":"Not Just Bikes does a great job covering the terrible \"stroad\".","text":"I would say in the US, our road design means our speed limits are way too high for safe travel. Speed limits are set high with no attempt at reducing points of conflict. It's a meme at this point, but Not Just Bikes does a great job covering the terrible \"stroad\". [VIDEO] Most places in the US are filled with wide, multi-lane roads posted for speed limits of 35-45 mph. But those roads will be packed with unsignaled driveways, where drivers entering the road needed to make unprotected and right left turns with and against traffic that's likely going 45-55mph, and drivers on the road need to predict the behavior of cars coming from almost any direction. Meanwhile, a thin stripe of white paint gets added to the gutter and is somehow called a bike lane - like anyone wants to ride their bike at the same grade as 2+ ton trucks flying by within a foot of your handlebars. Those same lanes will frequently disappear or reappear within a shoulder, and at intersections will suddenly cross or dump you out into a right turn lane. Better hope that driver turning right noticed you before you get hooked. It's even worse for pedestrians, where even in dense cities, a walk signal is by no means a safe time to walk. Unsignaled left and right-on-red means a car will likely fly through their path at any given moment. On your way to this intersection, you'll continuously cross driveways and slip lanes with curve radii to encourage speeding, sidewalks that dump you into parking lots (if they exist a"},{"id":31055756,"author":"spaethnl","month":"2022-04","story":"Virginia police routinely use secret GPS pings to track people’s cell phones","stance":"rec","why":"Great video on stroads and their problems.","quote":"Not Just Bikes has a great video about them here","text":"There are typically two kinds of roads these speeds are reached in the US: 1. Expressways, where pedestrians and cyclists are not allowed. They are walled off with specific entrances and exits. 2. Country highways, where pedestrians and cyclists are nearly unheard of, and are typically very easy to spot from a distance and adjust accordingly. There is a third area where these speeds are sometimes reached in urban areas that Strong Towns calls \"stroads\", and these have a whole host of problems. Going 10mph over the speed limit in these areas probably doesn't make a huge difference, because the are already so pedestrian and cyclist unfriendly to begin with. Not Just Bikes has a great video about them here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29759941,"author":"gregors","month":"2022-01","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2021?","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation on stroads in the US and Canada.","quote":"The Ugly, Dangerous, and Inefficient Stroads found all over the US & Canada","text":"This is pushing it as far as \"talk\", but more of a presentation. The Ugly, Dangerous, and Inefficient Stroads found all over the US & Canada [VIDEO]"},{"id":29505394,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-12","story":"How do you fix a stroad?","stance":"rec","why":"Contrasts streets and roads in the Netherlands with stroads.","quote":"This video from the Not Just Bikes channel shows the stark difference between a street and a road","text":"Except streets and roads are designed to solve different problems: > Streets: The function of a street is to serve as a platform for building wealth. On a street, we're attempting to grow the complex ecosystem that produces community wealth. In these environments, people (outside of their automobiles) are the indicator species of success. Successful streets are environments where humans, and human interaction, flourish. > Roads: In contrast, the function of a road is to connect productive places. You can think of a road as a refinement of the railroad — a road on rails — where people board in one place, depart in another and there is a high speed connection between the two. > Stroads: Stroads are a mash-up of these two types of paths. We like to call them \"the futon of transportation\" because, just as a futon is neither a particularly good bed nor a particularly good couch, a stroad is neither a particularly good road or a particularly good street. * [LINK] A high-speed, high-volume thoroughfare with minimal obstructions to flow will have its per-hour capacity decimated by having to stop every couple of hundred meters by traffic lights and crosswalks. On the pedestrian side, who wants to cross an area where cars travel 60 kph (or higher): what happens if they don't stop? This video from the Not Just Bikes channel shows the stark difference between a street and a road (using examples in the Netherlands) and stroads: * [VIDEO] If you make a place easier to get to primarily by c"},{"id":29302260,"author":"drivers99","month":"2021-11","story":"The Stroad","stance":"rec","why":"Introduces stroads and Strong Towns ideas.","quote":"Here’s Not Just Bikes’ video about Stroads","text":"Cool seeing strongtowns here. Not Just Bikes is a YouTube channel that has helped popularize them recently, and has a lot of other videos about what makes living in the Netherlands so great (i.e. it’s not just bikes, although that’s a big part of it). Here’s Not Just Bikes’ video about Stroads: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28790274,"author":"bodo11","month":"2021-10","story":"Is Ford about to reinvent the bicycle derailleur?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why cycling may be poor in your area.","quote":"For another view point of why biking may be shit in your area, I invite you to watch a few videos of this channel","text":"While I agree that more standardisation would be beneficial, especially regarding electronics(!), the rest of the bike is fairly standardized (wheel / bottom bracket / pedal screw / steering column diameter etc.) . Unfortunately, I think the introduction of electronics via ebikes and other \"new\" technologies for the bike are diluting that standardisation in my perception. For another view point of why biking may be shit in your area, I invite you to watch a few videos of this channel: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28134660,"author":"vinceguidry","month":"2021-08","story":"Culdesac Tempe: The first car-free neighborhood built from scratch in the US","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"it really needs to be spread out more","text":"Lack of demarcation makes things safer by raising the awareness of the people around you. Making things 'safer' actually reduces safety. I've posted this video on another thread, but it really needs to be spread out more: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27543262,"author":"panopticon","month":"2021-06","story":"Kids need freedom, too","stance":"rec","why":"Covers deficiencies of suburban road design.","quote":"This is a good video on the deficiencies of suburbia road design if you're curious","text":"> Well, the roads are safe (for cars). Not necessarily. Your risk of being in a fatal car accident is nearly 3x higher on suburban roads than urban roads. This is a good video on the deficiencies of suburbia road design if you're curious: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39244395,"author":"canucker2016","month":"2024-02","story":"Paris Vote Targets SUV Drivers with Parking Fees Set to Triple","stance":"reference","why":"The stroad-related video.","quote":"Here's the stroad-related video","text":"I've never encountered the term 'stroad' until I watched videos from an urban-planning-related YouTube channel, NotJustBikes, [LINK] . Here's the stroad-related video: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":37434548,"author":"notjoemama","month":"2023-09","story":"I brought down a scamming operation with 15 bytes of PHP","stance":"reference","why":"Critiques of US and European road design.","quote":"I saw a couple great critiques of road and city designs comparing the US and certain places in Europe.","text":"City/road design, greater distances of travel, higher speeds. No character, moral, or legal failing required. Some designs work better in some contexts than others. I wish I could remember the YouTube channel but I saw a couple great critiques of road and city designs comparing the US and certain places in Europe. Ah ha! I found it searching “stroads”, this is part of a series: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31473625,"author":"TheRealPomax","month":"2022-05","story":"Pedal Me bans staff riders from wearing helmets for safety reasons","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"You need a helmet, because stroads[1] guarantee accidents.","text":"They're marginally effective on Dutch roads , which were overhauled to be cyclist-safe decades ago, with new roads being safe by default. The Dutch approach unfortunately does not reflect even remotely on North American cities. You need a helmet, because stroads[1] guarantee accidents. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31103343,"author":"miketery","month":"2022-04","story":"A few concrete arguments for e-bikes","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Infrastructure caters too much for cars. If we had real streets, this wouldn't be an issue.","text":"> Chronic use on infrastructure meant for pedestrians. The issue is Stroads [1][2]. Infrastructure caters too much for cars. If we had real streets, this wouldn't be an issue. I was just in Nashville, and they have scooters you can use. It felt 10x more dangerous than me biking at high speed in NYC. Even though there was a bike path, it was next to cars going 50mph, not fun, nor safe. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29466444,"author":"Gwypaas","month":"2021-12","story":"In 2019 40% of San Francisco traffic fatalities are from left turns","stance":"reference","why":"Covers stroads in the US and Canada.","quote":"The Ugly, Dangerous, and Inefficient Stroads found all over the US & Canada","text":"You have to separate the road from the stroad. Roads in Europe might be up to 60-70 kph even in a city center, but they also have calming intersections with roundabouts and to get on an off regularly or even ramps. Streets tend to be 30-40 kph depending on the place with lots of calming. The Ugly, Dangerous, and Inefficient Stroads found all over the US & Canada: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29084305,"author":"mperham","month":"2021-11","story":"The story behind Flexport founder's supply chain tweets","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates road maintenance burden and car-centric infrastructure.","quote":"Roads are a massive treadmill of maintenance.","text":"We can't afford it. Roads are a massive treadmill of maintenance. To step back, why do we have so many square feet of road space? Why do we keep expanding highways when it is proven that it does not help? The automobile and its inefficient infrastructure is bankrupting this country. We keep building more roads rather than investing in transportation infrastructure which is far cheaper in the long run. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28072343,"author":"xorfish","month":"2021-08","story":"The slow collapse of Amazon’s drone delivery dream","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why stroads are bad as both street and road.","quote":"Stroads are ugly, inefficient and dangerous.","text":"Obstacles are only one of the ways you can calm traffic. Planting trees near the streets, narrower lanes, speed humps, roundabouts are all measures with a proven record that they improve safety. Crossings should also be one lane at a time. > In all my experience with high-visibility roads, even though you'd expect people to be better able to pay attention to everything on road with good visibility and clear markings, they actually seem much worse in those conditions This is nothing new. You shouldn't design a street like a road. You will end up with something called a stroad, that is bad at both beeing a street(a place where you can live, shop and whatever) and a road(something that connects two places) Stroads are ugly, inefficient and dangerous. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27027111,"author":"bratcomplex","month":"2021-05","story":"Co-founder of Neuralink leaves the company","stance":"reference","why":"Separating streets and roads is safer and more profitable.","quote":"As a result they are safer, cheaper, and land use has a higher tax revenue.","text":"they set standards for what a street, road, and highway are. and they do not mix them. As a result they are safer, cheaper, and land use has a higher tax revenue. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bnKIVX968PQ",2],["uxykI30fS54",2],["K3lP3BhvnSo",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["1FXuMs6YRCY",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["-ZDZtBRTyeI",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["lBVvnDfW2Xo",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["4j_UjIshzMc",1],["NSaRb-iFwPA",1]]},"dljNabciEGg":{"wy":"HN people point to it as the best demonstration of literate devops, showing documentation and execution woven together in Emacs.","q":{"x":"His video on literate programming for DevOps via Emacs' Org-mode was very impressive and inspiring to me. It's my favorite.","i":36962621,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36962621,"author":"pxc","month":"2023-08","story":"Emacs is my new window manager","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Impressive, inspiring literate devops via org-mode.","quote":"His video on literate programming for DevOps via Emacs' Org-mode was very impressive and inspiring to me. It's my favorite.","text":"This is the blog of Howard Abrams, who also has a great YouTube channel full of Emacs demos and tutorials. His video on literate programming for DevOps via Emacs' Org-mode was very impressive and inspiring to me. It's my favorite. :) [VIDEO]"},{"id":31029794,"author":"kkfx","month":"2022-04","story":"A Dotfile History","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Emacs power in action.","quote":"Try seeing this","text":"Personally I've failed with Emacs years ago, just tried it and decide that's a heavy and horrid mess, I re-tried it few years ago when I see it in action and was at that point in time that I realize it's power. Emacs is one-tool one-job BUT the tool is just a function and functions can be combined like unix commands in pipes but in far more powerful manner and with far less overhead. Try seeing this: [LINK] or this [VIDEO] the real issue when starting without a friend of video-tutorials is that Emacs defaults are barebone and really old, the juicy things come only when you discover them. The power of Emacs is not much Emacs itself but the classic desktop paradigm: anything is integrated so anything can be combined. You type some math in a mail? Why not have it rendered by LaTeX, since your system do have LaTeX support installed and why not solve the ode you are typing since you have a CAS? Why being tied on GUIs to cut&paste as the sole IPC? That's is. Is extremely powerful but so different than actual environments that most users fail to comprehend without seeing it in action..."},{"id":23723594,"author":"elviejo","month":"2020-07","story":"Ask HN: What do you use to write documentation? LaTeX? Word? Markdown?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows weaving instructions and their execution together in Emacs.","quote":"In this video [0] by Howard Abrahams you can see how to wave the instructions and the execution of them with Emacs.","text":"Org-mode in Emacs. Because it enables to do literate programming. In this video [0] by Howard Abrahams you can see how to wave the instructions and the execution of them with Emacs. Another advantage is that you can mix your diagrams (in plantuml or ASCII art) with your code, in the same org-mode file. Gitub displays .org files the same way it does Markdown. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17254999,"author":"ScarZy","month":"2018-06","story":"Remote Only","stance":"rec","why":"Documenting workflow with commands and output for remote teams.","quote":"The stuff that Howard Abrahams discusses on the below video is something I think would be highly successful in a remote environment.","text":"This is the main reason I want to try and learn Emacs, OrgMode, and Babel. The stuff that Howard Abrahams discusses on the below video is something I think would be highly successful in a remote environment. Documenting your workflow as you go, including commands and output, then emailing it to your team that day or uploading it to a document store/gitbook: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17007811,"author":"unhammer","month":"2018-05","story":"ZSH, tmux, Emacs and SSH: A copy-paste story","stance":"rec","why":"Neat demo combining org-babel and tramp.","quote":"is a pretty neat demo of combining org-babel and tramp","text":"All these workarounds sound like a reason to use start using tramp more (apropos, [VIDEO] / [LINK] is a pretty neat demo of combining org-babel and tramp). ----- There is also emamux, which seems to be able to copy from tmux: [LINK]"},{"id":15773801,"author":"deerpig","month":"2017-11","story":"What Is a Computational Essay?","stance":"rec","why":"Code in documents configures local or remote servers via Babel.","quote":"Howard Abrams has extended the concept to create literate devops","text":"Literate programing is used to tangle text and code. You tangle the document and the code blocks are pulled out to create a working program. But Orgmode's Babel, iPython and Wolfram's Computational Essays are literate computing, with code being executed in the document. Howard Abrams has extended the concept to create literate devops ( [VIDEO] ) using orgmode babel where the code in the document is used to configure software running on local or remote servers. I use Babel as part of my daily workflow. Very cool stuff."},{"id":10926067,"author":"julienchastang","month":"2016-01","story":"Org-Mode for Writing: Structure and Focus","stance":"rec","why":"Literate devops with org-babel.","quote":"Also checkout this literate devops video","text":"I could go on and on about the benefits of org. And the org-Babel full fledged literate programming environment is another game changer. I used org for a scientific conference I attended recently. From one org file, I could provision a VM in the cloud and get various Docker containers running via tangling with plenty of human friendly exposition. (See tramp mode too.) I could then export that file as an extended abstract via Latex export (conforming to the conference formatting guidelines) and also produce attractive HTML (see ReadTheOrg). Also checkout this literate devops video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10637911,"author":"julienchastang","month":"2015-11","story":"Worg, the Org-Mode Community","stance":"rec","why":"Literate devops using org-babel.","quote":"Check out this literate devops video","text":"I use org-mode everyday, as well. And the org-babel literate programming environment is a game changer, and something I find new uses for all the time. Check out this literate devops video [1] But I share your sentiment for the stability issues which I think reflect problems with emacs in general. One day, you update your packages and suddenly your favorite mode does not work anymore and you have to spend 1/2 day figuring out why literally digging into the source with edebug. Because of this I take the following measures. I use melpa-stable which as the name indicates are the stable versions of emacs packages. (The problem is not all packages are in melpa-stable so you have to deal with that too.) Also, I manage my own version of org-mode cloned from the canonical org-mode repo that I update when I decide and I can back out of if there are problems. Finally, I run emacs within docker which allows me to freeze dry and rehydrate environments that are completely under my control so that I don’t encounter problems with a changing environment either. Here is my literate org-babel .emacs, if interested [2] (I don’t have the Dockerfile on github, yet, unfortunately.) [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":10281948,"author":"brudgers","month":"2015-09","story":"Emacs Artist Mode","stance":"rec","why":"Example of what org-mode code blocks can do for literate devops.","quote":"Howard Abrams literate devops is an interesting example of what org-mode code blocks can do.","text":"Emacs org-mode supports a lot of languages [1] and you can mix and match within a file. Howard Abrams literate devops is an interesting example of what org-mode code blocks can do. [2][3] Org-mode also allows embedding images or source blocks in languages like LaTeX that resolve to rendered images. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44472965,"author":"brudgers","month":"2025-07","story":"Ask HN: What clever tools/scripts do you use to manage development environments?","stance":"reference","why":"Literate devops reminiscent of managing infrastructure.","quote":"Immediately reminded me of this (which I am no where near clever enough to use)","text":"managing infrastructure services Immediately reminded me of this (which I am no where near clever enough to use): [VIDEO]"},{"id":44323964,"author":"txgvnn","month":"2025-06","story":"Literate programming tool for any language","stance":"reference","why":"Example of literate programming in Emacs.","quote":"Example","text":"If you are interested with Literate programming, you should try Emacs. Some packages are org-mode, eev and even elisp are best for literate programming. Example [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["0Rnq1NpHdmw",1],["KqFNbCcyFkk",1],["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["YX3iRjKj7C0",1],["CZIINXhGDcs",1],["jtMWEiCdsfc",1],["Tl9kD693ie4",1],["FV0DXNB94NE",1],["Av0PQDVTP4A",1],["Nbj1AR_aAcE",1],["-Ht4qiDRZE8",1],["9-IWMbJXoLM",1],["agzNANfNlTs",1],["dPHPNgIihR0",1]]},"xsSnOQynTHs":{"wy":"Seen as the canonical Redux introduction and a model live-coding talk about better developer tools.","q":{"x":"One of my favourites I watched recently was Dan Abramov introducing React hot loading, Redux and Redux Dev Tools in the same 30 minute talk","i":16838949,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16838949,"author":"mcintyre1994","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Covers hot loading, Redux and Redux DevTools in one 30 minute talk.","quote":"One of my favourites I watched recently was Dan Abramov introducing React hot loading, Redux and Redux Dev Tools in the same 30 minute talk","text":"One of my favourites I watched recently was Dan Abramov introducing React hot loading, Redux and Redux Dev Tools in the same 30 minute talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10562741,"author":"jongalloway2","month":"2015-11","story":"Writing code doesn't mix well with oration","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Exemplary live-coding talk where slides are driven by application state.","quote":"Dan Abramov's talk on Redux and hot loader at ReactEurope really nails live coding for maximum effect","text":"Dan Abramov's talk on Redux and hot loader at ReactEurope really nails live coding for maximum effect: [VIDEO] His presentation is web-based, so his live coding interacts with his \"slide-ware\", e.g. changing slides is accomplished by setting application state."},{"id":9944127,"author":"GordyMD","month":"2015-07","story":"Ask HN: Can you suggest some good JavaScript projects to read and learn?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains the motivation behind React Hot Loader and Redux.","quote":"Watch Dan Abramov's talk [1] about Redux (Reducers + Flux). Gives great insight into his motivation for creating React Hot Loader and Redux.","text":"Not a readable resource, but I feel it is worth the exception - Watch Dan Abramov's talk [1] about Redux (Reducers + Flux). Gives great insight into his motivation for creating React Hot Loader and Redux. +1 for Dan (aka Gaearon) being an excellent developer [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31782511,"author":"swyx","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Best dev tool pitches of all time?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among favorite smaller dev tool pitches.","quote":"Redux","text":"i also asked this on twitter so just putting some replies here: - Doug Engelbart [LINK] - Twilio [LINK] - Stripe - \"7 lines of code\" - Netlify - [LINK] - Heroku - [LINK] and git push heroku master - Cloudflare - [LINK] - Node ( [LINK] ) and Deno ( [LINK] ) - Firebase (reportedly) - [LINK] smaller companies/less impactful pitches that i still like - Redux [VIDEO] - Stackblitz [LINK] - Comm [LINK] - Mongodb [LINK] - Let's Encrypt [LINK] - Figwheel-Clojurescript [LINK] - Svelte [LINK]"},{"id":15743237,"author":"sailfast","month":"2017-11","story":"How Redux Works: A Counter-Example","stance":"rec","why":"Helpful context for how to think about state with Redux.","quote":"here's what I see with state, and here's how I think about it and did the thing","text":"Yeah - I can see that. \"It's all functions so it all works\" haha. I think the context setter that helped for me was to see \"hey here's what I see with state, and here's how I think about it and did the thing\" at React EU: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11413835,"author":"iheartmemcache","month":"2016-04","story":"Introducing React Storybook","stance":"rec","why":"Canonical talk on hot reload and time-travel debugging.","quote":"is the canonical talk on it, certainly worth a watch.","text":"Dan Abramov's DevTools[0] with Hot Reload and \"Time Travel\" (historical debugging) is basically the same thing too, though its tied to Redux pretty heavily IIRC. So yeah, nothing new. (\"TimeWarp OS\"[1] was a project developed in the late 80s that did the same thing at the OS level, primarily for physics simulations. (Something would break, you'd go back to state foo, change parameters mu,delta,sigma to yield foo' and continue the run.)) [0] [VIDEO] is the canonical talk on it, certainly worth a watch. [1] [LINK] Brian Beckman formerly of Microsoft as a second author. All those RX features I'd imagine were done largely in part by him and De Smet."},{"id":10944278,"author":"eicnix","month":"2016-01","story":"Ask HN: Best (Tech) talks from 2015","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on hot reloading ideas applicable to other stacks.","quote":"I find this talk very interesting","text":"As somebody who hasn't worked with react before I find this talk very interesting: [VIDEO] He talks about improving the development workflow by hot reloading parts of the application during runtime. Slow workflows affect every developer and the idea from this talk can be used to build tools for other stacks or languages."},{"id":10334675,"author":"GordyMD","month":"2015-10","story":"The 3REE Stack: React, Redux, RethinkDB and Express.js","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Redux motivations centered on better debugging and developer tools.","quote":"Check out his talk he gave at React-Europe. He basically is talking about improving developer tools.","text":"I think if you take a lot at the motivations that Dan Abramov had for creating Redux [1], you'll see that improving debugging was at the centre of it. Check out his talk he gave at React-Europe. He basically is talking about improving developer tools. The functional approach Redux takes allows for you to see state transitions and replay actions - making it relatively trivial (compared to say Angular.js) to understand how to reproduce a buggy state. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10365552,"author":"kmg","month":"2015-10","story":"Ask HN: Which is best React or Angular?","stance":"rec","why":"Great introduction to Redux with hot reloading and time travel.","quote":"Live React: Hot Reloading with Time Travel introducing Redux is great.","text":"Though I don't have experience with a large rewrite like this. I am really liking what I see in React. React + Redux for the Flux pattern seems to be the best combination available right now. Watch this talk by Jessica Kerr - Functional Principles In React [LINK] about why Functional / Declarative approach is good for front end development. Also Dan Abramov (Redux Author) Talk - Live React: Hot Reloading with Time Travel [VIDEO] introducing Redux is great. Watch this awesome talk by Ryan Florence - Don't Rewrite, React! [LINK] on how to approach rewrites bottom up and he rewrites a part of the TodoMVC written in Backbone.js."},{"id":9833022,"author":"danabramov","month":"2015-07","story":"Redux: Atomic Flux with Hot Reloading","stance":"rec","why":"Redux author's talk demoing hot reloading and time-travel devtools built on reducers.","quote":"I wrote Redux for my React Europe talk about Hot Reloading and Time Travel","text":"Hey, lib author here. Let me first explain Flux in five seconds. Instead of mutating your models, any data mutation in your app is described as a plain object describing “what happened” (e.g. { type: LIKED_POST, postId: 42 }) and broadcasted globally. Models (called Stores in Flux) have no setters and change their internal state in response to specific mutation objects (called Actions). The goal of Flux is to have single source of truth and make it easy to avoid race conditions and cascading updates. If some data is wrong, just repeat the same Actions in the same order, and you'll get the same data in Stores. Very easy to debug. Now, for Redux. It keeps the properties of Flux (mutations described as objects), but instead of Stores mutating internal state, it has Reducers—pure functions with (state, action) => state signature. While writing Flux apps, I noticed that the _essence_ of every Store I ever wrote was a reducer function. It turns out that using pure functions instead of event emitters has many benefits: they are composable ( [LINK] ), it is possible to hot reload their logic ( [LINK] ). I wrote Redux for my React Europe talk about Hot Reloading and Time Travel ( [LINK] ). It got popular before I explained _why_ I made it. In my talk I show what kind of powerful devtools it is possible to build on top of Redux. The video of my talk will be up soon, you can check when it appears here: [LINK] . Also check out my article on the subject: [LINK] EDIT: THE VIDEO IS UP! [VID"},{"id":9857641,"author":"lingojs","month":"2015-07","story":"Angular vs. React – the tie breaker","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on Flux-style state that convinced the commenter to try it.","quote":"the presentation has convinced me to try it out on my next project","text":"Directives are replaced by React components. I would argue, IMO, that directives are actually a half-assed implementation of components. In terms of services, I would recommend using Flux to perform actions / store your state. This is what a lot of developers are leaning towards these days. There was a great talk at React Conf about Reflux, I've never used it, but the presentation has convinced me to try it out on my next project: [VIDEO] When you build with React, imagine building an application in Angular using only Directives. It isn't that simple, but that is sort of the idea with React components. I started with Angular (and still work day to day with it at work) but if someone asked me how I would architect an application tomorrow, I would go with Flux + React + ES6, no questions asked. Also, Angular2 is heavily influenced by React, so learning React now will allow you to have a better understanding of Angular2."},{"id":9968619,"author":"adeptima","month":"2015-07","story":"Flux Architecture Visual Cheatsheet","stance":"rec","why":"Points to Redux as a more current alternative to older Flux implementations.","quote":"Check Redux","text":"This is out-dated from my perspective. Check Redux [VIDEO] [LINK] Many Flux implementation lucks of isomorphic, proper hot-reloading, etc. ES6 transition is on its way. Most code will be abandoned. Alt, Reflux, etc are all in trouble."},{"id":9970288,"author":"findjashua","month":"2015-07","story":"Flux Architecture Visual Cheatsheet","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk introducing Redux as reducers plus Flux.","quote":"He also gave a very good talk on it at react-europe","text":"If you're wondering about which flux implementation to start with, I'd highly recommend checking out Redux : [LINK] The main idea is to think of stores as reducers (redux = reducers + flux). He also gave a very good talk on it at react-europe: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28544915,"author":"NiceWayToDoIT","month":"2021-09","story":"Show HN: Time travel debugger for web development","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as an example of time-travel debugging, alongside Bret Victor.","quote":"","text":"Dan Abramov 2015 [VIDEO] But there was one earlier presentation I cannot find it, guy was showing live debugging of the video game. Not sure is it TED or one of conferences ... Edit: This is it: Bred Victor: [LINK] 2013 Future of programming [LINK]"},{"id":26582023,"author":"acemarke","month":"2021-03","story":"Comparing Svelte and React","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as dating Redux's debut at React Europe in July 2015.","quote":"demoed at React Europe on July 5, 2015 [0]","text":"That's wrong. Dan created Redux while he was actually unemployed. I'd have to check the exact timeline, but I believe he left Stampsy in late 2014 or early 2015, and was actually getting some funding from an OSS collective-type thing while he developed Redux in preparation for React Europe. Redux was developed in June/July 2015, and demoed at React Europe on July 5, 2015 [0]. Redux 1.0 was released on Aug 14, 2015 [1]. Per the tweet linked from that page [2], Dan didn't join Facebook until November 2015, well after Redux had been released. So, he most definitely was _not_ working at FB when he developed Redux. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":15554219,"author":"breatheoften","month":"2017-10","story":"Apollo Client 2.0","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the gripping original Redux demo the commenter wants an Apollo equivalent of.","quote":"something gripping like the original redux conference demo[1]","text":"Is Apollo good? I feel like I’ve been aware of their existence for a long time — they have a large marketing presence but I don’t actually have any idea what it is ... there’s a server for making graphql servers and a client for doing ...??? I know they have tools for compiling graphql queries into various typed language interfaces which I have lusted for. I feel like every blog post of theirs I’ve ever clicked is high percentage marketing and not a lot of focused explanation ... They have a tough communication nut to crack — they have to sell to people who’ve never heard of graphql, to people who’ve lusted after the vision communicated very clearly by Facebook for graphql and relay, to people using redux and the disarray of different mechanisms for straddling the various in practice divides between local and server state. Being able to map rest apis into graphql apis on the browser side seems like a nice migration strategy but there still seems like something missing to me to feel like I know what the value proposition of the Apollo Client is ... Are there any good live demo style introductions to Apollo — something gripping like the original redux conference demo[1] ...? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15110012,"author":"stankot","month":"2017-08","story":"Ask HN: Experienced JavaScript programmers, what are your recommendations?","stance":"reference","why":"Linked within a long list of JavaScript learning advice.","quote":"","text":"Hey there, I'll start with the disclaimer, as lately HN comments have tendency of becoming toxic - everything I write is my personal opinion. With that behind us I can start. I'll try to write this for someone who knows very little about JS, but knows her/his way around basic programming/cs. Firstly I love JavaScript, I have been writing it for 10 years now, but there are bad parts of it as well. Let's start with good stuff * It is evolving in the right direction * You can do a lot of things with it (web apps, games, mobile apps, server...) * Materials for learning are everywhere * It will be easy to find a job Bad stuff * It is moving at crazy to follow pace * There are a lot of different ways of doing same things * As it is very hip thing to do, there is a lot of bu___hit (beware of the hype train) Learn plain javascript before moving to frameworks. Focus on ES6 and new stuff as it will make your life way easier. * Someone already mentioned Dr. Axel Rauschmayer, pick any of his books - [LINK] * Another good one is You Dont Know JS - [LINK] Try to make small app on your own. You'll get into all sort of problems, but that's the fun part I guess. That will help you to understand what frameworks are trying to solve. Then you can start learning one of the frameworks. React, Vue, Angular are all solid choices. My pick is React, as I know it best. Apply the same mindset as with learning plain JS. Try to write your own stuff on a small scale before importing huge libraries. Good ex"},{"id":10877751,"author":"igravious","month":"2016-01","story":"Ask HN: How does a 1990s web developer get back on track?","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of time-travel and hot reloading that sparked the commenter's interest.","quote":"seeing Dan's demo of Redux's time-travel and hot reloading ability, I was intrigued.","text":"I've a good few years web programming experience: Rails, PHP, HTML/CSS/JS (jQuery), SQL, ... you get the idea. Apart from keeping up to date with Rails nothing 'modern'. I've spent the last week (finally!) trying and in the end succeeding to wrap my head around React+Redux. It took a many many many hours of puzzlement. I began with the react+slingshot[0] @coryhouse made as a starter kit and going through the React docs and Redux docs. I guess my desire stemmed from seeing Dan's demo of Redux's time-travel and hot reloading ability, I was intrigued. I copied the TODO example from the Redux docs and fashioned it to my liking exploring all the parts. I'm here to tell you that it's not easy. The landscape is still in flux (buh-dum-tish, I'll be here all week folks) and that for every piece of the dev tool puzzle you've two or three options. I think you can get away with React+Redux on the client side which replaces the V in your MVC. {This in practice means the `react', `react-dom', `react-redux', and `redux' files. _Everything_ else is tooling, but my God so much tooling}. YMMV. That's as far as I'd go, otherwise you're throwing out all your server-side dev experience, and why would you want to do that? What I'd like to ask folks is: what happens to my nice static HTML/CSS pages if React+Redux is all JS? Server-side rendering? I'll come back and edit this and put in a few more links I found handy. Do believe the hype, React+Redux is pretty neat. [0] [LINK] --- Edit! React docs: "},{"id":10610136,"author":"lewisl9029","month":"2015-11","story":"What is a coder's worst nightmare? (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Linked only as a reference to Redux in a broader React defense.","quote":"","text":"I agree that it sucks when the \"worse\" technology wins, but your points on why React is an example of one of these \"worse\" technologies are seriously misinformed regarding its shortcomings and have largely glossed over some of its most significant merits over existing technology. > React seems to be designed to ignore 40 years of accumulated software best practices. Best practices in software is not set in stone. Sometimes you need to break from existing best practices of the past in order to arrive at truly powerful new approaches to solving problems that may not seem intuitive at first, but eventually manage to redefine best practices through their technical merits. The Virtual DOM and re-rendering on every change is one such approach popularized by React, but it's definitely not the only one, nor is it even the most significant one, in my humble opinion. Before React, mutable models was the only game in town. Angular imposed mutable models, Ember imposed mutable models, Backbone imposed mutable models, your average custom ad-hoc JQuery framework probably also imposed mutable models. Everyone blindly followed this \"best practice\" simply because this was the status quo ever since the earliest days of UI development. React is the first JS UI framework that does not impose a mutable model. And as a result, the ClojureScript community was able to build on top of it and show the world that change detection can be implemented as a constant-time reference comparison when immutable"},{"id":10078277,"author":"Buetol","month":"2015-08","story":"Working Toward Bret Victor's “Learnable Programming”","stance":"reference","why":"Example of direct-manipulation live editing with react-hot-loader and redux.","quote":"I think the closer we are to the bret Victor's ideal (of seeing what you do directly) is this: (react-hot-loader + redux).","text":"But most things we see are 2D outputs (webpages, world if you close one eye, text console,..). I think the closer we are to the bret Victor's ideal (of seeing what you do directly) is this: [VIDEO] (react-hot-loader + redux). And this is already practical. Another nice tool who's the near his ideal is the chrome dev console: visual interaction, live edit, start/stop debugging,.. The future is bright."}],"r":[["8fHi36dvTdE",1],["ztspvPYybIY",1],["u-x7PdnvCyI",1],["ByNs9TG30E8",1]]},"HUEvRyemKSg":{"wy":"HN returns to it constantly as a prescient talk, cited whenever threads cover DRM, locked-down devices, app verification or restrictions on running your own software.","q":{"x":"which I very strongly recommend that everybody watch","i":8261388,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8261388,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2014-09","story":"Lost Lessons from 8-Bit BASIC","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why Turing completeness scares powers that want locked-down computers.","quote":"which I very strongly recommend that everybody watch","text":"> the idea that users shouldn't be developers has lead to a situation in which access to development tools and information are seemingly treated as a privilege instead of a right Unfortunately, a lot of people have been starting the War On General Purpose Computing[1] for a while now. While many people saw Doctorow's first talk[1] on the War On General Purpose computing, his sequel talk[2] about the upcoming Civil -War On General Purpose Computing is one of the most important talks I've ever seen. The talk is about exactly this point: Turing completeness scares various traditional powers (and some new/upcoming powers), and their instinct is to lash out and try to mandate that someone make a computer that can't run the various programs that scare them. Not understanding what \"Turing Complete\" means, they end up in a cycle that tries to blacklist anything that allows these scary programs to be written and used. Hiding the dev tools is just one step on a path that - by definition - must end up with the hobbling of computing devices until they are no longer Turing complete. This problem needs to be fought right now , in a big way. The non-technical population is just starting to understand the full impact of what it means to have a General Purpose Computer, and we are only seeing the first skirmishes. The 2nd talk I mentioned above by Doctorow[2] - which I very strongly recommend that everybody watch - points out how modern concerns over \"dev tools\" (or \"copyright\") are trivial. "},{"id":3411143,"author":"ugh","month":"2011-12","story":"Doctorow: The coming war on general-purpose computation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Doctorow keynote on war against general-purpose computation.","quote":"I like to call it the secret keynote of 28C3.","text":"It’s worth watching the talk (linked in the article, but special pointing out seems appropriate): [VIDEO] I like to call it the secret keynote of 28C3."},{"id":47581406,"author":"supern0va","month":"2026-03","story":"Android Developer Verification","stance":"rec","why":"Why restricting running your own software is a societal problem.","quote":"on why this is a serious problem for society","text":"Is it a serious problem that you can run whatever software you want on your computer? Should we make it so that no one can do that without permission to protect them? I recommend Cory Doctorow's talk on why this is a serious problem for society: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37012996,"author":"freefaler","month":"2023-08","story":"Will Browsers Be Required by Law to Stop You from Visiting Infringing Sites?","stance":"rec","why":"Prescient talk predicting control over general computing platforms.","quote":"Cory Doctorow has a prescient talk","text":"Cory Doctorow has a prescient talk: \"The coming war on general computation\" from 2011 (12 years ago) in which he argues that all general computing platforms (OS, phone OS, browser) would face challenges by governments and corporations. This looks like another way to control content distribution and put more control in Google's hands. They've made a great strategic choice in building Chrome browser and effectively superseded Microsoft and Apple on desktops as a platform. The talk can be found here: [VIDEO] When people have means to consume or produce content without control any governmental bureaucrat will try to control this activity. History supports this argument. No printing press > no cheap books > illiterate population > easy to control what spreads as information and what do not. Printing press arrived > cheap books > growing literacy > control over the presses. Lenin's first targets were the post/telegraph (communication), bridges and railways (movement of people) and all big printing houses (Lenin's Full Collected works - vol. 32, page 282) Radio arrived > Fixed Frequencies Radio sets sold & Governmental Radio jamming stations TV > Licence to broadcast Internet > Big Firewall of China & etc... And don't even think about moving money freely as experienced by requirement to show why are you paying someone and those nice people from bank compliance departments. The cost of enforcement in digital economy is way cheaper than before. The tools are there or can be built chea"},{"id":29060738,"author":"woojoo666","month":"2021-10","story":"Grand jury subpoena for Signal user data, Central District of California","stance":"rec","why":"Famous talk on the coming war on general computation.","quote":"much of this has already come true","text":"You might have already seen this but in case you havent, there's a famous talk about this called \"the coming war on general computation\" ( [VIDEO] ), and much of this has already come true"},{"id":23470710,"author":"akersten","month":"2020-06","story":"How SGX Fails in Practice","stance":"rec","why":"CCC talk on trusted computing and user control.","quote":"old but still very good CCC talk that encapsulates my feelings about this","text":"I'm comfortable with it on SmartCards or EMV chips, since those are tailored devices for a specific purpose and the trust model is understood by the participants. I'm not particularly upset that I can't root my credit card. Mobile phones, it's disappointing, but total consumer control was kind of a lost cause from day 1. What truly bothers me is the introduction of trusted computing into general-purpose consumer CPUs, where previously we had complete freedom. There's an old but still very good CCC talk that encapsulates my feelings about this [0]. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45721442,"author":"bo1024","month":"2025-10","story":"What happened to running what you wanted on your own machine?","stance":"reference","why":"Doctorow's 2011 speech on general computation.","quote":"","text":"\"The Coming War on General Computation\", Cory Doctorow (2011). Speech: [VIDEO] Transcript: [LINK] (Of course, Stallman warned of this type of thing much earlier as well.)"},{"id":42953513,"author":"66fm472tjy7","month":"2025-02","story":"Microsoft deletes official Windows 11 CPU/TPM bypass for unsupported PCs","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"puts tinfoil hat on Ensuring that a critical mass of people use remote attestation[0] capable devices. The next step is a browser API[1] for this so that content owners can exclude devices capable of storing the content, or stripping out ads/tracking, etc. Sure, there will be a cat-and-mouse game where people will figure out how to fake the attestation for some period of time, but general computation[2] is probably on the way out. ---- [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34511530,"author":"loup-vaillant","month":"2023-01","story":"UK proposal to criminalize “sophisticated encrypted communication devices”","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Heard of the coming war on general computation?","text":"Computers are a little different than specialised devices like… well, bombs & drugs let's say. Heard of the coming war on general computation? [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":26261080,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2021-02","story":"The decline of computers as a general-purpose technology","stance":"reference","why":"Doctorow keynote on general computation.","quote":"","text":"> Software is economically worthless without making it artificially scarce. A lot of \"software innovation\" is not about building better software, but building software you can charge for, which means \"walled gardens\", patents, DRM, license servers, anti-tampering mechanisms, and the like. In essence all content (music, movies, software, books, etc) follows the same pattern. You remind me of Cory Doctorow's \"The Coming War on General Computation\" keynote, which interestingly didn't notice that streaming and similar cloud-based services would become the DRM of the last decade. Or, as it turns out, the rising appeal of non -general purpose devices that are more efficient. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":19610715,"author":"Schnitz","month":"2019-04","story":"Notarizing Your App Before Distribution","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This isn't a big surprise, you should expect the tightening of the screws to continue and the pace of it to accelerate. The real issue here is general purpose computing. General purpose computing makes it possible to block ads, to rip your CDs instead of the industry forcing you to buy the same music again, etc. and as a result it is THE major obstacle to unlimited rent seeking and squeezing the last penny out of every user. Expect the industry to keep working in this direction until everything is as locked down as an iPhone. See also: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14284633,"author":"arximboldi","month":"2017-05","story":"Deep Learning’s Impact on Image Processing, Mathematics, and Humanity","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is an interesting article. It made me think of the socio-political consequences of the move towards neural networks. Many of us, children of the personal computer revolution, were attracted to computers for its empowering and democratizing effects. Just with a computer you could build anything! You are as powerful as any of \"them\"! The proprietary software model was developed to monetize end consumers by enchaining them, but the free software model and the fertile communities of the early Internet showed that the model of personal computation could not simply be displaced by legalese. But if we take the author's position on the impact of deep networks, the model of computation is changing again. If teaching deep networks is the new way to write useful programs, our brains and personal computers are not enough... they are obsolete. We now need clusters and, most importantly, massive access to data! This gives extraordinary leverage to the hoarders of data and servers of the Internet, the googles and the facebooks. I'd like to think that maybe we are just at the beginning, the early \"mainframe neural networks\" era. That we just have to wait until there is enough technology and new markets discovered to build the \"personal neural network\". That consensual, open and distributed ways of sharing data will emerge and that new massively parallel computers will become affordable by the masses. That the models of neural networks will become well divulged and simplified and kids wi"},{"id":13453649,"author":"themusicgod1","month":"2017-01","story":"US Announces Withdrawal from TPP","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It was part of the War on General Purpose Computation","text":"The TPP was far greater than a class issue. It was part of the War on General Purpose Computation ( [VIDEO] ). It cut through class lines, into our ability to control the technology in our lives."},{"id":8736171,"author":"zxcdw","month":"2014-12","story":"Peter Sunde: 'I went to jail for my cause. What did you do?'","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> And if I decided to show one of them to a someone who gave me money in return, that still doesn't give anybody who didn't pay me or anyone else for that matter, any right to access that file, no matter how little \"intrinsic value\" it represents. Sure, this is unconditionally right if we go by the book and agree with the law. No argument here. Though, by going by the said law... The full albums, songs, movies, movie clips, whatever unauthorized there is in YouTube for example for billions of people to see every day creates a huge problem. For example here in Finland, any person listening to an unauthorized upload of an album on YouTube breaks the law. Practically a huge majority of the youth are criminals as they do this on a daily basis, and take it for granted. Obviously it is utterly senseless to go after these people for what they are doing. > If someone invented a teleport that allowed people to beam to whereever and back, that would cause a rise in theft of tangible goods all over the world. But this would not mean that because the old systems of doors and locks were now inefficient, people should not be allowed to claim ownership of property anymore. But this, of course, is a flawed analogy. A better one would be with an ability to clone material for free. Say, if we were able to build a car, and then clone it for free for everyone. Why wouldn't we? For many goods we could just come up with better and better ones again and again, and people would immediately clone the"},{"id":8027357,"author":"higherpurpose","month":"2014-07","story":"EA is File Snooping with the Origin Client","stance":"reference","why":"Doctorow's predictions about DRM.","quote":"Basically everything Cory Doctorow has predicted [1] would happen with DRM","text":"Basically everything Cory Doctorow has predicted [1] would happen with DRM, and of course what Richard Stallman said many years before that. [1] [VIDEO] And now we're going to get OS level DRM that works through all of our browsers, thanks to Netflix, Google, Microsoft, Apple, and last, but not least, the W3C. Terrific."},{"id":7818610,"author":"sp332","month":"2014-05","story":"US cybercrime laws being used to target security researchers","stance":"reference","why":"Why locked-down computing can't work without removing general computation.","quote":"All anti-virus and DRM systems are provably limited unless general-purpose computation is removed.","text":"Cory Doctorow's keynote at Chaos Communication Congress (2011) was about this trend. It's not technically feasible to make a turing-complete system that only does things that the creator likes. All anti-virus and DRM systems are provably limited unless general-purpose computation is removed. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6890964,"author":"stefanix","month":"2013-12","story":"Ubuntu Touch OS wins its first smartphone partner","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"In other words I like my computers to be general","text":"Dev here. Moved from OSX to Ubuntu a year ago. Love not having to agree-click ULAs all the time. Setting up dev stacks is very often so much easier and better documented. Working with open source software to develop open source projects is very enjoyable and very often I feel this is just the beginning of something very profound. With proprietary OSes I constantly have the feeling of banging against elaborately engineered limitations. This feels so counter-productive. Instead of producing something great why not spend incredible amounts of work-years on creating artificial limitations for the users, right. No thanks. In other words I like my computers to be general: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5830670,"author":"kijin","month":"2013-06","story":"Welcome to Echelon 2.0","stance":"reference","why":"Doctorow talk on general-purpose computing under threat.","quote":"","text":"What next? This [1], of course. Of all the electronic device you own that are capable of being exploited for automated surveillance, your PC or Mac is probably the only thing that is not actually being used for automated surveillance (not counting your web browsing habits, and provided you don't have any spyware on your computer). How dare you use a computing device that (hopefully) does not have any backdoors built into it, is not locked into any vendor's monthly payment plan, is not locked into any vendor's proprietary OS, and even supports \"terrorist\" techniques such as full-disk encryption? Fortunately for Big Brother, key hardware components of general-purpose computers are only produced by a handful of corporations such as Intel and AMD. Don't be surprised if they agree to build all sorts of defects-by-design into their processors, graphics cards, HDDs/SSDs, etc. There are already plenty of excuses: I have popcorn ready for the day when graphics cards finally implement security systems to prevent them from being used for bitcoin mining or password brute-forcing. The FSF should change their name to FCF, Free Computing Foundation. We need them badly, more than ever before. [1] Cory Doctorow, \"The Coming War on General Computation\", [VIDEO]"},{"id":5467206,"author":"shmerl","month":"2013-03","story":"Dear Congress: It's time for a real fix on unlocking and the DMCA","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You asked a straight question - how to reduce piracy. I answered - treat customers with respect (no DRM), and many of the current pirates will become paying users. If they want to \"eliminate piracy\" - they can forget about it, it will never happen. They can however increase their profits by acting decently. Today they are mostly focusing on chasing pirates wasting resources on that completely in vain. They should focus on catering to legitimate users instead. And it will pay off. As proven by those who act decently and principally don't use DRM. > Because if I'm selling files for $5 with no copy protection and Joe Pirate is giving them away for $0 (but he doesn't want me to disrespect him by filing legal action against him or hurting his feeling by labeling him a pirate), tell me why people are going to give me $5 for the same thing that they could get for free? I thought this is simple. People already can get it for free by pirating it. I.e. you have such choices: 1. Sell for $5 with DRM that reduces usability by limiting platforms where this can be used and etc. etc. By using DRM the distributor also offends paying customers by default by treating them as potential criminals. Joe pirate breaks DRM, and gives this out for $0 with no DRM, which can be used anywhere. 2. Sell for $5 with no DRM. It gives wide platforms availability and shows respect to the customer. Joe pirate gives out the same thing for $0. Note, Joe pirate will give it away in either case. So what is better "},{"id":3935586,"author":"ChristianMarks","month":"2012-05","story":"Elsevier's recent update to its letter to the mathematical community","stance":"reference","why":"Doctorow talk on the coming war on general computation.","quote":"Cory Doctorow spoke about the coming war on general computation at 28c3","text":"It is possible that in one hundred years or so that many of the \"most useful\" results published in copyrighted journals of the 20th century and available nowhere else will have found their way into the public domain. But it need not turn out that way. Cory Doctorow spoke about the coming war on general computation at 28c3: [VIDEO] So we cannot be complacent."}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1]]},"gbYXBJOFgeI":{"wy":"It is HN's go-to reference whenever device lockdown, DRM, or restrictions on installing software come up, with commenters noting how prescient it was.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend reading up on what Cory has to say on DRM and the war on general purpose computing. It may change your mind.","i":16121525,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16121525,"author":"freedomben","month":"2018-01","story":"Cory Doctorow: Persuasion, Adaptation, and the Arms Race for Your Attention","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Doctorow talk on DRM and war on general purpose computing","quote":"I highly recommend reading up on what Cory has to say on DRM and the war on general purpose computing. It may change your mind.","text":"Always interested in what Cory Doctorow has to say. While this particular blog post isn't about DRM, his work is benefiting everyone (except those who would rather enrich themselves at the expense of humanity). It's probably bigger than you think. I highly recommend reading up on what Cory has to say on DRM and the war on general purpose computing. It may change your mind. [LINK] [LINK] Also watch the various youtube videos of his talks, like this one: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31543246,"author":"est31","month":"2022-05","story":"Google has banned the training of deepfakes in Colab","stance":"rec","why":"Doctorow's talk predicting restrictions on general purpose computing","quote":"It's slowly becoming reality.","text":"There is this talk about the civil war on general purpose computing by Cory Doctorow: [LINK] He gave the same talk at Google: [VIDEO] It's slowly becoming reality."},{"id":19146530,"author":"freedomben","month":"2019-02","story":"Apple fails to block porn and gambling “enterprise” apps","stance":"rec","why":"Doctorow on trading freedom for security in locked ecosystems","quote":"I refer you to Cory Doctorow's War on General Purpose Computing","text":"> Really? We're there now? It's become Apple's \"responsibility\" to police every last corner of iOS? I find it disgusting, but really Apple is the only one to blame for that when they started aggressively policing and removing apps, and then marketing to customers based on that. I've heard people on HN state ad nauseam that they love that Apple does this, and that it's one of the reasons they live in the Apple ecosystem. They want to be protected from running apps that might do something objectionable (according to Apple's standard), and are absolutely willing to trade their freedom for some security. I obviously don't agree with this at all (I refer you to Cory Doctorow's War on General Purpose Computing: [VIDEO] ) but it is a position I can understand."},{"id":14730342,"author":"zAy0LfpBZLC8mAC","month":"2017-07","story":"DRM Is Toxic to Culture","stance":"rec","why":"Doctorow talk on DRM and control of computers","quote":"See also Cory Doctorow's talks on the topic, like maybe this one","text":"> How does the arbitrariness of those technological restrictions differ from the arbitrariness of the legal restrictions which I'm sure the author would insist upon, should you decide to republish their work? One of those is decided by a democratic process, the other is dictated by one party. > but that is a problem of execution, not one of concept. Actually, it is a problem of the concept. It is asking for computers to be robbed of their defining characteristic, and to be put under dictatorial control. See also Cory Doctorow's talks on the topic, like maybe this one: [VIDEO] (he gave this and similar talks in many places, you can find quite a few of them on youtube, I have no clue whether this particular one is good)"},{"id":14561825,"author":"zAy0LfpBZLC8mAC","month":"2017-06","story":"Telegram founder: US intelligence tried to bribe us to weaken encryption","stance":"rec","why":"Doctorow on the war against general-purpose computing","quote":"you might be interested in some of Cory Doctorow's talks","text":"> are you okay with me being able to spend two weeks on the dark-web researching how to make and detonate a bomb using totally innocent chemical purchases, and then your spouse, parents, relatives, or you, being an innocent victim of my exploding the results, or would you want me to be stopped at some point after I started doing that? Yes, of course. Just as I am okay with people being able to spend ten minutes to take a knife from the kitchen and kill me. People are able to do all kinds of bad stuff, doesn't mean they actually do. The only alternative to a world where people are able to do bad stuff is totalitarianism, which is itself bad stuff (if only because I don't get to say what is considered good and what is considered bad), so not actually a solution either. Also, what you seem to think is just a logical impossibility. Either your communication can be read by third parties or it can not. You cannot build crypto that only leaks nuclear bomb plans, but keeps your medical information uncrackable. And you might be interested in some of Cory Doctorow's talks (or blog articles or maybe even books) on the general topic of \"the war against general-purpose computing\", like maybe one of these: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":7875678,"author":"jcr","month":"2014-06","story":"Cory Doctorow novel pulled from school reading for 'questioning authority'","stance":"rec","why":"Doctorow on the coming civil war over general-purpose computing","quote":"Also, you might enjoy Cory Doctorow's talk","text":"The book, \"Little Brother,\" is available in multiple formats under Creative Commons licensing (free): [LINK] Also, you might enjoy Cory Doctorow's talk: \"The Coming Civil War over General-purpose Computing\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":49197271,"author":"freefaler","month":"2026-08","story":"On non-rooted Android 17, ADB uninstall of system apps fails","stance":"reference","why":"Doctorow warned of such enforcement","quote":"14 years ago, Cory Doctorow warned us about that","text":"14 years ago, Cory Doctorow warned us about that: [VIDEO] The modern societies run via those devices and the enforcement will move to the mostly free Internet that was \"a long time ago, when it didn't matter as much\"."},{"id":46244531,"author":"freefaler","month":"2025-12","story":"UK House of Lords attempting to ban use of VPNs by anyone under 16","stance":"reference","why":"Doctorow warned of such enforcement","quote":"14 years ago, Cory Doctorow warned us about that","text":"14 years ago, Cory Doctorow warned us about that: [VIDEO] The modern societies run via those devices and the enforcement will move to the mostly free Internet that was \"a long time ago, when it didn't matter as much\"."},{"id":45085329,"author":"freefaler","month":"2025-08","story":"Google: 'Your $1000 phone needs our permission to install apps now' [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Doctorow warned of this years ago","quote":"13 years ago, Cory Doctorow warned us","text":"13 years ago, Cory Doctorow warned us: [VIDEO] So basically market forces and profit optimization is at work here as always. However, if we can still unlock the boot loader and install Lineage OS or something like that and have a way to pay for developers to release their apps on stores like f-droid we can use the hardware. The biggest problem with having freedom to use our devices is that the model is broken for the developers who support them. You \"can donate\", but from the numbers I've seen it's like 1 in 1000 donate. No pay == developers can't invest their time to improve the software. So if there is \"really\" a substantial number of enthusiasts that are ready to pay for the freedom they crave, then companies like Librem will have enough customers to create decent and usable products for this audience. Want digital freedom - prepare to support the people who provide it. Yes, that might mean that we'll need to have 2 devices, 1 for \"banking/government services\" that is \"certified\" and one for our own usage. Shitty but we'll be forced to do that sooner on later. The efficiencies for the government to enforce the policies is so strong that they can't helps themselves. And corporations like to have more data to squeeze every cent from the customer. So if there is a working business model for \"freedom\" we might have a partial freedom. If there isn't we'd be just a digital farm animals to be optimized for max profits and max compliance."},{"id":45086475,"author":"bonoboTP","month":"2025-08","story":"Google: 'Your $1000 phone needs our permission to install apps now' [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Notes audience Q&A advocating digital freedoms at Google","quote":"The audience (I assume Google employees) are openly advocating for digital freedoms and the classic hacker ethos.","text":"> 13 years ago, Cory Doctorow warned us: > [VIDEO] Wow, that's in Talks at Google . Listening to the Q&A is just so weird. The audience (I assume Google employees) are openly advocating for digital freedoms and the classic hacker ethos. Crazy how much the Overton window has shifted. I wonder where those people are now."},{"id":22985214,"author":"jakeogh","month":"2020-04","story":"‘Quarantine fatigue’: Researchers find more Americans venturing against orders","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as related reading on tech regulation","quote":"The Coming Civil War over General-purpose Computing","text":"Tech giant briar patch. You want the gov to decide what can be remembered a-la GDPR? Maybe some Article13 after? [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] The Coming Civil War over General-purpose Computing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21811716,"author":"jakeogh","month":"2019-12","story":"United States to fund gun-violence research after 20-year freeze","stance":"reference","why":"Warns of a two-class split in computing access","quote":"You are arguing for two classes of people; those with access to general purpose computing, and those without.","text":"You are arguing for two classes of people; those with access to general purpose computing, and those without. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15126078,"author":"_nrvs","month":"2017-08","story":"KIA Dealership in Canada remotely disables car over $200 fee","stance":"reference","why":"Doctorow anticipated such scenarios in his 2012 talk","quote":"Cory Doctrow riffing on similar scenarios in 2012 with his talk","text":"Glad this made me remember Cory Doctrow riffing on similar scenarios in 2012 with his talk \"The Coming Civil War over General-purpose Computing\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":12271731,"author":"bryanbuckley","month":"2016-08","story":"GPLv4 – Starting the Conversation (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Doctorow on locked-down computing devices and ownership","quote":"If you buy an iPhone, what exactly do you own?","text":"> When someone sell property, the seller should not have a right to exercise powers that are exclusively to the new owner. Sorry to say, but it is already the case of many computing devices: [VIDEO] If you buy an iPhone, what exactly do you own? I hope this stuff gets cleared up, of course. Would be nice to run some free software on any computing device you own. > DRM is the technical method to remain in control over property after sale, where contract would be the legal method when renting out property. I agree at least the \"owner\" should be allowed to boot alternative SW, but it means DRM for digital assets will not be functional (you could dual boot..), as well as some silicon, I imagine. > In open DRM, how and whose digital rights are being managed by said technology? If I lock down my laptops boot loader, is the laptop the digital right that I am \"managing\" with said security No. I mean SW or digital assets (or digital titles to physical assets). Open DRM would be one in which anyone that has some SW/digital asset they wish to use DRM to control the usage of is capable of doing so. Whether or not the gatekeepers will allow it remains to be seen. The effect of p2p DRM on society would be hard to predict."},{"id":12294345,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2016-08","story":"Cortana: The spy in Windows 10","stance":"reference","why":"Doctorow's case for user freedom over general purpose computers","quote":"","text":"This offensive, paternalistic attitude towards users needs to stop. You do not get to decide these things for end users. > It's unfair to expect most users to be able to protect themselves. Correct. Problems in this area are the fault of the vendor, not the user , for producing shoddy software, not caring enough about security, and/or not going far enough to educate the user about important thins they do need to understand. None of this requires stealing user data (or \"telemetry\"), nor does it require taking agency away from the user. Your \"solution\" is to take away the General Purpose Computer from the user. I realize that General Purpose Computers are frightening when in the hands of people who might make a mistake* or run some software you don't like, but that is what we call \"freedom\"[1]. Instead of supporting freedom, you seem determined to force centralized control on people. Do you not see how this is a major shift in power? > Therefore, as a matter of public safety, we're going to have to put safety and security rules on publicly networked computers. This is a cheap excuse to not address actual security. The negligent security practices of most businesses over the last few decades are becoming a serious problem, and as usual the guilty are trying to deflect the blame onto the people least responsible: the users. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10986966,"author":"jakeogh","month":"2016-01","story":"California Police Used Stingrays in Planes to Spy on Phones","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Is it accurate to state they have made illegal (via 'rules') to use open source (user controlled) EM comm above 100um (below 3000GHz[1]) (except a few windows)? Or is it 9kHz to 275GHz? I bet when line of sight NIR comms (there's a nice window around 1550nm) get popular, they will (to stay relevant) want to 'regulate'[2] that too. Prior restraint is the problem here, anyone can mess with the spectrum with basic electronic skills, mandating 'approved' software seems less than pointless. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7318033,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2014-02","story":"A message from Lawrence Lessig [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Doctorow's caution about the future","quote":"Doctorow's caution for the future[1] a year earlier.","text":"[comments don't always parse as intended, so let me first say that I mean this in the very best way possible, and intend to find ways to support Lessing and everybody else fighting corruption] This may be the most powerful talk I've ever seen. Mr. Lessig has alway given a very interesting (and entertaining) talk. There have been some good talks recently, such as some of the 30c3 talks and Doctorow's caution for the future[1] a year earlier. There are, believe, there's two reasons this talk is so effective. First, Mr. Lessig gave such a clear and simple goal. This prevents the problem from being immediately interpreted as one of those \"big problems\" that never get solved. It doesn't even matter if campaign finance really is the \"root\" of the problem. It's still worthwhile, and even a small success has got to a lest help some when fighting for other causes in the future. When dealing with other difficult situations such as addiction or depression, a common suggestion is to make small goals first, that are actually attainable, because trying to solve everything at once often ends up conditioning for apathy over the \"impossible task\". Those voting numbers shown at the end, however, suggests some victories are easily within reach. That said... I believe the key reason this talk was so powerful is that Mr. Lessig called on one of the original ways of rallying people to the cause. It works because it's a reminder that people matter, and some things cannot wait until later... ...beca"},{"id":4863350,"author":"mtgx","month":"2012-12","story":"Julian Assange: Cryptographic Call to Arms","stance":"reference","why":"Doctorow on built-in interception in routers","quote":"he said that all routers today have interception technology in them.","text":"In Cory Doctorow's talk at Google, he said that all routers today have interception technology in them. It's only a matter of being enabled or not in some countries or in others. But it seems so many have asked for it, that they don't even bother to build routers without that interception technology in them, so now their default router has interception built-in by default. [VIDEO]"},{"id":4928234,"author":"mtgx","month":"2012-12","story":"Massive New Surveillance Program Uncovered by Wall Street Journal","stance":"reference","why":"Doctorow on cheap mass monitoring","quote":"it's becoming cheaper and easier for authorities to just monitor everyone and store information about everyone","text":"People have warned that this will happen eventually, but I don't think that many believed it. We're witnessing the first real steps towards making pre-crime recognition a reality in the country. Good bye \"innocent until proven guilty\". Cory Doctorow has said in his \"war against general computation\" talk [1] that it's becoming cheaper and easier for authorities to just monitor everyone and store information about everyone, and then use algorithms to catch them, than focusing on who they they need to catch and following just that one. Now it's up to the people to stop this from becoming legal or from being done even illegally, not just in US, but in all countries, as I'm sure many others will try doing it, too. The ironic part is that the developed \"democratic\" countries may be the first to do it, because they are more advanced technologically. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4958333,"author":"vy8vWJlco","month":"2012-12","story":"The Copyright Monopoly Stands In Direct Opposition To Property Rights","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"From the article: \"(Some people would argue that even property as such is a governmentally-sanctioned private monopoly, in order to downplay the fact that the copyright monopoly stands in opposition to property rights, but that would not be what we mean by \"property\" and \"monopoly\" as concepts. If I own an umbrella, I control that umbrella. If I have a monopoly on umbrellas, I get to control everybody else's umbrella too, and get to call on the government to have that enforced.)\" I believe the above was meant to addresses your specific form of criticism. I've said it before, and I'll say it again... \"imaginary property\" != \"real property\" importanceof(\"real property\") > importanceof(\"imaginary property\") I believe my right to exist stems from my \"inalienable property right\" to my own body. I can move it left and right and up and down. If someone were to try to take that away from me, I'd be pretty upset and I'd do more than write my representative. Whereas, the basic idea of copyright is to encourage sharing of ideas - it doesn't actually have a basis as an inalienable or \"moral right.\" In order to encourage sharing ideas, societies have been allowing the encroachment of imaginary property rights (a euphemism for the independent traditions of copyright, patents, and trademaks) on tangible and even inalienable property rights (making it uncomfortable to say the least). The Internet, however, has shaken even that basic premise: the Internet is full of creativity (not just copyr"}],"r":[["bnKIVX968PQ",1],["snAhsXyO3Ck",1],["Nb2tebYAaOA",1],["8xDK2LgSeyk",1],["pRlh8LX4kQI",1],["ZCmnQR3_qWg",1],["wt-iVFxgFWk",1],["oOlDewpCfZQ",1],["hC3VTgIPoGU",1]]},"fE2KDzZaxvE":{"wy":"A perennial HN favorite whenever firmware, disks, ECC or opaque hardware come up, valued as both entertaining and sobering.","q":{"x":"As with all his talks, you can expect it to be quite entertaining as well as informative","i":22165352,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":43758926,"author":"thomasjudge","month":"2025-04","story":"Synology Lost the Plot with Hard Drive Locking Move","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining and terrifying perspective on firmware.","quote":"For an entertaining/terrifying perspective on firmware, obligatory Bryan Cantrill talk \"Zebras All the Way Down","text":"For an entertaining/terrifying perspective on firmware, obligatory Bryan Cantrill talk \"Zebras All the Way Down\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":25856114,"author":"Bayart","month":"2021-01","story":"Installing Debian on Modern Hardware","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining explanation of why black-box firmware is aggravating.","quote":"I'll link Bryan Cantrill's talk on the subject, he's more entertaining than I am.","text":">Why does anyone really care about non-free firmware blobs? Aside from the philosophical considerations, stuff breaking in a black-box firmware that you're lead into believing \"just works\" is particularly aggravating. I'll link Bryan Cantrill's talk on the subject, he's more entertaining than I am. [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22213803,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2020-02","story":"Ask HN: What were the things you did that made the biggest impact at your work?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on hardware and firmware bugs.","quote":"Bryan Cantrill's talk on the realted topic of hardware/firmware bugs is pretty good [3].","text":"In my time writing databases, I found that not only the mount-flags could result in data-loss despite fsync, given you use fsync as it is supposed to be used [0], but even enterprise-grade hardware would at times (at that scale [1]) drop and/or corrupt a few bits here and there. That reminds me of the case where AWS famously revealed that a single NIC in their ginaromous S3 fleet flipped a single bit once in a while and that caused an outage because their gossip-daemon responsible for fleet health-checks failed spectacularly [2]. Bryan Cantrill's talk on the realted topic of hardware/firmware bugs is pretty good [3]. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22165352,"author":"kbenson","month":"2020-01","story":"Five Years of Btrfs","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining, informative talk on hard-to-find bugs in data path and firmware.","quote":"As with all his talks, you can expect it to be quite entertaining as well as informative","text":"There's a good Bryan Cantrill talk about that.[1] The gist is that eventually, when you throw enough resources at a problem, all that's left are the really uncommon problem and bugs, and this is specifically what you get in the data path (including drive firmware) where things get harder and harder to figure out as the code gets more hidden and obscure. As with all his talks, you can expect it to be quite entertaining as well as informative and historical (if from his POV). 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20347825,"author":"kbenson","month":"2019-07","story":"Replacing a silently failing disk in a ZFS pool","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why remaining software bugs are rare zebras, not horses.","quote":"On this topic (and related in many ways) is Bryan Cantrill's talk Zebras All the way Down.","text":"On this topic (and related in many ways) is Bryan Cantrill's talk Zebras All the way Down.[1] The gist is that in medicine there's the concept of diseases that are zebras and horses, rare and common, and the saying goes \"when you hear hoof beats think horses not Zebras\". Unlike medicine, in software most the easy problems have already been abstracted away or made trivial through our software stacks, so often what's left are Zebras. What used to be the exceedingly rare bugs now account for a large proportion of the odd behavior we see in very robust tech stacks, because the easy bugs have been hunted down and eliminated already. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18226199,"author":"gt2","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk with great tangents that enrich its subjects.","quote":"Lots of great tangents that add to the subjects. I love talks like this.","text":"Zebras All the Way Down - Bryan Cantrill, Uptime 2017 [VIDEO] Lots of great tangents that add to the subjects. I love talks like this."},{"id":18345338,"author":"ar0b","month":"2018-10","story":"iPhones are allergic to helium","stance":"rec","why":"Favorite conference talk relating to hardware problems.","quote":"One of my favorite conference talks is semi related to problems like this.","text":"One of my favorite conference talks is semi related to problems like this. [VIDEO] Brian Cantrill - Zebras all the way down"},{"id":44534779,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2025-07","story":"Upgrading an M4 Pro Mac mini's storage for half the price","stance":"reference","why":"Describes ZFS catching hardware misdirected reads.","quote":"See Bryan Cantrill's talk \"Zebras All the way Down","text":"> Or you can spend half a billion dollars to solve the issue in hardware. And hope that your hardware/firmware doesn't ever get bugs. Or you can do checksumming at the hardware layer and checksumming at the software/FS layer. Protection in depth. ZFS has caught issues from hardware, like when LBA 123 is requested but LBA 456 is delivered: the hardware-level checksum for LBA 456 was fine, and so it was passed up the stack, but it wasn't actually the data that was asked for. See Bryan Cantrill's talk \"Zebras All the way Down\": * [VIDEO] And if checksums are not needed for a particular use-case, make them toggleable: even ZFS has a set checksums=off option. My problem is not having the option at all ."},{"id":38658434,"author":"ElectricalUnion","month":"2023-12","story":"Database Fundamentals","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for layers of unreliable hardware and firmware.","quote":"It's zebras all the way down","text":"> When we really should have been formally addressing problems from first principles. Because if our OS can't recover gracefully when the disk fills up, then we don't really have anything. And so many OSs (including our desktops) panic when the disk fills up, that we don't really have anything. At a low enough level, all of the tools we rely upon are trash. Unfortunately, the OS can't really \"recover\" most of the time because it is being constantly lied to by several(!!!) layers of uncooperative hardware/firmware. It's zebras all the way down: [VIDEO] > In this case, by letting programs wait until the user has cleared off disk space If your system is really in a no-disk-space-left state you might not be able to login to fix it: [LINK] And even assuming you can login, sometimes you can't delete files either; you need to free space in another way before being able to delete files. [LINK] Filesystems and persistence are hard."},{"id":25626167,"author":"tgbugs","month":"2021-01","story":"ECC matters","stance":"reference","why":"Segment on firmware hiding correctable errors.","quote":"A relevant Bryan Cantrill talk segment on this, which heightens the paranoia around this.","text":"A relevant Bryan Cantrill talk segment on this, which heightens the paranoia around this. Namely, firmware hiding correctable errors and only reporting uncorrectable errors. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20890329,"author":"floatboth","month":"2019-09","story":"Samsung Announces Key-Value SSD Prototype","stance":"reference","why":"Used to support point about opaque proprietary firmware.","quote":"","text":"Well, on the other hand, better to have the ability to deal with it rather than have everything obfuscated in unfixable proprietary firmware… [VIDEO]"},{"id":20466149,"author":"ShroudedNight","month":"2019-07","story":"Investigating Multi-Account IAM Issues in S3 and CloudFront","stance":"reference","why":"Argues zebras-vs-horses heuristic is unreliable in computing.","quote":"the reliability of this heuristic is questionable relative to what it would be in medicine","text":"It's worth noting that in a computing context the reliability of this heuristic is questionable relative to what it would be in medicine: [VIDEO] Zebras All the Way Down - Bryan Cantrill, Uptime 2017"},{"id":18656425,"author":"cyphar","month":"2018-12","story":"Why I'm usually unnerved when modern SSDs die on us","stance":"reference","why":"Source for hard drive head fly-height claim.","quote":"The fly-height is allegedly 0.8 nanometers (800 picometers).","text":"> skates of magnetic readers flying on a cushion of air above a rapidly rotating disc, with the gap separating a few dozen nanometers, often smaller than the process size in the controller's silicon. Complete aside, the fly-height of a magnetic head is actually fractions of a nanometer (i.e. hundreds of picometers). EDIT: I got this from a talk by Bryan Cantrill[1]. The fly-height is allegedly 0.8 nanometers (800 picometers). [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18657270,"author":"cyphar","month":"2018-12","story":"Why I'm usually unnerved when modern SSDs die on us","stance":"reference","why":"Source for claimed 0.8 nm head fly height.","quote":"a talk by Bryan Cantrill[1] where he states that an exec at a hard-drive manufacturer stated it was \".8 nanometers","text":"(Sheepishly) My only reference is a talk by Bryan Cantrill[1] where he states that an exec at a hard-drive manufacturer stated it was \".8 nanometers\". I will try to find a better source than that. EDIT: There is a paper from 2016 which did an analysis in the difference in the flying height of the head during different operations, and it was measured in Angstroms[2]. I couldn't find one that actually gives a precise value of the flying height. There is a 2011 paper which states that some system they were testing allowed for 4-9 nm flying heights[3] which is about half-an-order-of-magnitude larger than the claim -- but that's already 7 years old. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":18657423,"author":"cyphar","month":"2018-12","story":"Why I'm usually unnerved when modern SSDs die on us","stance":"reference","why":"Source for fly-height claim of 800 picometers.","quote":"Bryan Cantrill made this claim in a talk recently[1], and the fly-height is apparently 800 picometers.","text":"Bryan Cantrill made this claim in a talk recently[1], and the fly-height is apparently 800 picometers. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16839366,"author":"nordsieck","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"We Really Don't Know How to Compute: Gerry Sussman - [LINK] Zebras All the Way Down: Bryan Cantrill - [VIDEO] Jonathan Blow on Deep Work: Jonathan Blow - [LINK] Simple Made Easy: Rich Hickey - [LINK] Effective Programs - 10 Years of Clojure: Rich Hickey - [LINK] The Last Thing D Needs: Scott Meyers - [LINK]"},{"id":16046222,"author":"floatboth","month":"2018-01","story":"Ask HN: What’s your favorite talk from 2017?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"On The Turing Completeness of PowerPoint [LINK] Compiling C to printable x86, to make an executable research paper [LINK] HDR Photography in Microsoft Excel?! [LINK] Zebras All the Way Down [VIDEO] Solving Layout Problems with CSS Grid and Friends [LINK] Console Security - Switch [LINK] Let's move SMM out of firmware and into the kernel [LINK] Bringing Linux back to server boot ROMs with NERF and Heads [LINK] Sharing the Chisel [LINK] (looks like the actual conference video wasn't released)"},{"id":38546810,"author":"Voultapher","month":"2023-12","story":"Just about every Windows/Linux device vulnerable to new LogoFAIL firmware attack","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's truly zebras all the way down","text":"I'm shocked! Firmware vendors, the last bastion of high quality memory-safe C code, solving complex problems. Code firmly audited and formally verified to the highest degree. And now you tell me these people, with the highest known software quality got \"parsing image formats\" wrong? It's truly zebras all the way down [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AyOAjFNPAbA",1],["-pKR212H5vQ",1],["swQbA4zub20",1],["rWRbTnE1PEM",1],["gxAaO2rsdIs",1],["JEpsBg0AO6o",1],["PUv66718DII",1],["HJ-719EGIts",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["m3svKOdZijA",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["3qln2u1Vr2E",1],["bmSAYlu0NcY",1],["iffTJ1vPCSo",1],["nXaxk27zwlk",1],["coFIEH3vXPw",1],["9QMGAtxUlAc",1],["79fvDDPaIoY",1],["YKuRkGkf5HU",1],["5Vt8zqhHe_c",1]]},"pLPpl2ISKTg":{"wy":"Offered as an accessible, well-made explainer to share in threads on scam texts, crypto fraud and scam compounds.","q":{"x":"John Oliver did an excellent explainer about how pig butchering works.","i":39782074,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41705399,"author":"gcanyon","month":"2024-10","story":"Ask HN: Should you reply STOP to unwanted texts?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains the long-con and interviews victims who lost hundreds of thousands","quote":"It's well worth the watch","text":"[VIDEO] It's well worth the watch, but tl;dr: it's a long-con scam. They invest as long as it takes to establish a relationship with you, and then engage you to do something (crypto mostly, apparently) involving cash online. They will say they made a bunch of money, and point you at the super-easy online exchange they used. You buy the crypto, you see the crypto increase in value (because it has in the real world) so you buy more, and more and more. The problems start when you say you want to cash out. They switch from \"buy more, it's going up\" to \"there are fees to withdraw, just deposit another <whatever> and then you'll get the withdrawal amount plus <whatever>\" and of course no money ever comes out. Oliver interviews people who have lost hundreds of thousands of dollars this way, some of whom still believe that if they just toss in another <whatever> it will all be resolved and they'll get their money back. It's very sad, and I'm not doing the video justice."},{"id":39782074,"author":"not2b","month":"2024-03","story":"What is a pig butchering scam? (2023)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent explainer on how pig butchering works","quote":"John Oliver did an excellent explainer about how pig butchering works.","text":"John Oliver did an excellent explainer about how pig butchering works. [VIDEO] (apologies if the YouTube link has geographic restrictions, don't know if it does)"},{"id":45592169,"author":"ChrisMarshallNY","month":"2025-10","story":"DOJ seizes $15B in Bitcoin from 'pig butchering' scam based in Cambodia","stance":"rec","why":"Fairly decent bit on the scam, alternative to a podcast","quote":"John Oliver did a fairly decent bit about it","text":"From what I understand, a number of them are Chinese nationals. I suspect that taking Chinese slaves might not be a great idea. China tends to carry a big stick; especially in that region. Wouldn't surprise me, if the DOJ had some \"under-the-table\" help from Chinese sources, but not in any way that anyone can prove. If you don't want to sign up for a podcast, John Oliver did a fairly decent bit about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45248465,"author":"gcanyon","month":"2025-09","story":"A qualitative analysis of pig-butchering scams","stance":"rec","why":"Lightweight summary with heartbreaking victim interviews","quote":"For those interested in a lightweight summary, Last Week Tonight did a segment on pig-butchering about a year ago.","text":"For those interested in a lightweight summary, Last Week Tonight did a segment on pig-butchering about a year ago. [VIDEO] The interviews with the victims are heartbreaking. There's one dad/daughter pair where the dad has lost almost literally everything, is on camera being interviewed about everything that happened, and is still flipping between \"it was a total scam\" and \"maybe if I just send them another fee payment I can get at least some of my money back\" and the daughter saying, \"No, dad! It's a scam, and you are not sending them anymore money!\""},{"id":40834120,"author":"hamasho","month":"2024-06","story":"Anatomy of a cryptocurrency scam","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how fake relationships and fake apps defraud victims","quote":"I recently watched John Oliver's video[1] about the \"pig butchering\" scam.","text":"I recently watched John Oliver's video[1] about the \"pig butchering\" scam. It's a brutal scheme where scammers invest months in building fake relationships, gain a ton of trust, and then rob all of their money. Even though I'm pretty tech-savvy, I'm not sure I could totally avoid falling for this. These scammers don't ask for money directly; they casually mention how they're making big bucks through crypto trading on some app. Naturally, you get curious about the app, but you're still cautious. Then you see it's got a ton of good reviews on the app store, so your trust increases a little. You install the app, and it looks legit - like it was made by a solid dev team. It offers some limited-time crypto deals where you can't withdraw for a while. The victim invests a little, watches the crypto value climb, and sees their \"money\" grow on paper. So they put in more. When they finally try to cash out, they realize they can't. They panic and turn to their \"romantic partner\" for help. That's when the scammers and the fake app squeeze out the last bit of cash, claiming it's for taxes or fees, and they need to put some money. And the victim loses everything. It's not unreasonable for the victim to think the relationship is real if they've spent months chatting and calling, sharing really personal stuff. Plus, the app seems totally legit, both from the store reviews and how it looks and works. I really hope these scams will disappear soon. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39746854,"author":"xyst","month":"2024-03","story":"Filipino police free slaves toiling in romance scam operation","stance":"rec","why":"Halfway decent segment on pig butchering","quote":"halfway decent segment on pig butchering on \"Last Week Tonight\" with John Oliver [4]","text":"These pig butchering scams play the long game with their victims. It's crazy. The payoffs are huge as well. These are just some of the stories I have heard through various mediums (ie, podcasts, news stories, media segments) - Kansas bank CEO charged with $50M embezzlement as a result of being a victim to \"pig butchery\". The bank itself was forced into bankruptcy due to this debacle [1] - Woman lost $450K in pig butchering scam [2] - US government often the subject of cybersecurity fraud as well. Pandemic relief programs paid out billions to scammers [3] - halfway decent segment on pig butchering on \"Last Week Tonight\" with John Oliver [4] Researching potential victims is so easy now with the proliferation of online dating apps. Then given how many breaches there are amongst the many services we use, it's even easier to gather more data points that can be used against a victim to drop their defenses (ie, social engineering tactics). The Nigerian scammers of yesteryear have learned the errors of their ways. Notes taken from the \"cat fishers\" of the 2010s. The Indian scam call centers have graduated from script based tech support scammers to these romance scams as well since it has a larger audience beyond the elderly. Even preying on their own countrymen. At this point, I think any relationship that continues online for more than a month and refuses to meet IRL. The relationship should be sus. I wouldn't even trust Zoom/Facetime/Webex /Meets with the accessibility of AI to gen"},{"id":41709920,"author":"coleca","month":"2024-10","story":"Ask HN: Should you reply STOP to unwanted texts?","stance":"reference","why":"Segment showing unwanted texts are pig butchering scams","quote":"John Oliver did a whole segment on his show about it this summer.","text":"Look up \"pig butchering\" (seriously). That is what those texts are all about. John Oliver did a whole segment on his show about it this summer. TL;DR they are scams [VIDEO]"},{"id":41203260,"author":"fragmede","month":"2024-08","story":"USPS text scammers duped his wife, so he hacked their operation","stance":"reference","why":"Supplies the link to the Oliver segment","quote":"John Oliver: Pig butchering scam.","text":"Not sure why the chilling effect for linking to it, you have 26k karma, but here it is: John Oliver: Pig butchering scam. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39629527,"author":"noirscape","month":"2024-03","story":"On DMA eve, Google whines, Apple sounds alarms, and TikTok wants out","stance":"reference","why":"Segment on pig butchering scams that used fake apps on the App Store","quote":"John Oliver ran an LWT piece just last week about Pig Butchering scams[0]","text":"It's also not like Apple does a good job preventing scams on the app store to begin with. John Oliver ran an LWT piece just last week about Pig Butchering scams[0], which involved fake stock trading apps, with fake bought reviews, being used in... you guessed it, the iOS App Store. It's just such a nakedly bad argument because Apple already does a garbage job at maintaining the App Store against these supposed external threats. [0]: A \"long con\" scam, where the victim (the \"pig\") is lulled into a false sense of trust for an online stranger (making them fat and ready to for slaughter) and then convinced to spend large sums of money on dubious trading platforms (\"being butchered\") at a vulnerable moment. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39636748,"author":"manquer","month":"2024-03","story":"FBI: U.S. lost record $12.5B to online crime in 2023","stance":"reference","why":"Source of a figure about scam industry share of GDP","quote":"50% number came from the John Oliver segment [3]","text":"> 75 billion number comes from a study available on SSRN I am aware, I got the date range from page 2 of the study. 50% number came from the John Oliver segment [3] in which he refers to this UNODC report[4] (page 2 ). > \"Research carried out by UNODC to understand the illicit financial flows (IFFs) in the Mekong region suggests that the scam industry in one country in the region may be generating between USD7.5 and USD12.5 billion, which is half this country’s gross domestic product \" > least be a massive countrywide construction boom Why should there be one? These countries are only base of operations, the operators are likely neither local nor spending the money in these countries. The scammers - the ones doing the talking are also victims, UN OHCR reports their numbers in hundreds of thousands [1][2]. There are lot of illicit money flowing through tiny regions with favorable governance like say Cayman islands or BVI it doesn't reflect in the local economy all that much. There is of course more than just money moving through here , there are also tens of thousands people working to do this but in most countries in this region few hundred thousand people are not significant and they are not paid much if at all. Both report cite internal analysis, I am not saying UN sources can not be wrong, they of course can. However in the absence of strong counter data point I would trust it. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"}],"r":[["9W74aeuqsiU",1],["0XlO39kCQ-8",1],["5TkIe60y2GI",1],["bglWCuCMSWc",1],["S7TUe5w6RHo",1],["v3y8AIEX_dU",1],["Ds-v2-qyCc8",1],["sI-1YNAmWlk",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["VQKMoT-6XSg",1],["Iyp_X3mwE1w",1],["S0qjK3TWZE8",1],["M8F5hXqS-Ac",1],["CUjt36SD3h8",1],["oqu5DjzOBF8",1],["tLdRBsuvVKc",1]]},"GnBbhXBDmwU":{"wy":"Frequently linked as an entertaining and alarming account of OpenSSL code quality and why the fork happened.","q":{"x":"The entire talk is amazing, even 7 years later.","i":29040014,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29040014,"author":"mrweasel","month":"2021-10","story":"OpenBSD: How it all started","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on LibreSSL's first 30 days","quote":"The entire talk is amazing, even 7 years later.","text":"Bob Beck also covers this in his talk about the first 30 days of libreSSL. The entire talk is amazing, even 7 years later. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34699617,"author":"g0xA52A2A","month":"2023-02","story":"OpenSSL Security Advisory [7th February 2023]","stance":"rec","why":"Recounts fixing long-ignored OpenSSL allocator issues","quote":"around the 10 minute mark he recounts fixing issues in the memory allocator which had been reported 4 years prior","text":"This exemplifies one of the key reasons OpenBSD forked OpenSSL. For those who haven't seen it I'd recommend this presentation from Bob Beck[1], around the 10 minute mark he recounts fixing issues in the memory allocator which had been reported 4 years prior. Seems there's still a similar backlog sitting behind embargoes? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29082888,"author":"Bayart","month":"2021-11","story":"U.S. telecoms are going to start physically removing Huawei gear","stance":"rec","why":"Hilarious talk on pruning OpenSSL","quote":"talk from Bob Beck of OpenBSD on pruning OpenSSL","text":"The immediate effect of Heartbleed was the OpenBSD folk [1] and Google [2] forking OpenSSL. There's a talk from Bob Beck of OpenBSD on pruning OpenSSL, it's pretty hilarious [3]. In that case open source was at least able to react appropriately, even if it didn't act preemptively. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10420615,"author":"teamhappy","month":"2015-10","story":"BoringSSL","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of what is wrong with OpenSSL","quote":"I recommend you watch","text":"It does a lot of things it really shouldn't be doing. I recommend you watch Bob Beck's talk about LibreSSL[1] (Ted's there too). [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7812122,"author":"protomyth","month":"2014-05","story":"BSDCan 2014 Videos","stance":"rec","why":"Funny and scary LibreSSL talk","quote":"The LibreSSL talk is funny and scary.","text":"The LibreSSL talk is funny and scary. [watch the other version one [VIDEO] so you can hear the other people and see the end]"},{"id":7818386,"author":"lomnakkus","month":"2014-05","story":"OpenSSL, OpenSSH and NTP to receive support from Core Infrastructure Initiative","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what LibreSSL is about: fixing OpenSSL problems","quote":"please see this talk","text":"\"just fixing the problems in openssl.\" That's what libressl is about. If you're in any doubt, please see this talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46227068,"author":"shagie","month":"2025-12","story":"Rubio stages font coup: Times New Roman ousts Calibri","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> And Comic Sans for letters sent to friends finishing design school, obviously. ... and libressl. [LINK] (and the talk - [VIDEO] )"},{"id":31693468,"author":"e12e","month":"2022-06","story":"Important PostgreSQL 14 update to avoid silent corruption of indexes","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a terrible huge C codebase","quote":"(the first 30 days)","text":"> I've never seen a terrible huge C codebase libssl/openssl (before the rewrite/fixes especially)? Maybe doesn't qualify as huge? See eg: [VIDEO] \"LibreSSL with Bob Beck\" (the first 30 days)"},{"id":27314201,"author":"protomyth","month":"2021-05","story":"Replacements for existing software written in Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Notes OpenSSL was seen as bad code before Heartbleed","quote":"Bob Beck pointed that out in his LibreSSL talk","text":"I don't think anyone thought OpenSSL was good code even before Heartbleed. Bob Beck pointed that out in his LibreSSL talk [VIDEO]"},{"id":23875021,"author":"protomyth","month":"2020-07","story":"What changed in OpenSSL after heartbleed","stance":"reference","why":"Horror story from audience about big-endian support","quote":"the explanation from the person in the crowd is something of a horror story","text":"Big-endian Support for AMD64 - the explanation from the person in the crowd is something of a horror story that I think a lot of have witnessed in various forms. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16588206,"author":"protomyth","month":"2018-03","story":"Why Is SQLite Coded in C? (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Overview of how OpenSSL was regarded","quote":"had a good overview of what was thought of OpenSSL","text":"Bob Beck during his LibreSSL talk had a good overview of what was thought of OpenSSL [VIDEO] \"we are all guilty\""},{"id":12751907,"author":"gbrown_","month":"2016-10","story":"OpenSSL after Heartbleed","stance":"reference","why":"Fork motivated by memory allocation, not Heartbleed itself","quote":"The reason for the fork was not Heartbleed itself but the crazy memory allocation done behind the scenes.","text":"> it was created as a direct response to Heartbleed The reason for the fork was not Heartbleed itself but the crazy memory allocation done behind the scenes. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11661113,"author":"protomyth","month":"2016-05","story":"Why OpenBSD Is Important to Me","stance":"reference","why":"LibreSSL first 30 days includes oddities and comments","quote":"LibrSSL first 30 days along with quite a lot of other oddities.","text":"I meant my comment in the same spirit as a Southerner means \"Bless your heart\". I do believe Ted Unagnt's comment is included in the [VIDEO] LibrSSL first 30 days along with quite a lot of other oddities."},{"id":7818041,"author":"clarry","month":"2014-05","story":"OpenSSL, OpenSSH and NTP to receive support from Core Infrastructure Initiative","stance":"reference","why":"Video of the LibreSSL talk accompanying the slides","quote":"Just in case someone missed it","text":"Just in case someone missed it, here's video of the libressl talk to go with the linked slide(s). [VIDEO]"},{"id":7818365,"author":"forgottenpass","month":"2014-05","story":"OpenSSL, OpenSSH and NTP to receive support from Core Infrastructure Initiative","stance":"reference","why":"OpenBSD developer explains why OpenSSL was beyond saving","quote":"An OpenBSD guy gave a talk a few weeks back","text":"I'd much rather have 1 really really good ssl library that everyone uses instead of 2 so-so ones. That's reasonable, but those aren't the options at play here. Not only because GnuTLS is already a thing, but the chances of OpenSSL becoming really really good are questionable. An OpenBSD guy gave a talk a few weeks back where he said that Heartbleed wasn't the reason for the split, it was the reason for digging into the code and realizing that OpenSSL under current leadership isn't capable of being a really really good option. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7820423,"author":"simula67","month":"2014-05","story":"OpenSSL, OpenSSH and NTP to receive support from Core Infrastructure Initiative","stance":"reference","why":"Talk admits cleanup, not cryptographic review, so far","quote":"they are not reviewing the cryptography code, merely cleaning up the programming","text":"From Beck's talk[1] they are not reviewing the cryptography code, merely cleaning up the programming. They admit they don't yet have cryptography experts reviewing if the algorithms/implementations are doing what they are supposed to be doing and that they are in fact secure. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7826553,"author":"protomyth","month":"2014-05","story":"Pointers Gone Wild","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why nobody looked at the unsafe OpenSSL code","quote":"he explains why nobody looked (or admitted they looked)","text":"> Maybe I'm naieve, but I think perhaps the problems with OpenSSL had come to light a little bit sooner if all dangerous code had clearly been marked 'unsafe'. After listening to Bob Beck's talk[1], I think people did know how unsafe that code was but no one wanted to take a shot at fixing it until it all went bad. At 2:12 into the video (the Jon Snow slide), he explains why nobody looked (or admitted they looked)[2]. Code can be so bad in any language to discourage people from even attempting to figure out how it works. 1) [VIDEO] 2) quotes \"Why did WE not look\" & \"It is your parents talking about how you got made horrible\""},{"id":8075720,"author":"x0x0","month":"2014-07","story":"This is why software sucks","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They let critical security bugs sit in their queue for 4 years while they sat on them [1].","text":"to be blunt: openssl is a festering heap maintained by a bunch of hacks. They let critical security bugs sit in their queue for 4 years while they sat on them [1]. They also actively worked to disable automated testing with malloc tooling; while obviously that won't catch all bugs, every bit helps. Finally, it contained rafts of code that, bluntly, my boss would probably fire me for checking in. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7855049,"author":"x0x0","month":"2014-06","story":"New Bug Found in Widely Used OpenSSL Encryption","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"letting serious security issues sit in their bug tracker for years on end","text":"in the sense that it is utterly wrong, I suppose so... in reality, openssl appears to be a $1mm+/year for-profit fips consulting business [1] that appears to not care much about security, letting serious security issues sit in their bug tracker for years on end [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["l6XQUciI-Sc",1],["fE2KDzZaxvE",1],["0wtvQZijPzg",1],["EESYomdoeCs",1],["KXuZi9aeGTw",1],["RIctzAQOe44",1],["oM6S7FEUfkU",1],["3nwwKbM_vJc",1],["s8nFqwOho-s",1]]},"KAWA1DuvCnQ":{"wy":"HN commenters bring it up whenever C++ complexity or the pitfalls of language design come up, as a witty and authoritative warning.","q":{"x":"everyone should watch Scott Meyers's awesome presentation at a D conference","i":27938191,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27938191,"author":"jejones3141","month":"2021-07","story":"Modern C++ for C Programmers (2018)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Meyers on how convoluted C++ has become.","quote":"everyone should watch Scott Meyers's awesome presentation at a D conference","text":"Yes, and everyone should watch Scott Meyers's awesome presentation at a D conference with the title \"The Last Thing D Needs\". (tl,dr version: The last thing D needs is someone like him, really to be so insanely convoluted, as C++ is, as to need someone like him.) Link to the video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36197721,"author":"nordsieck","month":"2023-06","story":"Never trust a programmer who says they know C++","stance":"rec","why":"Meyers on C++ complexity.","quote":"I'll just leave this fantastic talk here","text":"I'll just leave this fantastic talk here: The Last Thing D Needs - Scott Meyers (2014) [VIDEO] The C++ type system really is quite... complicated."},{"id":33654186,"author":"noselasd","month":"2022-11","story":"Rust in the 6.2 kernel","stance":"rec","why":"Traps and pitfalls of C++ as warning for Rust.","quote":"Scott Meyers have a very nice talk here","text":"> You cannot say the same thing about Rust. At least not yet. My fear is it's _very_ easy to fall in to the same trap as C++ has, and become a big mess. Scott Meyers have a very nice talk here: [VIDEO] about all these traps and pifalls that we have to deal with in C++. That said, I'm starting a bit with rust and enjoy it so a lot, especially the tooling alone is worth it - and if I never have to write another line of C++, I would be very happy (ofc the real world argues I have a lot of C++ code that will need maintenance)."},{"id":17121451,"author":"SloopJon","month":"2018-05","story":"Meta Crush Saga: a C++17 compile-time game","stance":"rec","why":"Meyers on essential versus accidental complexity in C++.","quote":"You might enjoy the 2014 talk","text":"> C++ developers .. don't seem to be interested in actually improving the language In his talks at the 2014 and 2017 conferences for the D programming langue, Scott Meyers noted the difference between what individual C++ committee members say they value, and what the process actually produces. Will a new standard every three years make this better or worse? They do seem open to deprecating things that don't work. You might enjoy the 2014 talk, called \"The Last Thing D Needs.\" I don't want to spoil the punchline, which actually struck me as kind of bittersweet, but Scott talked about the distinction that Fred Brooks made in \"No Silver Bullet\" between essential and accidental complexity. C++ does have a lot of the latter. Edit: link for the 2014 talk: [VIDEO] and for the 2017 talk: [LINK]"},{"id":16839366,"author":"nordsieck","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among favourite tech talks.","quote":"","text":"We Really Don't Know How to Compute: Gerry Sussman - [LINK] Zebras All the Way Down: Bryan Cantrill - [LINK] Jonathan Blow on Deep Work: Jonathan Blow - [LINK] Simple Made Easy: Rich Hickey - [LINK] Effective Programs - 10 Years of Clojure: Rich Hickey - [LINK] The Last Thing D Needs: Scott Meyers - [VIDEO]"},{"id":44981393,"author":"hitekker","month":"2025-08","story":"The Core of Rust","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Your point on coherence is similar to the perspective of an ex-C++ maintainer. This video came out a decade ago [VIDEO] and I feel his lesson went unheeded. It's relevant to the bigger and more dangerous concept of Conceptual Integrity by Fred Brooks."},{"id":43959459,"author":"AlexCoventry","month":"2025-05","story":"Writing an LLM from scratch, part 13 – attention heads are dumb","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Scott Myers, author of the extremely popular Effective C++ explainers, gave up on the language because of its churn. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43260511,"author":"fuzztester","month":"2025-03","story":"Ask HN: What less-popular systems programming language are you using?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">I'd kill for an \"Effective D\" book [LINK] The Last Thing D Needs - Scott Meyers - DConf 2014 [VIDEO]"},{"id":39685917,"author":"vlovich123","month":"2024-03","story":"C++ Safety, in Context","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Instead of defensiveness, why not talk about the ways in which the C++ committee is changing how they're operating (or even leaving ISO) and changing their culture to shore up these things. Look at past Scott Meyers talks (e.g. [1]). He highlights how the committee has arbitrary set of principles that can be applied to justify or reject any proposal and the inconsistencies in the language are a reflection of this. This isn't a problem of the language itself but rather that it's design by committee with 4 major front-end implementations (Intel, MSVC, Clang, GCC although I believe Intel is standardizing on the Clang front-end at least). Organizational issues like that are tough to spot but it's become clear now for several years that Rust is going to beat C++ silly if the C++ committee doesn't clean up their act and steer the C++ community a bit better (e.g. still no ABI specification, no improvements on macros, no standardized build system, modules are a joke, no standardized package system, etc etc). They're not effective stewards not least of which because they can't even take good impactful ideas from Rust and copy them. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38504648,"author":"Kranar","month":"2023-12","story":"Talking with the Inventor of C++","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'd say a lot of this is a form of survivorship bias. I've programmed in C++ since the mid 90s so I am used to a lot of C++'s issues, but I also run a company and have to train new engineers and I see first hand the absolute nightmare that a lot of people encounter learning this language and it's demoralizing. While to me a lot of this is stuff I managed to figure out, I also understand that for many new developers this is just crap that no one should have to deal with in 2023. Lack of a good way to build third party libraries, build times that are astronomical and a true productivity killer, error messages that are entirely unhelpful and can produce giant walls of text for the most trivial of reasons, rampant undefined behavior that is incredibly hard to identify. Several things changed my view on C++ and now I see it mostly as a sort of zombie language. 1. As I mentioned once I started my own business and needed to train new engineers on how to use C++, I started to look at the language from a fresh point of view and realized it really is a complex mess just to get basic things working. 2. Scott Meyers \"retiring\" from C++, and in particular this video: [VIDEO] 3. I attended an ISO C++ meeting in Toronto and while I absolutely found that the people were very intelligent and knowledgeable, as well as well intentioned, I realized just how political C++ is and that much of its development and complexity is not due to technical constraints or driven based on what is in the best "},{"id":36929027,"author":"mananaysiempre","month":"2023-07","story":"Why I use the D programming language for scripting (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It is definitely related to C++ (both in ideology and in participants) and AFAIU even more features, but, for example, Scott Meyers views it[1] as having less random stupid inconsistencies than C++. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["2V1FtfBDsLU",1],["zVhQOhxb1Mc",1],["RUwS1uAdUcI",1]]},"mVVNJKv9esE":{"wy":"HN points to it as thought-provoking material on the tradeoffs and costs of abstraction, especially in frontend tooling debates.","q":{"x":"This talk is incredible food for thought","i":13967225,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13967225,"author":"K0SM0S","month":"2017-03","story":"Next.js 2.0","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Thought-provoking look at abstraction state of affairs circa 2016.","quote":"This talk is incredible food for thought","text":"This talk is incredible food for thought: On the spectrum of abstraction by Cheng Lou (react-europe 2016) [1] If you've read some of the classics, such as Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs (1979, Gerald Jay Sussman and Hal Abelson) [2], this talk is a really interesting look at the state of affairs on abstraction circa 2016. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":33230938,"author":"hnbad","month":"2022-10","story":"Moving from React to htmx","stance":"rec","why":"Tradeoffs of template languages versus React's approach.","quote":"There was a good talk about the spectrum of abstractions","text":"I know, this isn't a novel concept. There was a good talk about the spectrum of abstractions and the tradeoffs of template languages vs React's approach at React Europe a couple of years ago (in fact, he goes into that this means there are some optimizations that are simply unavailable to React): [VIDEO] The question isn't whether DSLs or \"just JS\" are more powerful, the question is which tradeoffs they represent and which tradeoffs you prefer. It seems that HTMX is primarily based in the Python ecosystem and as a former Pythonista I see the advantage of having a DSL over having to write JS, especially that the former is much narrower, which is what you want in this situation."},{"id":19951287,"author":"namelosw","month":"2019-05","story":"Virtual DOM is pure overhead (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent talk on function composition as abstraction.","quote":"Cheng Lou also has a really excellent talk on the topic","text":"React is more like 'Lambda the Ultimate Web Component'. A component is almost a function returns element. Expanding a component is like calling a function and give it the property. So you can have some abstract common behavior in HOC f and g, then you can have HOC `h = compose(f(g))`. A quick comparison with Angular: @Component({template, style}) seems composable if we stretch a lot. But why make template and style in the decorator... They are not something we consider most abstract at all. The function is the simplest yet composable abstraction over the template, Cheng Lou also has a really excellent talk on the topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16841186,"author":"rk06","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Guides choosing between competing solutions when the answer is it depends.","quote":"it guides on how to choose between competing solutions to a problem","text":"The spectrum of abstraction: [VIDEO] This is an high level talk. it guides on how to choose between competing solutions to a problem when the commonly accepted answer is \"it depends\""},{"id":12527378,"author":"Rygu","month":"2016-09","story":"On DRY and the cost of wrongful abstractions","stance":"rec","why":"Visual explanation of the cost of abstractions.","quote":"For a more visual explanation of the \"cost of abstractions\" watch this presentation by Cheng Lou (Facebook).","text":"For a more visual explanation of the \"cost of abstractions\" watch this presentation by Cheng Lou (Facebook). [VIDEO]"},{"id":17540794,"author":"YuukiRey","month":"2018-07","story":"Webpack 4 course: What is Webpack?","stance":"reference","why":"Argues DSLs are inadequate when requirements change; higher-level abstraction preferable.","quote":"mentions some arguments why a DSL would not be a good approach for frontend/JS build tools","text":"Interesting that you should mention it. This talk [VIDEO] mentions some arguments why a DSL would not be a good approach for frontend/JS build tools. Namely that a DSL isn't adequate if the underlying best practices or requirements change a lot. That's why operating at a higher level of abstraction (function chaining/piping/composition) would make more sense. Now... that doesn't really favor Webpack. And I still don't quite understand why that tool is so popular."}],"r":[["48sCx-wBs34",1],["-I_jE0l7sYQ",1],["Mlrrc_vy79E",1],["L-x8DYTOv7w",1],["biYciU1uiUw",1],["xMabpBvtXr4",1],["P55t6eryY3g",1],["3Y3jE3B8HsE",1],["4xGawJIseNY",1],["_3loq22TxSc",1],["uNjxe8ShM-8",1],["LMbI6sk-62E",1]]},"uk3A41U0iO4":{"wy":"Recommended as a fantastic talk on code-driven CAD and custom keyboard design, linked in threads on OpenSCAD and keyboards.","q":{"x":"it is a fantastic talk but the part related to this starts at 17:35","i":28909206,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28909206,"author":"tjoff","month":"2021-10","story":"Code CAD – Use code to create CAD models","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic talk demonstrating Clojure transpiled to OpenSCAD for keyboard design.","quote":"it is a fantastic talk but the part related to this starts at 17:35","text":"There is a small clojure project that transpiles clojure to openscad, (if I remember correctly...) [LINK] Demonstrated and talked about in this clip: [VIDEO] edit: it is a fantastic talk but the part related to this starts at 17:35"},{"id":22560810,"author":"tjoff","month":"2020-03","story":"OpenJSCAD","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on 3D printing ergonomic keyboards using Clojure and OpenSCAD.","quote":"enjoyed this talk (about 3d printing ergonomic keyboards using the above)","text":"There is an attempt to be able to write it in clojure. [LINK] Haven't tried it but enjoyed this talk (about 3d printing ergonomic keyboards using the above): [VIDEO]"},{"id":20113535,"author":"Tepix","month":"2019-06","story":"Tokyo mechanical keyboard meetup photos","stance":"rec","why":"Procedurally generated keyboard frame designed in Clojure.","quote":"Here's another great video about a procedurally generated keyboard frame","text":"Here's another great video about a procedurally generated keyboard frame clojure.core/typing - Matt Adereth [VIDEO]"},{"id":19792135,"author":"simias","month":"2019-04","story":"I made a smart watch from scratch","stance":"rec","why":"Step-by-step design of a custom keyboard using a Clojure DSL over OpenSCAD.","quote":"it's a step-by-step explanation of how he designed his dream keyboard","text":"Very impressive and inspiring. I've been thinking about making a custom keyboard for a while now but, while I have no problems with the electronic and software side of things, I'm a bit overwhelmed with the 3D modelling required. I tried learning Blender but it really feels ill-suited for designing physical objects. The author mentions considering openSCAD but I've heard pretty bad things about it so I haven't really given it a chance yet. The person making the dactyl keyboard had a very interesting talk where he explain how he basically ended up writing his own DSL in clojure on top of openSCAD, maybe I should try that: [VIDEO] (if you enjoyed TFA you'll probably like this video as well, it's a step-by-step explanation of how he designed his dream keyboard). Otherwise I suppose I'll have to betray rms and try this freeware Fusion 360 thing."},{"id":12639865,"author":"petr_tik","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"History of keyboards and a custom keyboard written in Clojure.","quote":"History of keyboards and a custom keyboard written in Clojure","text":"1 Martin Thompson busting myths about hardware and explaining why it's important to know. Mechanical sympathy makes you better, because you know how the code actually runs on the machine and interacts with different layers of memory [LINK] 2 Matt Godbolt (the man behind GCC explorer) - Emulating a 6502 system in Javascript Great talk about BBC micro and much more [LINK] 3 Matt Adereth - Clojure/typing History of keyboards and a custom keyboard written in Clojure [VIDEO] I like the 3 for their content and how each speaker presented the background and their project/hack/ideas. Highly recommend"},{"id":34741453,"author":"mvnuweucxqokii","month":"2023-02","story":"Show HN: Dslcad a programming language and interpreter for building 3D models","stance":"reference","why":"Clojure wrapper around OpenSCAD used to design a keyboard.","quote":"Years ago I came across this talk about a guy who built a Clojure wrapper around OpenSCAD in order to design his own keyboard.","text":"Years ago I came across this talk about a guy who built a Clojure wrapper around OpenSCAD in order to design his own keyboard. Potentially relevant: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18805313,"author":"nicwest","month":"2019-01","story":"OpenSCAD: Software for creating solid 3D CAD objects","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"have enjoyed programing objects in cljoure with scad-clj.","text":"I recently bought a 3D printer and have enjoyed programing objects in cljoure with scad-clj. [LINK] This is biggest project I know of that uses it: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16217371,"author":"sedachv","month":"2018-01","story":"Perl 6 Optimism","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> That being said I agree that these days the tendency across all languages is to go towards fewer special characters, not more. In this context Perl 6's syntax feels a bit anachronistic. This is just a comeback of a recurring dumb trend based on the fallacy that describing the operations of a program using English words is always superior to using appropriate specialized notation. The core of the fallacy is assuming that because something looks like a familiar English word, people who do not understand programming will understand the corresponding semantics of the operation in the programming language, because they know English. Which is complete nonsense - people who do not understand programming will reason by analogy, and through trial and error end up at a partially wrong and misguided understanding of that operation. English words only start you off with unnecessary baggage in the learning process. It is even worse for people who already know programming. A C for loop is not a JavaScript for loop is not a Python for loop... The details of their semantics are very different, and you will introduce bugs if you just assume \"I know how for loops work!\" There is nothing new about this trend either; it was the central premise behind COBOL, and a big motivation for BASIC. What BASIC was in the 1980s, Python is today. What is ironic about this trend resurfacing today is that it has never been easier to use special characters - Unicode is everywhere, and even making custom keybo"},{"id":12678425,"author":"Adrock","month":"2016-10","story":"How I built a keyboard by hand","stance":"reference","why":"Author's own conference talk about the Dactyl keyboard.","quote":"There's also a talk I gave a Clojure/conj about it here","text":"Thanks for the mention! For anyone interested, all the source and models for the Dactyl keyboard are available here: [LINK] There's also a talk I gave a Clojure/conj about it here: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ainyK6fXku0",1],["oVfHeWTKjag",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["HeQX2HjkcNo",1]]},"QZy4Yml3LTY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the fastest way to learn lenses by example, with far-out examples later on.","q":{"x":"you can learn enough lens to be productive in 1 hours with this talk without reading docs at all","i":23624492,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":41028127,"author":"turboponyy","month":"2024-07","story":"Ask HN: What resources do you recommend for learning Haskell?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great introduction to lenses by example, with humorous audience.","quote":"serves as a great introduction by example","text":"As with anything Haskell, one could simply reply with \"here's the formal definition.\" But in easily digestible terms, it's a way to narrow your view over a collection to a subset that satisfies some property, and perhaps perform some operations on that subset. This video presentation (with a very humorous audience dynamic, might I add) serves as a great introduction by example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23624492,"author":"platz","month":"2020-06","story":"Haskell for a New Decade [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Learn enough lens usage to be productive in an hour.","quote":"you can learn enough lens to be productive in 1 hours with this talk without reading docs at all","text":"and you can learn enough lens to be productive in 1 hours with this talk without reading docs at all. You can skip the theory and go straight to usage: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33417235,"author":"lgas","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: What piece of code/codebase blew your mind when you saw it?","stance":"rec","why":"Good lens video with far-out examples in the later portion.","quote":"I would also recommend as a good video on lenses","text":"Definitely agree on lenses being one of the more mind blowing things I've encountered and eventually grokked in programming. I would also recommend [VIDEO] as a good video on lenses in part because the latter portion has some pretty far-out examples of that deep well you mentioned."},{"id":31668429,"author":"ParetoOptimal","month":"2022-06","story":"Dhall: A Gateway Drug to Haskell","stance":"rec","why":"Good examples of lens usage.","quote":"Good examples in \"Putting lenses to work\" by John Weigley","text":"Good examples in \"Putting lenses to work\" by John Weigley: [VIDEO] Then for a great book that gives a great understanding see Chris Penner's amazing and fun \"Optics by example: [LINK]"},{"id":31891576,"author":"ParetoOptimal","month":"2022-06","story":"Lenses","stance":"rec","why":"More examples of lenses beyond record access.","quote":"More are in this presentation","text":"> Lenses let you do some amazing things, but I think most of their existence is just thanks to how truly horrible (nested) record field access and updates were. Only for the most basic uses. Architecting programs as folds or traversals can be powerful. One simple example of lenses i can think of being useful for more than record access is: > sumOf (folded . folded) [Just 1, Just 2] 3 More are in this presentation: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["fNpsgTIpODA",1],["_8bhtEgB8Mo",1],["ftcIcn8AmSY",1],["2DIl3Hfh9tY",1],["Agu6jipKfYw",1],["MEUWxNifJJ8",1],["FvNRlE4E9QQ",1],["74zOY-vgkyw",1]]},"hEx5DNLWGgA":{"wy":"HN cites it in C++ versus Rust safety debates for its lifetime profile demo, and recommends it as a modern C++ talk.","q":{"x":"Take a look at Herb Sutter's CppCon talk this year","i":10289585,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":16536336,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-03","story":"Ask HN: Best way to learn modern C++?","stance":"rec","why":"One of several recommended talks for learning modern C++.","quote":"Some of the Herb Sutter videos, \"Writing Good C++14... By Default","text":"- Tour of C++ ( [LINK] ) - Principles and Practice Using C++ ( [LINK] ) - From Mathematics to Generic Programming ( [LINK] ) - The Scott Meyers books Some of the Bjarne Stroustrup videos, \"Learning and Teaching Modern C++\" - [LINK] Some of the Herb Sutter videos, \"Writing Good C++14... By Default\" - [VIDEO] \"Back to the Basics! Essentials of Modern C++ Style\" - [LINK] Some of the Kate Gregory videos, \"Stop Teaching C\" - [LINK] \"10 Core Guidelines You Need to Start Using Now\" - [LINK] \"It's Complicated\" - [LINK]"},{"id":11721769,"author":"e12e","month":"2016-05","story":"Why I don't spend time with Modern C++ anymore","stance":"rec","why":"Uplifting talk on modern C++ among a curated CppCon playlist.","quote":"Is also rather uplifting","text":"I've been spending a few days going through talks from last years cppcon[1]. They're all good, but they've all left me with a strong desire for a strongly opinionated, there-is-no-past-only-the-future introduction to C++14 (and incidentally, C11). I've added a few of those talks I've watched and liked to a small playlist (with a couple of other talks on programming, unrelated - the relevant talks are prefixed with \"cppcon 2015\"): [LINK] The full cppcon playlist is here: [LINK] Of special relevance is the rather excellent talk by Kate Gregory on \"Stop Teaching C\" (when you're supposed to do an intro course to C++): \"CppCon 2015: Kate Gregory “Stop Teaching C\"\": [LINK] (But again, I want an open wiki/live tutorial, along the lines of \"How I start\", but longer, which goes through all this stuff. We really should make one, both for C++14 and for C11). CppCon 2015: Herb Sutter \"Writing Good C++14... By Default\": [VIDEO] Is also rather uplifting, and: CppCon 2015: Sean Parent \"Better Code: Data Structures\": [LINK] makes a pretty good case for simple, modern C++ being clear, easy and efficient. Finally, CppCon 2015: Phil Nash “Test Driven C++ with Catch”: [LINK] apart for being a pretty straight forward intro to a simple testing framework, appears to reveal some secrets combining template magic and still get sane compile times (hint: don't recompile more (template) code on every iteration than you need to). There's a couple of good ones that touch on meta-programming too (Look for t"},{"id":10719091,"author":"jbandela1","month":"2015-12","story":"Ask HN: C, C++, or Rust which language to learn next?","stance":"rec","why":"Sutter presents free static analysis tooling to improve C++ safety.","quote":"Take a look at Herb Sutter's talk from CppCon 2015.","text":"Take a look at Herb Sutter's talk from CppCon 2015. The slides are here ( [LINK] ) and the video is here [VIDEO] . Microsoft will be releasing the static analysis tool as a free cross-platform tool (and I think open-source IIRC). They shipped a preview for Visual Studio 2015 with Update 1, and are planning on shipping a cross-platform version soon. As tools like this become free and ubiquitous, it will greatly improve C++ code safety."},{"id":10485314,"author":"jbandela1","month":"2015-11","story":"Rust Lifetimes","stance":"rec","why":"Sutter addresses lifetime safety with a demo of an upcoming static analysis tool.","quote":"Here is a video of his talk and includes a demo of the upcoming static analysis tool","text":"At this year's CppCon Herb Sutter the chair of the ISO C++ committee addressed just this issue of lifetime safety. Here is a video of his talk and includes a demo of the upcoming static analysis tool that can do this with very minimal annotations [VIDEO] I remember thinking when watching the talk that this was a very bad day for Rust as C++ could now do most of what Rust was promising"},{"id":10289585,"author":"something123","month":"2015-09","story":"When Rust Makes Sense, or The State of Typed Languages","stance":"rec","why":"Points to Sutter's talk on making C++ code safe by default.","quote":"Take a look at Herb Sutter's CppCon talk this year","text":"We should be seeing completely safe C++ code soon. Take a look at Herb Sutter's CppCon talk this year: [VIDEO] Reinventing the wheel isn't entirely necessary. Though if you're a SysAdmin and you write your codebase in Rust.. that's some nice job security. ;)"},{"id":12984225,"author":"MaulingMonkey","month":"2016-11","story":"Rust and the Future of Systems Programming [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows MSVC's static analysis detecting invalidated pointers.","quote":"MSVC is continuing to improve their detection of invalidated pointers","text":"MSVC is continuing to improve their detection of invalidated pointers: [VIDEO] This is running the static analysis pass instead of the normal compile pass, but stuff is improving. Of course you're preaching to the choir as far as I'm concerned - this stuff is way late to the party, and speaking generally, has issues with false positives and failing to detect things."},{"id":11670785,"author":"swaroop","month":"2016-05","story":"A Farewell to FRP","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as a demo of lifetime-checking tools.","quote":"See for a demo of such tools.","text":"My understanding is that the tools are the answer - it's a linter that must be run before landing/merging. See [VIDEO] for a demo of such tools."},{"id":11157106,"author":"kibwen","month":"2016-02","story":"Rewrite Everything in Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Cited via MSDN as the source of the lifetime profile demo.","quote":"Tooling for the Lifetime profile demonstrated in Herb Sutter’s plenary talk","text":"I don't believe it is, unless something's changed. To quote MSDN: \"The package currently contains checkers for the Bounds and Type profiles. Tooling for the Lifetime profile demonstrated in Herb Sutter’s plenary talk (video at [VIDEO] ) will be made available in a future release of the code analysis tools.\" [LINK]"},{"id":10864966,"author":"jbandela1","month":"2016-01","story":"Why I love Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as showing C++ adopting borrow-checker-like tooling.","quote":"see Herb Sutter's talk at CppCon 2015","text":"I think that is a great answer. I actually see some parallels between the relationship of Rust/C++ to Scala/Java. Both basically use the backend of the established language and provide an 'improved' language on top of it. Scala uses the JVM, and Rust uses LLVM. What is interesting is that with the release of Java 8, a lot of Scala early adopters and cutting back their investment in Scala in favor of Java (for exampled LinkedIn). This is despite the fact that Scala is widely used in production, has a killer app in Spark, and allows pretty seamless use of the Java library. Now compare this to Rust. Rust also provides an 'improved' language in the same space as C++ utilizing LLVM. However, what is interesting is the nature of the improvements. The biggest feature of Rust is the memory safety. However, the memory safety is not a productivity improvement as more of a safety improvement. Unlike garbage collection where you just do not have to think about memory allocation issues, Rust forces you to think about ownership and mutability. That same exact thinking will allow you to pretty much get the same memory safety in C++ using unique_ptr and shared_ptr and const. The difference is that the compiler makes you think about it in Rust. Much like Scala inspired the adoption of features for Java, you can see C++ adopting pretty much the borrow checker as a tool - see Herb Sutter's talk at CppCon 2015 - [VIDEO] . As the experience with Scala shows, the adopted features of the establishe"},{"id":10958884,"author":"jbandela1","month":"2016-01","story":"Rust and the Blub Paradox","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among C++ talks addressing issues raised about Blub paradox.","quote":"Herb Sutter - Writing Good C++ by Default","text":"Wow, this is the first post I have ever read that even kind of implied that Andrei Alexandrescu was a Blub programmer. Part of being a Blub programmer is that you don't even think about the issues. In regards to the weird features brought up, the underlying issues behind these features have been in the C++ consciousness for some time. Below are some talks and resources covering at least some of these issues. Sean Parent - Inheritance is the Base Class of Evil - [LINK] Herb Sutter - You don't know const and mutable - [LINK] Andrei Alexandrescu - Systematic error handling in C++ - [LINK] Herb Sutter - Writing Good C++ by Default - [VIDEO] Andrew Sutton - Generic Programming with Concepts Lite - [LINK] One of the things that helps with avoiding \"Blubness\" in C++ is the significant interest in languages such as Haskel (which is not considered a Blub language by anyone except Agda programmers) and in applying the techniques and insights where possible to C++ whether through writing new libraries (see for example Fit by Paul Fultz II - [LINK] ) or through new language features (see this post by David Sankel - [LINK] ) Finally, is also interesting that Paul Graham's blub paradox is being brought up in regards to Rust, C++, and D. The Paul Graham article is in large part talking about the power of meta-programming. In this regard, D and C++ are significantly more powerful in that regard. Features like higher kinded types (template templates), variadics, and non-type template paramete"},{"id":10706689,"author":"jbandela1","month":"2015-12","story":"Ask HN: C, C++, or Rust which language to learn next?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence that C++ is adopting Rust-like safety tooling.","quote":"","text":"Learn C++. You will have the opportunity to learn low-level features but know how to wrap them up inside highly performant, high-level components. Rust is interesting, but I think it will remain an interesting language outside the mainstream without any major commercial backers (Mozilla does not count). In addition, C++ is taking a lot of the ideas from Rust and incorporating them along with tooling that will provide 95+% of the value of Rust while still keeping the advantages of C++ (see [VIDEO] ) What is interesting with C++ is that although a lot of vendors are pushing their own languages - Microsoft with C#, Apple with Swift, Google with Go, Facebook with Hack, Mozilla with Rust - they are all deeply involved with C++ because C++ powers their mission-critical code. Another way of putting it would be that despite all the promotion if all of Mozilla's Rust code were eliminated tomorrow nobody would notice, but if all of Mozilla's C++ code were eliminated, it would cease to exist. Also, C++ is rapidly evolving and by learning Modern C++ 14 now, you can avoid a lot of the mistakes of C++ that were common in the past and write safe, elegant code. C++ is also experiencing a renaissance (see [LINK] ). C++ is now the one high-level language that is natively supported by the system vendor across Windows, Linux, Mac, iOS, Android, Windows Phone, XBox, and Playstation."},{"id":10747383,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2015-12","story":"IncludeOS roadmap: tiny Node.js-style web services in highly efficient C++","stance":"reference","why":"Cites a timestamp where Sutter discusses lifetime annotations versus Rust.","quote":"around 00:32:00.","text":"Here CppCon 2015: Herb Sutter \"Writing Good C++14... By Default\" around 00:32:00. [VIDEO] > The work-in-progress ISO Core C++ lifetime profile does not work on unmodified code and requires a lot of annotations… Herb mentions that it still less than lifetimes in Rust given C++'s type system: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":10767168,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2015-12","story":"The Dangers of Super Smart Compilers","stance":"reference","why":"Cites Sutter's audience poll showing few use safety tools.","quote":"A tiny 1%, this on the conference of with many of the top C++ developers.","text":"If it was that easy. CppCon 2015, Herb Sutter's talk about writing safe code in C++14, just check how many in the audience use such tools. [VIDEO] A tiny 1%, this on the conference of with many of the top C++ developers. Now imagine how little they get used on the typical enterprise environment, specially worse with C, that lacks many of the C++ added safety layers."},{"id":10799718,"author":"gaul","month":"2015-12","story":"Optimizing C++ Const Objects in LLVM","stance":"reference","why":"Points to the timestamp where Sutter discusses const_cast.","quote":"Sutter comments on const_cast around 11m15s.","text":"CppCon 2015: Herb Sutter \"Writing Good C++14... By Default\": [VIDEO] Sutter comments on const_cast around 11m15s."},{"id":10282359,"author":"pjtr","month":"2015-09","story":"C++ Core Guidelines","stance":"reference","why":"Cites Sutter's audience poll about homegrown string types.","quote":"How many of you have your own homegrown string types at your companies?","text":"Sure. That was kind of my point. Still, as Herb Sutter asked at CppCon 2015: \"How many of you have your own homegrown string types at your companies? ... Don't lie. ... Right. And those of you who didn't put up your hand and are just bashful, ... we know we have them.\" [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10866180,"author":"pcwalton","month":"2016-01","story":"Why I love Rust","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Herb's claims about the ISO Core C++ lifetime profile requiring fewer annotations than Rust were incorrect.","text":"> you can see C++ adopting pretty much the borrow checker as a tool - see Herb Sutter's talk at CppCon 2015 - [VIDEO] . I have a lot of questions about this tool, both in terms of soundness/expressivity (how do you deal with shared_ptr and similar things if your goal is to statically guarantee no-alias on function entry) and in terms of practicality (the massive subsetting it requires basically makes ISO Core C++ an entirely new language). Also, Herb's claims about the ISO Core C++ lifetime profile requiring fewer annotations than Rust were incorrect. I gave basically this exact answer last time you posted a variant of this same comment on the last Rust thread."}],"r":[["YnWhqhNdYyk",2],["sWgDk-o-6ZE",1],["p6K4iIMlouI",1],["eX9m3g5J-XA",1],["LiWlvBro9eI",1],["EKWGGDXe5MA",1],["_ahvzDzKdB0",1],["dK3zRXhrFZY",1],["oKg1hTOQXoY",1],["Av0PQDVTP4A",1],["6wZmYMWKLkY",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1],["Eg_ToU7m1MI",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["p2LZLYcu_JY",1],["30jNsCVLpAE",1],["NH1Tta7purM",1],["xnqTKD8uD64",1],["DRq2NgeFcO0",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1]]},"nxyxcTZccsE":{"wy":"HN points to Lustig's talk when arguing that processed food changes metabolism and that a simple calories in/out model is poor.","q":{"x":"Watch this video below to understand more","i":41988232,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41988232,"author":"dp-hackernews","month":"2024-10","story":"Exercise Paradox","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why calories in versus calories out is a poor model","quote":"Watch this video below to understand more","text":"Calories are a measure of energy, NOT nutritional value. Forget calories, think utility. Some types of calories are good for you, others may as well be poison - therefore, the dose is the poison. By consuming calories in accordance with their utility you will do a better job at staying (or getting) metabolically healthy than only counting calories. TL;DR: Eat more fat and protein from natural, preferably animal, sources, and you will naturally be less inclined to consume junk calories from carbohydrates and you will quickly feel better for it. Watch this video below to understand more, for why this is the case, and why colories in v's calories out is a very poor model. --- \"Is a Calorie a Calorie? Processed Food, Experiment Gone Wrong\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":35309107,"author":"orangepurple","month":"2023-03","story":"The Efficient Frontier of Fat Loss","stance":"rec","why":"Lustig explains calories versus processed food","quote":"I would like to give you a succinct answer, but I am not as well spoken as Robert Lustig, MD","text":"I would like to give you a succinct answer, but I am not as well spoken as Robert Lustig, MD, a Professor of Pediatrics in the Division of Endocrinology at University of California, San Francisco, and the author of Fat Chance: Beating the Odds against Sugar, Processed Food, Obesity, and Disease. Is a Calorie a Calorie? Processed Food, Experiment Gone Wrong Presented by Stanford Health Care [VIDEO]"},{"id":28958330,"author":"dp-hackernews","month":"2021-10","story":"Obesity Pathogenesis: An Endocrine Society Scientific Statement","stance":"rec","why":"Lustig talk questioning calorie equivalence","quote":"Food for thought...","text":"Food for thought... Sugar: The Bitter Truth [LINK] Is a Calorie a Calorie? Processed Food, Experiment Gone Wrong [VIDEO] Does calorie counting work? (Almost never) [LINK] What The Fat (WTF) #133 Dr. Philip Ovadia| Keto \"Causes\" Heart Scarring [LINK] The art of misdirection | Apollo Robbins [LINK] Who's zoomin' who?"},{"id":11564542,"author":"buzzcut","month":"2016-04","story":"Diabetes: can you really eat to beat it?","stance":"rec","why":"Lustig on processed food and calories","quote":"If you want to learn more here are some sources","text":"Unfortunately I can only summarize. On the starvation study it's merely one example that highlights how calorie restriction affects people. Yes, it's an extreme version, but it is different only in degree from any other calorie restriction, and when people try to reduce by X calories, it's probably a linear effect; more restriction, more of these effects. Oddly though, fasting tends not to have these effects, it's only in sustained calorie restriction, so fasting in various forms is one of the tools people can use to lose weight. As for the lowered energy output Jason Fung wrote (pg. 53 of the Obesity Code, \"One major problem is that the basal metabolic rate does not stay stable. Decreased caloric intake can decrease basal metabolic rate by up to 40 percent. We shall see that increased caloric intake can increase it by 50%.\" If you want to learn more here are some sources: Jason Fung - The Obesity Code: [LINK] Robert Lustig on sugar: [LINK] and also here: [VIDEO] Peter Attia on ketosis and fat: [LINK] Reversal of diabetes by diet, also Jason Fung: [LINK]"}]},"DXvAlwMAxiA":{"wy":"HN commenters prefer it over other transistor explanations as an enlightening starting point that leads into building logic gates and a CPU.","q":{"x":"I found Ben Eater's videos on how semiconductors work and how transistors work interesting and enlightening.","i":33790592,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":38738536,"author":"1000100_1000101","month":"2023-12","story":"From Nand to Tetris (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Starting point for building up from transistors to gates and CPU.","quote":"Once you've got transistors, his videos walk you through making gates.","text":"Aside from his basic 8-bit CPU, Ben Eater goes into how transistors work too: [VIDEO] . Once you've got transistors, his videos walk you through making gates. Once you've got gates, he switches to the 74xx series and builds a CPU."},{"id":33790592,"author":"npilk","month":"2022-11","story":"How do transistors work, anyway?","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting, enlightening transistor explanation for non-engineers.","quote":"I found Ben Eater's videos on how semiconductors work and how transistors work interesting and enlightening.","text":"As someone who is not an electrical engineer, I found Ben Eater's videos on how semiconductors work and how transistors work interesting and enlightening. How semiconductors work: [LINK] How a transistor works: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32784066,"author":"ceronman","month":"2022-09","story":"How Transistors Work [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Preferred explanation of how transistors work.","quote":"I prefer the one by Ben Eater","text":"This is a good explanation, but I prefer the one by Ben Eater: How semiconductor works: [LINK] How a transistor works: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24202248,"author":"news_to_me","month":"2020-08","story":"Black silicon photodetector breaks the 100% efficiency limit","stance":"rec","why":"Helped the commenter understand how transistors work.","quote":"Knowing how transistors work helps a lot","text":"Great explanation — although only a week or so ago I wouldn't have understood a lot of it. Knowing how transistors work helps a lot: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["wSdV1M7n4gQ",1],["DJtef410XaM",1],["wIC-iGDTU40",1],["pgaEE27nsQw",1]]},"PJnJ8mK3Q3g":{"wy":"Commenters send it as a short, entertaining explanation of why people believe plastic is recyclable and how industry PR created that belief.","q":{"x":"This video is under 10mins and features a lot of the PR adverts.","i":36888144,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":46613161,"author":"mngnt","month":"2026-01","story":"There's a ridiculous amount of tech in a disposable vape","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why plastic recycling is a scam normalizing one-use plastic.","quote":"This youtube video explains why plastic recycling exists, how it's mostly ineffective and why is it a scam","text":"This youtube video explains why plastic recycling exists, how it's mostly ineffective and why is it a scam created to normalize one-use plastic. This basically applies to electronics and others. \"Why would I reuse or reduce, I can buy, consume an recycle\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":39405348,"author":"brikym","month":"2024-02","story":"Plastics producers deceived public about recycling, report reveals","stance":"rec","why":"How lobby groups invented the idea of recycling plastic.","quote":"If you have 10 minutes I recommend watching this video","text":"It makes a lot of sense. Corporations make more money the more junk they produce. If you have 10 minutes I recommend watching this video about how the lobby groups invented the whole idea of recycling plastic with the little numbered resin identification code that looks like a recycling symbol. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36875741,"author":"modo_mario","month":"2023-07","story":"Scientists Say Recycling Has Backfired Spectacularly","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of why many items can't actually be recycled.","quote":"This video gives a good overview about the reason this belief is so prevalent.","text":"A lot of things you'd think can be recycled actually can't be. This video gives a good overview about the reason this belief is so prevalent.: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36888144,"author":"andersrs","month":"2023-07","story":"Scientists Say Recycling Has Backfired Spectacularly","stance":"rec","why":"Short video showing PR adverts behind the recycling logo.","quote":"This video is under 10mins and features a lot of the PR adverts.","text":"> Why do we buy so much single-use packaging It's designed to be that way by the PR guys that created the plastic recycling logo. This video is under 10mins and features a lot of the PR adverts. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28472400,"author":"09bjb","month":"2021-09","story":"California aims to ban recycling symbols on things that aren’t recyclable","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how industry created the recycling symbol to confuse the public.","quote":"the plastics industry came up with that symbol in order to make it confusing to the layperson","text":"Would it surprise you to hear that the plastics industry came up with that symbol in order to make it confusing to the layperson to differentiate between recyclables and single-use plastics? \"Source\" (not a primary source): [VIDEO] <-- which has some links to further reading/viewing and a few sources."},{"id":42603750,"author":"TheRealPomax","month":"2025-01","story":"University of Alabama Engineer Pioneers New Process for Recycling Plastics","stance":"reference","why":"Supports the claim that plastic recycling is basically impossible at cost.","quote":"","text":"Okidokey: [LINK] and specifically [VIDEO] so there you go. Plastic recycling, because of what plastics are, is basically impossible at cost. PET recycling might make you think \"see, we can recycle plastics\" but first and foremost, PET recycling isn't recycling, it's reuse (using the PET as a base material to make something else, like fleece, which cannot be recycled), and second: the majority of plastics aren't PET and literally have no recycling path."},{"id":35180886,"author":"fredley","month":"2023-03","story":"Brazilian researchers find 'terrifying' plastic rocks on remote island","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Individual action alone cannot stem climate change and mass plastic pollution. All the cheapest food is packaged in single-use plastic. All the cheapest clothes and toys are made of plastic. One simply cannot opt out of this system without making massive, potentially ostrasizing changes to one's life. Many/most cannot afford fancy plastic-free goods. Most plastic pollution in the ocean is directly related to shipping and in particular fishing[0]. Nothing I can do individually can address this, change must come from World leaders and industry bodies working in good faith across international borders. They all know this is happening, they all know measures that could be taken to address it. Much of the framing of climate change and pollution as individual responsibility comes from large industry bodies themselves, who would much rather you feel guilty about it personally rather than wonder if they might be to blame.[1] [0]: [LINK] [1]: Plastic Recycling is an Actual Scam | Climate Town [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lG4VkPoG3ko",1],["t52LB2fYhoY",1]]},"YI3tsmFsrOg":{"wy":"HN commenters link it whenever phage therapy or antibiotic resistance comes up, as an accessible overview.","q":{"x":"Relevant Kurzgesagt video explaining _everything_ about bacteriophage[1].","i":17564716,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":34523950,"author":"bratwurst3000","month":"2023-01","story":"Antidepressants help bacteria resist antibiotics","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting introduction to phages and their therapeutic use.","quote":"And this video is rly interesting","text":"Thank you very much. I google a bit and here in germany we are allready building a database and a stock of phagea for specific bacteria. They allready have over 1000 phages. They also have „phage cocktails“ for broadband. They also state your points. Thanks again. Here the link in german [LINK] And this video is rly interesting [VIDEO]"},{"id":31371651,"author":"burnished","month":"2022-05","story":"Nanobots Can Swim Around a Wound and Kill Bacteria","stance":"rec","why":"Covers tension between antibiotic resistance and phage defenses, suggesting alternating treatments.","quote":"The latter half of a charming video by Kurzgesagt[0] talks about the tension between anti-bacterial resistance and bacteriophage defenses","text":"The latter half of a charming video by Kurzgesagt[0] talks about the tension between anti-bacterial resistance and bacteriophage defenses - we might be able to see-saw treatments based on what local microbes have adapted to. I only mention it as a tangent, and because your comment makes me think you'd be interested. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17564716,"author":"amingilani","month":"2018-07","story":"Using a virus to kill what antibiotics can’t","stance":"rec","why":"Explains bacteriophages comprehensively in an accessible animated format.","quote":"Relevant Kurzgesagt video explaining _everything_ about bacteriophage[1].","text":"Relevant Kurzgesagt video explaining _everything_ about bacteriophage[1]. I posted this a while ago, but since it's a video it didn't get much attention. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17086159,"author":"emilga","month":"2018-05","story":"Phages are making a comeback","stance":"rec","why":"Kurzgesagt overview of bacteriophages","quote":"Super cool, anyways!","text":"Kurzgesagt goes through bacterophages in this video: [VIDEO] I think this is the case mentioned by Kurzgesagt in the video, although he doesn't use names, so I can't be sure. Super cool, anyways!"},{"id":48102929,"author":"Intralexical","month":"2026-05","story":"Venom and Hot Peppers Offer a Key to Killing Resistant Bacteria","stance":"reference","why":"Mentions phage resistance trading off against antibiotic resistance.","quote":"developing phage resistance reduces antibiotic resistance, and vice versa","text":"I have no idea if this is true, but I remember seeing in a Kurzgesagt video that developing phage resistance reduces antibiotic resistance, and vice versa. So you might corner bacteria by using both. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26876827,"author":"Valgrim","month":"2021-04","story":"mRNA vaccines – a new era in vaccinology (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates how phages infect and burst bacteria, the basis of phage therapy.","quote":"Basically use viruses against bacteria.","text":"The whole concept of phage therapy is somehow based on this: Bacteriophages inject DNA into specific bacteria that makes them copy the phage inside them until they burst open. Basically use viruses against bacteria. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26644566,"author":"p1mrx","month":"2021-03","story":"Why Computers Won't Make Themselves Smarter","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how phages could serve as targeted antibiotics.","quote":"Bacteriophages (viruses that infect bacteria) are particularly interesting, because we might be able to use them as targeted antibiotics","text":"Bacteriophages (viruses that infect bacteria) are particularly interesting, because we might be able to use them as targeted antibiotics: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17337290,"author":"majewsky","month":"2018-06","story":"The Bugs Are Winning","stance":"reference","why":"Phages as possible treatment for superbugs","quote":"may be able to deploy bacteriophages (bacteria-attacking viruses) to combat superbugs","text":"Counterpoint: A recent Kurzgesagt video suggests that we may be able to deploy bacteriophages (bacteria-attacking viruses) to combat superbugs. [VIDEO] Note: I'm not a doctor/biologist/etc., so I cannot comment on the details of this claim (e.g. how far away we are from a widely deployed phage-based treatment)."}],"r":[["sBzRwzY7G-k",1],["hMOwbNUpDQA",1]]},"lG2dXobAXLI":{"wy":"Recommended as an amusing but thoughtful take on review inflation and game journalism, in threads about reviews and ratings.","q":{"x":"Dunkey presented a fairly well-argued idea why major publications tend to give 9+/10 reviews to games","i":20586892,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":32265051,"author":"seanhunter","month":"2022-07","story":"The man who made the “worst” video game in history (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Amusing yet thoughtful take on the problem with game journalism","quote":"An amusing and yet thoughtful take on the problem comes from Videogamedunkey","text":"There really are a lot of terrible game journalists. The ones who aren't terrible tend to be independents rather than writing for big publications. An amusing and yet thoughtful take on the problem comes from Videogamedunkey [VIDEO] and [LINK]"},{"id":28202453,"author":"MaXtreeM","month":"2021-08","story":"Video Game Pricing [video]","stance":"rec","why":"One of dunkey's good video essays, with a part two","quote":"From time to time he makes a video-essay which are usually great, I would recommend a \"Game Critics\"[0] and part two","text":"I never thought I would see videogamedunkey in top hot posts on HN but here we go. As others have already pointed out, he usually makes silly videos but has been consistent for many years if you like his kind of humour. From time to time he makes a video-essay which are usually great, I would recommend a \"Game Critics\"[0] and part two if you liked the first one. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20586892,"author":"fyrabanks","month":"2019-08","story":"Devilution – A web port of the original Diablo game","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why major outlets give inflated review scores","quote":"Dunkey presented a fairly well-argued idea why major publications tend to give 9+/10 reviews to games","text":"Dunkey presented a fairly well-argued idea why major publications tend to give 9+/10 reviews to games, even when the actual text of their review clearly points to major issues ( [VIDEO] ). The tl;dw version is that gamers, by and large, tend to disparage media outlets for giving poor ratings to games that aren't already widely disliked by the hive mind. It comes from the perspective of someone who does unbiased VG reviews, and I think it raises an interesting point that might otherwise be overlooked. (Of course, payola should certainly not be discounted as a factor.)"},{"id":17201255,"author":"ljm","month":"2018-05","story":"The Never-Ending War on Fake Reviews","stance":"rec","why":"Argues critic taste and integrity matter as much as the rating","quote":"his video about game critics[0], where he goes into some detail about the preferences and integrity of the critic","text":"I actually wonder why crowd-sourced reviews have become so ubiquitous, to the point of almost being necessary (along with five-star ratings). Has anyone legitimately attempted to challenge the status quo here? I'm reminded of videogamedunkey on YouTube and his video about game critics[0], where he goes into some detail about the preferences and integrity of the critic being just as important (if not more) than the review and rating. Professional critics build a strong reputation around their taste and preference and they gain notoriety from doggedly sticking to those principles. So you'd likely trust the opinion of Roger Ebert or Mark Kermode if you share their taste in cinema, and even watch something you normally wouldn't if even they recommended it. You're very likely to subscribe to a publication whose critics align with your own tastes because they're effectively curating content for you in the form of recommendations. None of that applies when you have reviews from a succession of total strangers - you're not going to research dozens or hundreds of commentators to establish a logical consistency in their point of view and decide whether or not their tastes align with yours. More often than not the reviews are low quality and low value, spread across a five-point scale but essentially treated as a binary like/didn't like system. At that point, crowd-sourced reviews tell you nothing you didn't know already: some people enjoyed it for one reason and others didn't for a dif"},{"id":42497291,"author":"operator-name","month":"2024-12","story":"Critics, not fans, perpetuate the failed second album myth, study shows","stance":"reference","why":"Critic whose taste aligns or misaligns with yours is useful","quote":"","text":"You don't, but it can be helpful if you find a critic that strongly aligns with your preferences, or even strongly misaligns with your preferences. Just like a reccomendation from a friend, you can use it to consider if something is worth even giving a try. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["kya_LXa_y1E",1],["fNk_zzaMoSs",1],["NPOHf20slZg",1]]},"v_UyVmITiYQ":{"wy":"HN returns to it as the original Wave pitch, both for its wow factor and as a case study in a hyped product that was later shut down.","q":{"x":"their demo exhibits a wow factor that is undeniable","i":671236,"y":"2009"},"m":[{"id":45297055,"author":"rkagerer","month":"2025-09","story":"Meta’s live demo fails; “AI” recording plays before the actor takes the steps","stance":"rec","why":"Empathetic example of ad-libbing over demo hiccups","quote":"Here's one of my favorites, of Lars doing the Wave dance on stage to ad-lib over connectivity hiccups.","text":"Here's one of my favorites, of Lars doing the Wave dance on stage to ad-lib over connectivity hiccups. For some reason it evoked a lot more empathy from me... [VIDEO]"},{"id":5429760,"author":"Samuel_Michon","month":"2013-03","story":"Finale for Now on Google's Self-Inflicted Trust Problem","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation excited commenter about Wave before it was killed","quote":"The presentation[1] got me very excited and I couldn't wait to start using it.","text":"For me it was Google Wave. The presentation[1] got me very excited and I couldn't wait to start using it. I signed up for the beta and told everyone I work with to do so as well. It quickly became an invaluable tool for me, I couldn't imagine what I'd do without it. And then, a year after it was introduced, only 3 months after it was released to the general public, Google announced Wave was dead. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":671236,"author":"bmelton","month":"2009-06","story":"Review my startup: Revizr.  You haven't seen this before.","stance":"rec","why":"Long demo with undeniable wow factor; skip around","quote":"their demo exhibits a wow factor that is undeniable","text":"I'm surprised nobody has compared this to google's wave yet. If you haven't, you should go check out the demo -- I'm not for sure yet how much feature overlap there is, but their demo exhibits a wow factor that is undeniable. It's long, but skip around and you're sure to hit on at least some of the important bits: [VIDEO]"},{"id":631481,"author":"luigi","month":"2009-05","story":"Five Reasons to Be Terrified of Google Wave ","stance":"rec","why":"Watch the keynote to judge Wave yourself","quote":"you should draw your own conclusions by watching the keynote","text":"That's probably the most blatant example of FUD in internet history. Jumping to conclusions while remaining blissfully ignorant to the actual implementation. Until we get access to the sandbox, you should draw your own conclusions by watching the keynote: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44313038,"author":"rkagerer","month":"2025-06","story":"Google aims to reinvent email with Wave (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Lars doing the Wave dance over connectivity hiccups","quote":"I have fond memories of Lars doing the Wave dance on stage to ad-lib over the connectivity hiccups","text":"I was in the audience when they previewed it for the first time at I/O. The Internet at that conference was awfully intermittent (ironic considering this was Google), and I have fond memories of Lars doing the Wave dance on stage to ad-lib over the connectivity hiccups: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20773595,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2019-08","story":"A Pissed-Off Tutorial for Google Wave (2010) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Original announcement seen as pretentious in retrospect","quote":"the original world-shattering announcement and demo of Wave at Google IO","text":"I've made a timeline with the video of the original world-shattering announcement and demo of Wave at Google IO, and some of the Google Wave related submissions to HN. How pretentious and audacious it seems in retrospect! It’s hard to appreciate all the snarky jokes in the Pissed Off Wave Tutorial, if you don’t remember how, um, over-confident they were when they announced Wave. Google Wave Developer Preview at Google I/O 2009 (May 28, 2009) [VIDEO] Google Wave Drips With Ambition. A New Communication Platform For A New Web. (May 28, 2009) [LINK] [LINK] Google Wave: What Might Email Look Like If It Were Invented Today (May 29, 2009) [LINK] [LINK] What problems does Google Wave solve? (Oct 15, 2009) [LINK] [LINK] Google Kills Wave (Aug 4, 2010) [LINK] [LINK] What makes Slack so successful whereas Google Wave wasn't? (Oct 10, 2015) [LINK] What in the Hell Was Google Wave Trying to Be Anyway? – Gizmodo UK (May 27, 2019) [LINK] On a lighter note, the Game Helpin’ Squad (who made the Wave tutorial) also has several spot-on parodies of various video game genres! “Time Travel Understander” is a parody of Braid: [LINK] “Severe Running” parodies those Flash running games: [LINK] And “World Quester 2” is the ultimate D&D RPG parody: [LINK] \"Pretend Gas Station\" is a SimCity parody: [LINK] “Solitare Party” parodies all those card games: [LINK]"},{"id":16443572,"author":"sitkack","month":"2018-02","story":"Retiring Wave","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as background on Wave","quote":"Wave is one of those things that everyone raves about but has never used.","text":"git clone [LINK] git clone [LINK] git clone [LINK] Does it even build? Wave is one of those things that everyone raves about but has never used. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7534066,"author":"maddisc2","month":"2014-04","story":"Apache Wave","stance":"reference","why":"Recalls watching the developer preview","quote":"I remember watching the Google Wave Developer Preview at Google I/O in 2009!","text":"I remember watching the Google Wave Developer Preview at Google I/O in 2009! [VIDEO] I agree that the invite only really slowed it down."},{"id":5760760,"author":"greggman","month":"2013-05","story":"Google Abandons Open Standards for Instant Messaging","stance":"reference","why":"Intro presentation suggests no new protocol was needed","quote":"Watching the Wave introduction presentation from Google I/O 2009 it's hard to buy that a new protocol was needed.","text":"Wave was built on XMPP wasn't it? If I understand correctly XMPP is fully extensible which means Google can add whatever data they want and still be inter-operable with all the common parts and then define the new parts. Video seems like it could be out of band using WebRTC and initiated by new XMPP data. Watching the Wave introduction presentation from Google I/O 2009 it's hard to buy that a new protocol was needed. [VIDEO]"},{"id":1520762,"author":"urza","month":"2010-07","story":"Intro to Google's open source JavaScript rich text / HTML editor","stance":"reference","why":"Cites spell-checker popup in Wave demo","quote":"something like the spell-checker in this google wave demonstration","text":"Hello, I have a question. Does anybody know about javascript rich text / HTML editor, that could easily show small popup under carret (or certain word) while the user is typing? I mean something like the spell-checker in this google wave demonstration - see at 0:44:40 [VIDEO] Or lets say, you want to implement something like this [LINK] but in web browser. How would you go about it?"}],"r":[["1WupH8oyrAo",1],["Mz3JeYfBTcY",1],["Erp8IAUouus",1],["rNu8XDBSn10",1],["6bqBsHSwPgw",1],["E9KJ_f7REGw",1]]},"NngCHTImH1g":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a clear, short tour of Terraform's synthetic fuel approach and goals.","q":{"x":"Excellent S3 piece on Terraform [20min]","i":40684621,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40684621,"author":"consumer451","month":"2024-06","story":"What is the opposite of oil drilling?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent short piece on Terraform's synthetic fuel approach.","quote":"Excellent S3 piece on Terraform [20min]","text":"Related, and yet not mentioned in TFA: \"Terraform makes carbon neutral natural gas\" [LINK] Excellent S3 piece on Terraform [20min]: [VIDEO] I agree with Terraform's founder on at least one thing: atmospheric carbon capture needs to be cheaper than ground extraction. Market forces will decide the rest. This is not a CO2 cop-out, we need to both stop introducing CO2 into the atmosphere, and also extract gigatons from it. Terraform Industries is addressing both sides."},{"id":41285429,"author":"aesch","month":"2024-08","story":"Energy economics and rocket science with Casey Handmer","stance":"rec","why":"Tour of Terraform and its goals from the founder.","quote":"Here is a good video where he gives a tour of the company and talks about its goals","text":"Casey Handmer's company is creating synthetic hydrocarbons from renewable energy. Here is a good video where he gives a tour of the company and talks about its goals: [VIDEO] . Curious what people think about the idea of synthetic hydrocarbons? It is a seemingly obvious idea that I hadn't heard about until recently, as long as you can use energy efficiently to create the synthetic hydrocarbons."},{"id":39923611,"author":"mattjaynes","month":"2024-04","story":"Terraform makes carbon neutral natural gas","stance":"rec","why":"Overview and tour of Terraform.","quote":"I'd recommend Jason Carman's recently released videos on Terraform","text":"For more, I'd recommend Jason Carman's recently released videos on Terraform: Overview and tour (~20min): [VIDEO] Deeper Interview (~40min): [LINK]"},{"id":39935257,"author":"bryanlarsen","month":"2024-04","story":"Terraform makes carbon neutral natural gas","stance":"reference","why":"States Terraform's cost goals for carbon capture and hydrogen.","quote":"he says the goal is $100/tonne carbon capture and $1/kg H2","text":"On [VIDEO] he says the goal is $100/tonne carbon capture and $1/kg H2, which puts those inputs well under the $10/kcf leaving some margin for the cost of the sabatier. Both processes are directly proportional to the cost of electricity and heat so assuming the thesis of decreasing solar costs holds true, it's a question of when not if."}],"r":[["MRqh8oLY7Ik",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["GZ5XN_mJE8Y",1],["gDfNRWsMRsU",1],["lqzJewjZUkk",1]]},"MHTizZ_XcUM":{"wy":"HN calls it a great, impressive engineering project involving real-time trajectory calculation.","q":{"x":"Another great video is his automatic bulls eye dartboard [2].","i":15625627,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":18708237,"author":"anonu","month":"2018-12","story":"Glitter bomb tricks parcel thieves","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Dartboard project calculating projectile trajectory in realtime.","quote":"even more impressive than this glitter machine IMHO","text":"This is an an awesome project... the kinda stuff I wish I had more time to hack away at... In the video he mentions his automatic bullseye dartboard project... even more impressive than this glitter machine IMHO: [VIDEO] Calculating a projectile's trajectory in realtime.... wouldn't even know where to start. EDIT: dartboard code from that youtube link: [LINK]"},{"id":15625627,"author":"jonathanfoster","month":"2017-11","story":"Carnival scam science, and how to win [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mark Rober's automatic bullseye dartboard.","quote":"Another great video is his automatic bulls eye dartboard [2].","text":"The actual video is by Mark Rober and man you gotta check out his YouTube channel [1]. I've been in a technical slump here recently and watching his videos totally re-energized me. He reminded me how great it feels to explore, build, and solve problems. Another great video is his automatic bulls eye dartboard [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22903767,"author":"JshWright","month":"2020-04","story":"Show HN: A basketball hoop to maximize shots that go in [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Dartboard with active compensation, related to the backboard.","quote":"a dart board that does the same this as this backboard, but with active compensation","text":"Mark Rober (who is linked elsewhere in this thread for a dart board that does the same this as this backboard, but with active compensation) also did a video on how carnival games are rigged. Dart Board: [VIDEO] Rigged Carnival Games: [LINK] (Bonus, in the theme of sports equipment that wins for you) Auto bowling ball: [LINK]"}],"r":[["TQB0KK2rxcw",1]]},"QMiubC6LdTA":{"wy":"Recommended as an approachable, detailed look at console hardware architecture, FreeBSD in the PS4 and console hacking.","q":{"x":"This is a good watch, it goes into detail on the architecture of the PS4","i":40133607,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40133607,"author":"s1gsegv","month":"2024-04","story":"How to reenable undervolting in 2020 Dell Laptops by editing EFI variables","stance":"rec","why":"Details PS4 architecture and how it differs from a standard x86_64 PC.","quote":"This is a good watch, it goes into detail on the architecture of the PS4","text":"This is a good watch, it goes into detail on the architecture of the PS4 showing how it differs from a standard x86_64 “PC.” [VIDEO] The “too long; didn’t watch” summary is that there’s several peripherals, timers, RTC, etc outside of the processor that software will expect to be present (or at least emulated) with specific interfaces. You can implement x86_64 without a lot of this, or redone in a more modern style, but software such as a Linux kernel will have to be patched for it. Typically a modern laptop will have all the legacy peripherals emulated, and the modern peripherals will live alongside them."},{"id":29160546,"author":"lathiat","month":"2021-11","story":"Hacking group says it has found encryption keys needed to unlock the PS5","stance":"rec","why":"Approachable CCC talk on how PS4 was hacked.","quote":"there are some great CCC talks very approachable and understandable about how it was done on other consoles historically.","text":"The key is on the console (not server side). Historically either they made some silly choices or there was a hardware bug or glitch that let you read out the key that the processor has to be able to access to decrypt the firmware. Search YouTube for fail0verflow and there are some great CCC talks very approachable and understandable about how it was done on other consoles historically. PS4: [VIDEO] Wii U: [LINK]"},{"id":13801638,"author":"izacus","month":"2017-03","story":"What the death of the CRT display technology means for classic arcade machines","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how PS4 differs from a PC and issues running Linux.","quote":"This 33c3 talk gives a lot of fascinating information on how PS4 differs from your run-of-the-mill PC","text":"While the CPU is indeed x86-64, the architecture of the SoC and the device is rather different so emulation still won't be all that easy. This 33c3 talk gives a lot of fascinating information on how PS4 differs from your run-of-the-mill PC and what kind of issues they hit when trying to run Linux on it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13900779,"author":"jvdh","month":"2017-03","story":"The BSD family tree","stance":"rec","why":"Covers PS4 hardware, running Linux, and FreeBSD in the PS4.","quote":"Here's a nice talk at CCC last year which explains some bits of FreeBSD in the PS4.","text":"`It's interesting to note that the PS4 OS is also a forked FreeBSD variant as well.` Here's a nice talk at CCC last year which explains some bits of FreeBSD in the PS4. It's mostly about hardware and how to run Linux on it, but FreeBSD gets a nice mention there too. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28731814,"author":"drekipus","month":"2021-10","story":"Google developing own CPUs for Chromebook laptops","stance":"reference","why":"PS4 talk with concept similar to PS3 approach.","quote":"This talk is about the PS4, similar in concept","text":"If I remember correctly that's similar what the PS3 did, but that's a different issue and comes with cool discussions This talk is about the PS4, similar in concept [VIDEO]"},{"id":25557647,"author":"marcan_42","month":"2020-12","story":"Raspberry Pi PCIe Devices","stance":"reference","why":"Speaker's own PS4 talk including the PCIe over serial story.","quote":"PS4 talk including the PCIe story","text":"Ah, but have you ever run PCIe over 115200 baud serial? :) We did that for the PS4 hardware experiments, and that also involved a PCIe hotplug capable motherboard, as we had a PS4 southbridge patched in as a PCIe device on a normal PC, and every time we powered on the PS4 that was effectively hotplugging it. Fun times. These days PCIe hotplug is a consumer technology, it's called Thunderbolt (it's hotplug at the logical layer, not physical PCIe lanes, but to the OS/software it's about the same). PS4 talk including the PCIe story: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22431955,"author":"raverbashing","month":"2020-02","story":"Cursed Adapters","stance":"reference","why":"Shows PCI-E tunneled over RS232 in PS4 reverse engineering.","quote":"they they tunneled PCI-E over RS232 (around 8m15s)","text":"Reminds me of some PS4 or Xbox (I think) reverse engineering video where they they tunneled PCI-E over RS232 (around 8m15s) [VIDEO]"},{"id":19260871,"author":"wmf","month":"2019-02","story":"Struck by a Thunderbolt","stance":"reference","why":"Shows PCIe tunneled over RS-232.","quote":"Check out PCIe over RS-232","text":"PCIe is also packet-based BTW [1]. The benefit from the custom protocol is being able to run PCIe and DisplayPort (and I guess networking which no one cares about) over the same cable at the same time with dynamic bandwidth allocation. USB-C has alternate modes but they're not as powerful. In theory PCIe alternate mode over USB-C should be possible and it would be much simpler than Thunderbolt but nobody has tried to build it. I imagine the spec doesn't really exist because Intel has been too lazy to properly write it (I wouldn't be surprised if Thunderbolt is basically defined as whatever the original Ridge controller implemented) and it's probably also gross. Adoption seems to be limited by cost, not by secrecy (thinking back to Firewire, it was open but there were very few controller chips for it). [1] Check out PCIe over RS-232: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13939887,"author":"Asooka","month":"2017-03","story":"Ask HN: Why is BSD becoming more popular in embedded devices?","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates WebKit and BSD exploits used to run Linux on a PS4.","quote":"","text":"> since using a vanilla kernel would make a public exploit in say, FreeBSD, easily ported to their device You mean like at the recent CCC where they demonstrated using a WebKit exploit to trigger a BSD exploit via which they install a working Linux system on a PS4 and launch Steam? Link: [VIDEO] It's not as simple as that because the PS4 is a custom board, so even though the CPU and GPU are standard AMD components, some hacking was required. But using exploits that have long been patched to gain access to hardware by Sony and Nintendo is nothing new."}],"r":[["yT9bit2-1pg",1],["oHf1vD5_b5I",1],["NSuaUok-wTY",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["noQcWra6sbU",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["uYbdx4I7STg",1],["ra_9zU-mnl8",1],["4YYvBLAF4T8",1],["a9b9IYqsb_U",1],["rNu8XDBSn10",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["NmGaXEmfTIo",1],["ZUvGfuLlZus",1],["DOWDNBu9DkU",1]]},"XxCha4Kez9c":{"wy":"Recommended in address and OCR threads as a look at the last human mail-coding operation.","q":{"x":"This Tom Scott video shows some of the surprising (to me) lengths to which the USPS does go to figure out the right address","i":34999084,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":40842797,"author":"Caligatio","month":"2024-07","story":"Postzegelcode","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting Tom Scott video on the same topic.","quote":"here's a Ton Scott video about the same topic","text":"And like almost all things that are interesting, here's a Ton Scott video about the same topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39911947,"author":"LordDragonfang","month":"2024-04","story":"Amazon ditches 'just walk out' checkouts at its grocery stores","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the last remaining manual mail coding operation with stenography-like keyboards.","quote":"Tom Scott did a great video showing the last one in operation.","text":"Tom Scott did a great video showing the last one in operation. They use totally unique keyboards with what is essentially a stenography system: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34997488,"author":"bombcar","month":"2023-03","story":"What Is the Most Minimal UK Address Possible?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what machines read and the accuracy required.","quote":"might be of interest - it has some of what the machines are trying to read and what accuracy they need.","text":"[VIDEO] might be of interest - it has some of what the machines are trying to read and what accuracy they need."},{"id":34999084,"author":"simmonmt","month":"2023-03","story":"What Is the Most Minimal UK Address Possible?","stance":"rec","why":"Surprising lengths USPS goes to decode bad handwriting addresses.","quote":"This Tom Scott video shows some of the surprising (to me) lengths to which the USPS does go to figure out the right address","text":"This Tom Scott video shows some of the surprising (to me) lengths to which the USPS does go to figure out the right address: [VIDEO] Obviously this doesn't apply to your case, but I certainly didn't think they'd have a national center for decoding terrible handwriting."},{"id":47682608,"author":"shagie","month":"2026-04","story":"AI helps add 10k more photos to OldNYC","stance":"reference","why":"OCR handling hand-lettered addresses at the US Postal Service.","quote":"","text":"As a profession (and under time constraints) ... Tom Scott : How the US Postal Service reads terrible handwriting - [VIDEO] Part of the story is that the OCR that is handling hand lettered addresses. I also chuckled at the cursive letter recognition sheet on the side of the cube."},{"id":47050413,"author":"yencabulator","month":"2026-02","story":"Using an Engineering Notebook","stance":"reference","why":"USPS handwriting recognition uses restricted dictionary and human fixers.","quote":"","text":"> Though USPS has been doing handwriting recognition since the 80's. On a very restricted dictionary of possible content, with a pool of people employed to fix anything where the computer is unsure. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34997314,"author":"bombcar","month":"2023-03","story":"What Is the Most Minimal UK Address Possible?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the postal branch that handles undeliverable letters.","quote":"There's a specific branch of the post office that works on these - the dead letter office","text":"One of Don Rosa's books has the letters he sent to a friend in college, each with a drawing on the envelope and less and less address, until finally it was all drawing and barely part of a name - they all got (eventually) delivered. There's a specific branch of the post office that works on these - the dead letter office: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33910849,"author":"omginternets","month":"2022-12","story":"Apple Kills Plans to Scan for CSAM in iCloud","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Actually, we're talking about both, and you are wrong on both counts. If you weren't, you would be able to point to an existing system that has demonstrated the capability of classifying an image set on the order of iCloud's, without producing a false-positive. You can't, because such a system doesn't exist. Even the \"solved\" problem of OCR isn't capable of such a feat: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["S2xHZPH5Sng",1],["xMabpBvtXr4",1],["GDzBE6vz5r0",1],["2guvwQvElA8",1]]},"_KRb_qV9P4g":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it in motion-smoothing and interpolation threads as an overview of why interpolation hurts animation.","q":{"x":"A really good video on the subject","i":40908951,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40908951,"author":"vgbm","month":"2024-07","story":"Roku update turns on hated motion-smoothing feature, with no off switch","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how interpolation harms animation timing.","quote":"A really good video on the subject","text":"A really good video on the subject: [VIDEO] The gist is that, for animation, frame interpolation messes with intended timing and can produce incoherent images on interpolated frames and odd frame rate issues for certain kinds of animations. Interpolation can thus cause animations to lose their punch and feel wrong. While interpolation may be nice for live action films, it should still be an option to turn off."},{"id":40909038,"author":"kec","month":"2024-07","story":"Roku update turns on hated motion-smoothing feature, with no off switch","stance":"rec","why":"Profane but good overview of why interpolation isn't inherently better.","quote":"give a pretty good overview of why interpolation does not inherently make things look better","text":"This video is laced with profanity and focused specifically on animation, but give a pretty good overview of why interpolation does not inherently make things look better: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40396495,"author":"zbirkenbuel","month":"2024-05","story":"Sakuga-42M Dataset: Scaling Up Cartoon Research","stance":"rec","why":"Essential viewing on inbetween frames and interpolation.","quote":"Can't have a conversation about inbetween frames without sharing this[1] Noodle video.","text":"Can't have a conversation about inbetween frames without sharing this[1] Noodle video. Now granted the interpolation there is pretty rigid, and the goal of this dataset will be to train an LLM replicate hand tweening, but I can't help but feel there's going to be a massive uncanny valley. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38521897,"author":"nyx","month":"2023-12","story":"Switch off bad TV settings","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of why interpolation hurts animation.","quote":"a pretty good overview of why frame interpolation sucks","text":"Counterpoint: this YouTube rant by an animation person called Noodle is a pretty good overview of why frame interpolation sucks. [VIDEO] Basically, low FPS can be a stylistic choice, and making up new frames at playback time often completely butchers some of the nuances present in good animation."},{"id":36088395,"author":"internetter","month":"2023-05","story":"There oughta be a bullet time video booth","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of complaints about AI framerate increase.","quote":"","text":"I'm usually a big critic of AI, and I remember seeing people complaining years ago about increasing framerates with AI[0], but maybe this could be a good usecase. You do, say, half the cameras, and then some crafty solution to interpolate (filling in the edges with AI, some sort of angle adjust) [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34063001,"author":"bcraven","month":"2022-12","story":"FILM: Frame Interpolation for Large Motion","stance":"reference","why":"Animator explains why he dislikes interpolation.","quote":"explains why he despises the idea of interpolated video","text":"The animator Noodle recently put this video on YouTube that explains why he despises the idea of interpolated video: [VIDEO]"}]},"lIFE7h3m40U":{"wy":"HN links it as a charming defense of bodging and of AutoHotkey, and as the go-to example when custom keyboards or hotkey hacks come up.","q":{"x":"I saw a good talk on it from Tom Scott some time ago[0] which I think is relevant.","i":24150252,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":32954906,"author":"lcnPylGDnU4H9OF","month":"2022-09","story":"Software I’m thankful for (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"A compelling argument for Windows.","quote":"Easily the most compelling argument I've heard for Windows is Tom Scott's","text":"I can't disagree, even if I would prefer something else. Easily the most compelling argument I've heard for Windows is Tom Scott's: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":24150252,"author":"dijit","month":"2020-08","story":"Jira Is a Microcosm of What’s Broken in Software Development","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk on why bodges keep the world running.","quote":"I saw a good talk on it from Tom Scott some time ago[0] which I think is relevant.","text":"Never underestimate the art of the bodge, it's quite literally keeping the world ticking over, perfection has a place but it is rare and fleeting. I saw a good talk on it from Tom Scott some time ago[0] which I think is relevant. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23158049,"author":"modernerd","month":"2020-05","story":"In Praise of AutoHotKey","stance":"rec","why":"Ode to AutoHotkey as a bodge that helps make other bodges.","quote":"a bodge that helps you make other bodges","text":"AutoHotKey (in addition to WSL and VS Code) was enough to make me Windows-curious and eventually try a Windows laptop again after 10+ years on Mac. I first found out about AHK via Tom Scott's similar ode here: [VIDEO] He describes it as “a bodge that helps you make other bodges”, which is beautifully put. [For those outside the UK, a “bodge” is a clumsy hack or repair.]"},{"id":23160681,"author":"jedimastert","month":"2020-05","story":"In Praise of AutoHotKey","stance":"rec","why":"Details of the emoji keyboard with thousands of hand-placed stickers.","quote":"He goes into more detail here[1], but it's beautiful.","text":"Fun AutoHotKey abuse: Tom Scott's emoji keyboard[0]. It's every single emoji (at the time of recording) on 14 full keyboards with 1,000s of hand-placed bespoke stickers. He goes into more detail here[1], but it's beautiful. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41183567,"author":"avidseeker","month":"2024-08","story":"Emotional about X11: I'm creating a pure X11 “emoji keyboard”","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of Tom Scott's \"Art of Bodge up\" video where he attempts to make an emoji keyboard on Windows.","text":"Reminds me of Tom Scott's \"Art of Bodge up\" video where he attempts to make an emoji keyboard on Windows. [VIDEO] It's funny at the end he says \"and don't ever suggest me I use Linux, just don't.\" I felt he expected it to be really hard project on Linux, while iterally his project is a single keyboard layout file."},{"id":27015598,"author":"jedimastert","month":"2021-05","story":"Windows Terminal Quake Mode","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott uses AHK and Lua for an emoji keyboard.","quote":"Here's Tom Scott talking about how he used AHK and Lua to make a keyboard for every emoji","text":"Auto Hotkey is surprisingly powerful Here's Tom Scott talking about how he used AHK and Lua to make a keyboard for every emoji [VIDEO]"},{"id":18282942,"author":"modernerd","month":"2018-10","story":"How IBM’s ThinkPad Became a Design Icon (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott praising Windows and AHK.","quote":"See Tom Scott waxing lyrical about Windows and AHK here","text":"Yes, there is the Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL), which is a compatibility layer for running Linux binaries natively on Windows: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] I haven't switched to Windows 10 full-time yet, but WSL works remarkably well whenever I've tried it. Cmder ( [LINK] ) and ConEmu ( [LINK] ) are pretty decent terminal emulators and Terminus ( [LINK] ) looks promising too. (It's hard to beat iTerm, though.) My other concern with Windows 10 was security and viruses. Last time I used Windows every day (~1999!) that was a huge concern, but it seems to be less of one now. Most seem to consider using the standard Windows-supplied virus checker just fine if you're not torrenting dodgy stuff and opening strange email attachments. The following guide (from the @SwiftOnSecurity twitter author) was helpful: [LINK] It contains much the same advice for Windows that you'd expect to see for keeping macOS secure too — keep stuff up-to-date, encrypt your drives, don't try to disable User Account Control or Device Guard (like Gatekeeper on Mac). I've found that chocolatey ( [LINK] ) is a great replacement for brew, Laragon ( [LINK] ) is a good alternative to Valet, and Autohotkey ( [LINK] ) is unrivaled for general system automation. (See Tom Scott waxing lyrical about Windows and AHK here: [VIDEO] )."},{"id":17761902,"author":"Kagerjay","month":"2018-08","story":"Setting up a second keyboard for more shortcuts in Emacs","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott builds a custom keyboard with AutoHotkey and Lua.","quote":"There's a tomscott video on it","text":"With lua + autohotkey scripts in windows this is definitely possible the last time I checked. You run a luascript on another keyboard mapped to a generic key, luascript inputs that as the proper text-based language There's a tomscott video on it [VIDEO] Checkout Teran van Hemert (linustechtips editor) for some potential ideas too. He's got crazy macro schemas, I've used a couple of his and there great. All windows based as well though. [LINK] Mac, I can only assume its something you would write in applescript. But maybe you find some interesting ideas there. I found something called \" [LINK] that might be of interest to you"}],"r":[["re96UgMk6GQ",1],["UF8uR6Z6KLc",1],["AJdEqssNZ-U",1],["72y2EC5fkcE",1],["0ifChJ0nJfM",1],["sPhpelUfu8Q",1],["PHe0bXAIuk0",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["LdOe18KhtT4",1],["fD2briZ6fB0",1],["8YUWDrLazCg",1],["qLjgXPDzeZo",1],["m7PVZixO35c",1],["Jzfpyo-q-RM",1],["h4Uu5eyN6VU",1],["8PmM6SUn7Es",1],["3AtBE9BOvvk",1],["Oo0jm1PPRuo",1],["GsyhGHUEt-k",1],["XGo4PJd1lng",1]]},"tiKCORN-6m8":{"wy":"Pointed to in tuning and temperament threads as an audible demonstration of how meantone differs from equal temperament.","q":{"x":"Brandon Acker gives a wonderful overview on the classical guitar","i":46888861,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47497178,"author":"magicalhippo","month":"2026-03","story":"Why your guitar goes sharp when you play hard: the Kirchoff–Carrier equation","stance":"rec","why":"More informative and historical take on microtonal music","quote":"(for a more informative and historical video)","text":"I'd just say I'm playing microtonal music[1][2] and not worry about it. Jokes aside, great read. Always so satisfying when the predictions match experiment. [1]: [LINK] (for the latest craze in microtonal music) [2]: [VIDEO] (for a more informative and historical video)"},{"id":46888861,"author":"mbo","month":"2026-02","story":"The Mathematics of Tuning Systems","stance":"rec","why":"Shows audibly how quarter-comma meantone differs from equal temperament","quote":"Brandon Acker gives a wonderful overview on the classical guitar","text":"If anyone wants to hear the practical effects of a 1/4 comma meantone temperament compared to an equal temperament, Brandon Acker gives a wonderful overview on the classical guitar: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32628435,"author":"tzs","month":"2022-08","story":"Why are D-sharp and E-flat considered to be two different notes?","stance":"rec","why":"Tied gut frets and microtonal tuning possibilities on a classical guitar","quote":"Guitarist might find three videos recently posted to classical guitarist and lutist Brandon Acker's YouTube channel interesting.","text":"Guitarist might find three videos recently posted to classical guitarist and lutist Brandon Acker's YouTube channel interesting. Lutes and early guitars (before around 1800) did not have metal frets. Instead they used pieces of string tied around the neck. They did this because the strings were very expensive, with a set of strings for a lute often costing more than the rest of the lute, and strings were not as robust as more modern strings. With metal frets strings would wear out faster. You could easily end up spending more per year on strings than you had initially spent on your instrument. By making the frets of the same material as the strings they didn't need to change strings as often. Acker and luthier M.E. Brune took a classical guitar Brune was building but had not yet put frets on and played around with putting on tied gut frets and gut strings. In the first video [1] they just go over the history of tied on frets, and do some comparisons with metal frets. With tied on frets it is relatively easy to try tunings other than 12-TET. You can change the position of a fret, or you can add extra frets. You can also add partial frets. Renaissance lutists would often glue on small pieces of string behind or in front of a fret. The fret would give them some particular note from a sharp/flat pair, and the little mini fret, called a tastino, would give them the other note from that pair. The second video [2] explores the tuning possibilities of tied on frets and tastinos. Acke"},{"id":46888082,"author":"tzs","month":"2026-02","story":"The Mathematics of Tuning Systems","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates tied frets and tastini for different 12-tone tunings","quote":"Brandon Acker uses a guitar that his luthier friend was building that had not yet had the frets installed","text":"An interesting option for fretted instruments is to build it with 12 regular frets per octave, and then add temporary extra frets if you want to make specific notes more in tune for the key you are in. This was common for lute players in the Renaissance and Baroque periods. Lute frets were usually made of the same material as strings and were tied on around the neck, so adding an extra full width fret was not hard. Of course being tied on lute frets were also movable, so they could also reposition the regular 12 frets instead of adding extras. Instead of tying on an extra full width frets another common option was partial frets. They would glue a piece of string or wood under a specific string, to just give an option to play one note with either the regular fret or the more in tone little fret (called a \"tastino\" (plural is \"tastini\")). In this video [1] Brandon Acker uses a guitar that his luthier friend was building that had not yet had the frets installed to demonstrate with lute style tied on frets some different 12 tone tunings, then demonstrates using tastini to improve specific notes, such as little fret on the G string a little before the first regular fret so that G♯ and A♭ can be different notes. The tastini he uses in the video are simply pieces of string held on with a piece of tape, so quick and easy to add and remove. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40100724,"author":"tzs","month":"2024-04","story":"What Is Wrong with Everyone?","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates tied-on gut frets on a modern classical guitar","quote":"Here are a couple of videos where Brandon Acker (guitarist/lutist/etc) and Marshall Brune (luthier) played around with doing this","text":"Lutes and other fretted instruments in western music back in the renaissance used tied on frets. Each fret was a piece of string tied in a loop around the neck. This meant it was relatively easy to change the position of frets if you wanted to try different temperaments. They could also take small pieces of string and glue them on, so at some places on some strings have an extra option, so that say fretting at the tied on fret would give say Db but fretting on the little glued on fret next to that would give C#. That sounds like what that viola da gamba player might have had. Here are a couple of videos where Brandon Acker (guitarist/lutist/etc) and Marshall Brune (luthier) played around with doing this on a modern Spanish style classical guitar. In the first video [1] they take a guitar Brune was building but had not yet had frets put on and tied gut frets on it. In the second [2] Acker explores moving the frets to better tune the guitar for specific pieces, and then explores adding those extra small frets to some notes. [1] [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bAUVCn_jw5I",1],["EgiMu8A3pi0",1],["HUMGc8hEkpc",1],["mceaM2_zQd8",1],["jhicDUgXyNg",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1],["36GT2zI8lVA",1],["fRMMSyCcTDI",1],["ghUs-84NAAU",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1],["85ZCTuekjGA",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["TLm7Q92xMjQ",1],["94ed4v_T6YM",1],["K4JhruinbWc",1],["YycAzdtUIko",1],["gJ5KV3rzuag",1],["jyQwgBAaBag",1]]},"3Hdn0MuCK_0":{"wy":"Cited in threads about cheap chargers as proof of why proper certified and isolated power supplies are worth paying for.","q":{"x":"He earned my respect from a video on a USB charger with an electrocution risk","i":20702556,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20702556,"author":"bigtrakzapzap","month":"2019-08","story":"Inside a Poundland computer mouse [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows electrocution risk of cheap USB chargers","quote":"He earned my respect from a video on a USB charger with an electrocution risk","text":"Big clive on HN. Hurray! He earned my respect from a video on a USB charger with an electrocution risk and why you should never buy cheap USB chargers. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13918096,"author":"BrailleHunting","month":"2017-03","story":"Man dies charging iPhone while in the bath","stance":"rec","why":"Teardowns showing why quality isolated chargers are worth paying for","quote":"Watch these USB charger teardown horror-shows to further understand why it's rational to pay for proper, authentic CE+UL","text":"TL;DR: spend the $19 to buy a real, big brand USB charger because they have more to lose than grey market no brand. Tragic. Probably cheap USB without mains isolation or maybe enough water dripped down length of cable and socket to create a circuit, because USB is supposed to be 5V DC. Watch these USB charger teardown horror-shows to further understand why it's rational to pay for proper, authentic CE+UL, high-low isolation and quality components (ie insulated tape in transformers): [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":41116476,"author":"bankcust08385","month":"2024-07","story":"Amazon forced to recall 400K products that could kill, electrocute people","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This has been a common occurrence since 2016","text":"So I guess there will be more Bigclivedotcom deathdapter teardowns. This has been a common occurrence since 2016: [VIDEO] There need to be more class-action lawsuits against Amazon, and the FTC and CPSC need to surveil Amazon's products and take regular enforcement action."},{"id":39857005,"author":"1letterunixname","month":"2024-03","story":"We made a hot dog talk with RF [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"(2016) BigClive Electrocution risk of cheap USB chargers","text":"Related classics: (2019) BigClive overclocks a 120v Presto hot dogger to 250v [LINK] (2019) Veratasium grape microwave plasma [LINK] (2022) BigClive The dangers of microwave transformers [LINK] (2016) BigClive Electrocution risk of cheap USB chargers [VIDEO] (2020) ElectroBOOM proves 5G isn't going to give you cancer or eat your children [LINK]"},{"id":27011620,"author":"airhead969","month":"2021-05","story":"California appeals court finds Amazon responsible for 3rd party sellers products","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"No more cheap/counterfeit USB deathdapters","text":"It stands to reason that selling arbitrary offshore products without liability insurance will come to an end. Almost every offshore brand and reseller has a throwaway name so they can just fold up and start over should anything happen. No more cheap/counterfeit USB deathdapters [VIDEO] Also, I think electrical, li-ion, and RF products will have to prove their safety and compliance certification paperwork."},{"id":27098129,"author":"failwhaleshark","month":"2021-05","story":"Amazon Fake Reviews Scam Exposed in Data Breach","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Computer killing powered USB hub. (Mac murderer) [LINK] Horrific USB power supply fault. (Electrocution risk.) [VIDEO]"},{"id":11478638,"author":"oxplot","month":"2016-04","story":"USB power supply puts 220 Mains power out on USB jack","stance":"reference","why":"Possible explanation for tingling from charger casings","quote":"Finally a possible explanation [1] as to why Apple chargers (USB and laptop) give you tingles","text":"Finally a possible explanation [1] as to why Apple chargers (USB and laptop) give you tingles when touching the metal casing of the device they're plugged into. Would love to know if the mentioned capacitor is indeed the reason and whether it leads to enough efficiency to justify the scare it causes with first time customers. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11583149,"author":"mrob","month":"2016-04","story":"Searching for USB Power Supplies That Won't Explode","stance":"reference","why":"Teardown of a USB supply dangerous from transformer damage","quote":"here's one of a USB power supply that's dangerous because of internal damage to the transformer","text":"Sometimes even a well designed and assembled power supply can be dangerous because of defective components. Big Clive on Youtube does many teardowns of cheap electronics, and here's one of a USB power supply that's dangerous because of internal damage to the transformer: [VIDEO] Because this is an intermittent fault it's possible that non-destructive testing would not find it."}],"r":[["uRe9w5PKmsE",1],["QPKKQnijnsM",1]]},"QPgqfnKG_T4":{"wy":"HN loves it as a funny, inspiring hobby project and calls it one of the best conference talks ever.","q":{"x":"Perhaps the best presentation to a programming language conference in history.","i":8440420,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8440420,"author":"kjs3","month":"2014-10","story":"Human vs. Squirrel: The Battle of Wits Is On","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Considered one of the best programming conference presentations.","quote":"Perhaps the best presentation to a programming language conference in history.","text":"\"Militarizing Your Backyard With Python\" - Perhaps the best presentation to a programming language conference in history. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17340136,"author":"Tiki","month":"2018-06","story":"Face recognition in images and video with Python","stance":"rec","why":"Project ideas: ML and servo-controlled squirt gun against squirrels.","quote":"Check this video out for ideas","text":"I don't think you'll find a 'quick hack' but what you're asking is definitely possible as a project. Check this video out for ideas(guy used ML/servo controlled squirt gun to get rid of squirrels from his bird house) [VIDEO]"},{"id":8440008,"author":"mpdehaan2","month":"2014-10","story":"Human vs. Squirrel: The Battle of Wits Is On","stance":"rec","why":"Builds a turret that recognizes and blasts squirrels with water.","quote":"shows how to build a water-hose-turret that automatically recognizes and blasts squirrels","text":"In related squirrel news: The \"Daylight Robbery\" documentary has some very interesting squirrel obstacle courses. You can find it on youtube - [LINK] \"Militarizing your Backyard with Python\" shows how to build a water-hose-turret that automatically recognizes and blasts squirrels - [VIDEO]"},{"id":36139528,"author":"Retric","month":"2023-05","story":"Less than 168 hours since Photoshop dropped the new “Generative Fill” feature","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a hobbyist's AI-controlled water sentry gun.","quote":"AI controlled sentry gun for shooting squirrels with water","text":"Some Hobbyists still want to push the envelope. Think the artist equivalent of the guy building a AI controlled sentry gun for shooting squirrels with water. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31523836,"author":"pjmorris","month":"2022-05","story":"Why unprecedented birdflu outbreaks sweeping the world are concerning scientists","stance":"reference","why":"Squirrel detector and water gun protecting bird feeders harmlessly.","quote":"somebody built a squirrel detector/watergun combo to protect their bird feeders","text":"Ten years ago, somebody built a squirrel detector/watergun combo to protect their bird feeders without harming the squirrels, [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7912113,"author":"sudont","month":"2014-06","story":"African firm is selling pepper-spray bullet firing drones","stance":"reference","why":"Using computer vision to fire a squirt gun at squirrels.","quote":"It deals with using computer vision to fire a squirt gun at squirrels.","text":"Indeed. Here's something somewhat related, from PyCon 2012. It deals with using computer vision to fire a squirt gun at squirrels. The principle is basically the same. [VIDEO]"}]},"cQWlnTyOSig":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it in college-ranking and higher-ed threads as an explanation of why rankings are a hustle for foreign student revenue.","q":{"x":"Polymatter recently had a great video on the way the college ranking system is a hustle for foreign student money","i":30745955,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30745955,"author":"acidbaseextract","month":"2022-03","story":"I think US college education is nearer to collapsing than it appears","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how rankings are a hustle for foreign student money.","quote":"Polymatter recently had a great video on the way the college ranking system is a hustle for foreign student money","text":"Polymatter recently had a great video on the way the college ranking system is a hustle for foreign student money, and just how heavily it distorts colleges incentives: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32063437,"author":"dnissley","month":"2022-07","story":"U.S. News pulls Columbia University from its 2022 rankings","stance":"rec","why":"Episode on pressures that distort university rankings.","quote":"Polymatter did a great episode on the pressures that create outcomes like this in regards to rankings","text":"Polymatter did a great episode on the pressures that create outcomes like this in regards to rankings: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33075127,"author":"TheLoafOfBread","month":"2022-10","story":"NYU organic chemistry professor terminated for tough grading","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for rankings being arbitrary metrics aimed at foreign students.","quote":"see PolyMatter The Chinese Student Crisis","text":"The \"how good is university\" is based on some arbitrary metric, which does not tell anything to the student, but works on foreign students see PolyMatter The Chinese Student Crisis - [VIDEO] It has even lower value for Europeans, because even if some university in Paris would be THE BEST in the world, it still will have only a national character. For example some guy from Poland is not going to study there, simply because when you are 20 years old, then you can barely speak English on A2, but at that case, to actually understand and learn, you would need to speak French on B2 (of course, we can find rare exceptions). So in the end you are choosing your school from less than dozen national universities (could be less or more, depends on size of your country) and then you will decide by their program - Do you want to study agriculture university with a program on genetical engineering of plants? Do you want to study technical university with a program of computer science? etc. All those universities are national (public), there are also some private universities, but you will go in those if you are mandated to have diploma (i.e. some governmental positions). The problem with private universities is that non-governmental employers are not taking them seriously and they will look at you as on somebody who accidentally bought his diploma with his newspapers."}],"r":[["K_q-xnYRugQ",1],["oLkfnc_UMcE",1],["gSKzgpt4HBU",1],["Pi7l8mMjYVE",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1]]},"vVX-PrBRtTY":{"wy":"It is the go-to link in any HN thread on map projections, usually posted as the obligatory West Wing reference.","q":{"x":"one of the best ways to teach someone about how different our classic maps are from reality","i":10583829,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10583829,"author":"Amorymeltzer","month":"2015-11","story":"The last unmapped places on Earth","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how classic maps differ from reality.","quote":"one of the best ways to teach someone about how different our classic maps are from reality","text":"The West Wing clip on maps is still one of the best ways to teach someone about how different our classic maps are from reality: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25104992,"author":"eyelidlessness","month":"2020-11","story":"The True Size Of Countries: Mercator projection","stance":"rec","why":"Fantastic West Wing scene on map projection.","quote":"Requisite West Wing scene[1] on the subject","text":"Requisite West Wing scene[1] on the subject (which is probably always going to be my top YouTube search because it’s fantastic). [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40114757,"author":"brianshaler","month":"2024-04","story":"A flat map with the least error possible: The Gott-Goldberg-Vanderbei projection","stance":"reference","why":"West Wing bit used in map projection debates","quote":"this West Wing bit is my go-to for map projection discussions","text":"I made an interactive WebGL implementation of this without realizing it was a thing: [LINK] I don't really agree with the claims in the articles linked in OP, and don't find it to be a generally useful projection, even for the tool I made using it. It was novel as a representation that included daylight context (instead of just \"what time is it there?\" it helped express \"is it getting dark there?\") that preserved area better than a globe and was more intuitive than a day/night waveform on a rectilinear projection. But ultimately, if you're showing anything that has to do with populations (cities, people) pretty much any projection will waste large amounts of space on oceans and unpopulated land regions. That is to say, before choosing a favorite map projection, I think it's probably better to not to use a map projection at all unless you're going for a hike or setting sail. Somebody beat me to the obligatory xkcd, but this West Wing bit is my go-to for map projection discussions: [VIDEO] I like framing map projections by what they prioritize or sacrifice—fidelity in axis, position, size—and what projection is \"best\" depends entirely on which characteristics are more important. I disagree with OP's claim about this projection being \"the most accurate flat map of the Earth yet\" though haven't put a ton of thought into the physical, back-to-back definition of \"flat\" vs on-screen."},{"id":36425436,"author":"resolutebat","month":"2023-06","story":"Maps Distort How We See the World","stance":"reference","why":"TV scene advocating Gall-Peters projection","quote":"Although the projection it proclaims as superior, Gall-Peters, has grievous flaws of its own.","text":"I'd like to think you're being ironic here, but I suspect you're not, so no: the vast majority of the world is outside the US and has not, in fact, seen an obscure episode of a TV drama about American politics. For others who haven't, here it is: [VIDEO] Although the projection it proclaims as superior, Gall-Peters, has grievous flaws of its own. [LINK]"},{"id":29546833,"author":"greggman3","month":"2021-12","story":"Reimagining projections for the interactive maps era","stance":"reference","why":"West Wing scene on political implications of the Mercator projection","quote":"obligatory link to West Wing scene where they point out the political implications of the mercator projection","text":"obligatory link to West Wing scene where they point out the political implications of the mercator projection [VIDEO]"},{"id":25106045,"author":"ZeroCool2u","month":"2020-11","story":"The True Size Of Countries: Mercator projection","stance":"reference","why":"West Wing episode explaining the map-size problem quickly.","quote":"which explains the problem pretty quickly","text":"I still remember the first time I learned about this. I was watching the West Wing (Season 2 Episode 16[1] which explains the problem pretty quickly) for the first time in the early 2010's and was absolutely blown away that we'd never been taught about this in public school (U.S.A.). Having graduated High School and a couple of years through undergrad, and just then realizing that my entire perception of the relative size, and in some cases position(!), of the continents is basically just wrong. It was actually a generally useful thought exercise though, because it really forced me to confront the fact that I need to consistently reflect on whether my previous assumptions on even basic topics hold true. I'm not sure it would be worthwhile to advocate for changing the default projection at this point due to the logistics, but at least teaching kids about the issue, which can be covered in no more than a few minutes and is obviously not super complex, just so they're aware of it seems totally worth it! [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10202856,"author":"teh_klev","month":"2015-09","story":"Steve Wozniak on Steve Jobs Movie","stance":"reference","why":"West Wing scene teaching the Peters projection.","quote":"dropping in interesting educational nuggets such as the Peters Projection cartography stuff","text":"I dunno, I like most of Sorkin's work. However I couldn't watch \"The Newsroom\" past four or five episodes. You're right, it seems like The West Wing's characters all re-hashed into TV news characters and it was too cloying. I still go back and re-watch The West Wing every couple of years. Yes some of it is cringe-worthy, but as a non-US citiizen when first broadcast I learned a few things about US politics and it caused me find out more, especially from a historical point of view. He also does some nice things such as dropping in interesting educational nuggets such as the Peters Projection cartography stuff in the second \"Big Block of Cheese\" episode (S2E16) [0]. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9466690,"author":"teh_klev","month":"2015-04","story":"Printing a wall-sized world map and what I've learned from it","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"obligatory West Wing \"Gall Peters Projection\" clip","text":"And obligatory West Wing \"Gall Peters Projection\" clip: \"What the hell is that?\" \"It's where you've been living this whole time\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["d_FEaFgJyfA",1]]},"069y1MpOkQY":{"wy":"HN points to it as a short, clear answer to why budget airlines are so cheap.","q":{"x":"Wendover has a short 8 minute video","i":15828808,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":19562677,"author":"pimmen","month":"2019-04","story":"Sweden Wants to Revive Europe’s Overnight Trains","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why European budget airlines are so cheap.","quote":"Well, lots and lots and lots of reasons why.","text":"Europe has extremely cheap prizes because the big airlines are trying to compete with the budget airlines. The real question is \"why are budget airlines so cheap in Europe (they often cost about a third of what a budget ticket in the US would cost)?\". Well, lots and lots and lots of reasons why. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15828808,"author":"jasode","month":"2017-12","story":"Trying to spark an emotional connection in the commoditized airline market","stance":"rec","why":"Short explainer on how budget airlines charge lower prices.","quote":"Wendover has a short 8 minute video","text":"For some extra background on EasyJet and Ryanair's ability to charge lower prices than Air France and KLM, Wendover has a short 8 minute video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19148465,"author":"chipperyman573","month":"2019-02","story":"The Colgan Air disaster was a milestone in aviation safety","stance":"reference","why":"Source for airline per-passenger margins.","quote":"You would be pretty surprised how many costs airlines have to pay.","text":"You should check this video out: [LINK] You would be pretty surprised how many costs airlines have to pay. An extra $4/passenger/flight would eat significantly into their bottom line. This is especially true for budget airlines, which often already make less than this per passenger already (source: [VIDEO] )"}],"r":[["B2482h_TNwg",1],["Bd8_vO5zrjo",1],["gDfNRWsMRsU",1],["wh4aWZRtTwU",1],["HSxSgbNQi-g",1],["qs2QcycggWU",1],["NlIdzF1_b5M",1],["KRcecxdGxvQ",1],["0MeRN7LE1LQ",1],["hBpetDxIEMU",1]]},"1faaOrtHJ-A":{"wy":"HN points to it as a first-hand account of game netcode and engineering lessons from a Blizzard and Guild Wars developer.","q":{"x":"good interview with him talking about screwing up Diablo and doing a good job on Guild Wars","i":17339911,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":37966802,"author":"andai","month":"2023-10","story":"MMO Architecture: clients, sockets, threads and connection-oriented servers","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with a Blizzard netcode developer.","quote":"Patrick Wyatt interviewed by Casey Muratori at HandmadeCon","text":"See also: Patrick Wyatt interviewed by Casey Muratori at HandmadeCon: [VIDEO] He was the 2nd employee at Blizzard, did the netcode for their games and also for Guild Wars. His blog is great [LINK]"},{"id":17339911,"author":"gameswithgo","month":"2018-06","story":"Diablo devolved – magic behind the 1996 computer game","stance":"rec","why":"Interview about Diablo mistakes and Guild Wars success.","quote":"good interview with him talking about screwing up Diablo and doing a good job on Guild Wars","text":"What is fascinating is the guy who coded and designed the multiplayer is actually a genius, he was just inexperienced at the time. His next big project was a huge, complicated, MMO that you could start playing before it finished downloading, and everything worked from day 1, which is rather unique among MMOs!] edit, sorry: Pat Wyatt of Guild Wars good interview with him talking about screwing up Diablo and doing a good job on Guild Wars [VIDEO]"},{"id":31428005,"author":"evilturnip","month":"2022-05","story":"Foundations of Game Engine Development","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Someone like Pat Wyatt comes to mind","text":"There are a lot fewer people with this expertise, and of those, that can communicate it well and have the time to write a book I'm sure makes them a relatively small group of people. They probably mostly work in AAA studios, since only they have the scale and money to encounter and tackle these problems. I do wish more of them would, however. Someone like Pat Wyatt comes to mind: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12340090,"author":"xjay","month":"2016-08","story":"The Making of Warcraft (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Talk about network code in games.","quote":"where they talked about the network code/design in games he had worked on","text":"He was at HandmadeCon 2015 [1] where they talked about the network code/design in games he had worked on, and various subjects relating to the gaming industry. This article was referenced at Gamasutra a few days ago, to which his response was that he should blog more. [2] [1] HandmadeCon 2015 - Pat Wyatt [VIDEO] [2] Patrick Wyatt [LINK]"}],"r":[["BzhbAK1pdPM",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["qv6UVOQ0F44",1],["cfyWvJdsDRI",1],["eQ2OZKitRwc",1]]},"3BJU2drrtCM":{"wy":"Frequently linked as visual proof of how a CRT's raster scan and phosphor decay work.","q":{"x":"This YouTube video by \"The Slow Mo Guys\" shows this in action","i":44551618,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":47426853,"author":"kevincox","month":"2026-03","story":"JPEG Compression","stance":"rec","why":"Great slow-motion footage of CRT scanning and phosphor trails.","quote":"This video has some great slow-mo of CRTs","text":"That's not quite true. Only one pixel is being activated at a time but the phosphors continue to emit light for many pixels. In practice you get a handful of lines lit to varying degrees at at time. Maybe 1-2 lines quite brightly lit and then a trail of lines that are fading pretty significantly (but still emitting light). They yes, our persistence of vision fills in the rest to provide the appearance of a fully lit screen. This video has some great slow-mo of CRTs [VIDEO]"},{"id":44551618,"author":"retrac","month":"2025-07","story":"How does a screen work?","stance":"rec","why":"Slow Mo Guys show the CRT single-dot raster scan in action.","quote":"This YouTube video by \"The Slow Mo Guys\" shows this in action","text":"CRTs are still slightly magical to me. The image doesn't really exist. It's an illusion. If your eyes operated at electronic speeds, you would see a single incredibly bright dot-point drawing the raster pattern over and over. This YouTube video by \"The Slow Mo Guys\" shows this in action: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42609717,"author":"rcxdude","month":"2025-01","story":"Blur Busters Open Source Display Initiative – Refresh Cycle Shaders","stance":"reference","why":"Slow-motion video illustrating CRT scanning flicker.","quote":"see this youtube video showing one in slow-motion to get an idea","text":"They frequently flicker or are only showing part of an image in video footage or photos, for exactly this reason. They're a right headache to film clearly. (see this youtube video showing one in slow-motion to get an idea: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":38539664,"author":"chongli","month":"2023-12","story":"Checkmate 19\" IPS Retro Monitor","stance":"reference","why":"High-speed video of a CRT raster scan.","quote":"If you look at high-speed video of a CRT during a raster scan, the line is visible for far less time than the entire frame [1].","text":"That's an oversimplification of the physics. CRT televisions have very low persistence phosphors. If you look at high-speed video of a CRT during a raster scan, the line is visible for far less time than the entire frame [1]. This technique relies on persistence of vision [2] to create the illusion of a complete frame. If you want to calculate the response time of a single dot on the phosphor, it is way down into the microseconds. I don't know enough about OLED displays to comment. I do know that LCDs don't even come close to the speed of CRTs at transitioning through high contrast images, the key to producing fast and responsive motion of sprites (which are high contrast against the background by design in most retro games). [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":38328295,"author":"mortehu","month":"2023-11","story":"Simple streetlight hack could protect astronomy from urban light pollution","stance":"reference","why":"High speed footage shows only a few CRT scan lines lit at once.","quote":"If you look at high speed footage of a CRT monitor, you'll see that only a few scan lines are lit at a time.","text":"If you look at high speed footage of a CRT monitor, you'll see that only a few scan lines are lit at a time. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37821941,"author":"aimor","month":"2023-10","story":"How Games Used to Look: Why Retro Gaming on a CRT Looks Different [video]","stance":"reference","why":"High-framerate CRT video showing colors decaying at different rates.","quote":"in this high framerate video of a CRT you can see the different colors decay at different rates.","text":"\"The video makes it seems that there's no temporal bleeding on CRTs. This sounds unlikely to me...\" There is, but I don't remember it being noticeable even on cheap TVs. I still miss the lack of motion blur that CRTs gave by default. I found a forum post where someone lists these values: \"Phosphors in current use for CRT-applications: Red: phosphor = Y2O2S:Eu3+. Lifetime (1/e time) = 150 us, single exponential. Green: phosphor = Zn2SiO4:Mn2+. Lifetime (1/e time) = 10-15 ms. Clearly, this is quite long and is the limiting factor in increasing frame-frequency. Note: the emission decay has a highly non-single-exponential decay --> at long times (~1 s) still some emission can be observed by the eye. This can be seen clearly by looking into the green CRT directly after the image was switched off. However, the intensity is too low to cause problems in an active image when the image-frequency is below ~100 Hz. Blue: phosphor = ZnS:Ag,Cl. Lifetime (1/e time) = ~100-200 us. Note: not single-exponential decay, but no emission at long times. (Source: Shionoya & Yen, \"Phosphor Handbook\", 1998.)\" [LINK] And in this high framerate video of a CRT you can see the different colors decay at different rates. Here the blue seems to decay the fastest. But they're all imperceptibly dark by the time the scan line comes around again. I have no idea if there's any cumulative effect that's perceptible. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":35118630,"author":"iforgotpassword","month":"2023-03","story":"Secret Colours of the Commodore 64","stance":"reference","why":"Slow-motion footage showing how CRT phosphor persistence works.","quote":"CRTs did have some afterglow but it wasn't even for a full frame, let alone two.","text":"It's said later in the article that you'd need two colors with similar brightness to avoid perceivable flicker. CRTs did have some afterglow but it wasn't even for a full frame, let alone two. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31641435,"author":"rasz","month":"2022-06","story":"Video codecs and 4k","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"1. As GL1zdA already pointed out in blog comments 1992 Creative Video Blaster CT6000 was a single frame screen grabber and not a video encoding/decoding accelerator. YT \"Creative Videoblaster CT6000 in action\" [LINK] shows what it does. [LINK] \"converts a standard full-motion video image (composite video source) into a format for display on a computer graphics monitor\" emphasis on the _image_. \"It can position and scale the video image onto the output display and allows the video image to be merged with computer graphics for interactive multimedia applicaiton.\" means overlay analog video on VGA monitor. The first true video decoding accelerator on PC market came out of David Sarnoff Research Center owned by RCA, later purchased by GE, then finally video tech carved out and sold to Intel. Called Digital Video Interactive [LINK] it did in fact \"real time audio and video decompression, 30 frames per second with a resolution of 320x240\". Intel repackaged it into one chip i750 and called it Indeo. Creative released a product based on it in mid 1994 - Video Blaster RT300. PCI bus was already mainstream in 1994, as were Pentiums. You most likely blurred those two together into one product. 2. \"so the quality could be very high. Streams of 8 to 12 mbit of data for a single channel were no exception.\" SD video DVD bitrate is 10Mbit, and here we have OTA stations pushing 720p/1080i at same or lower bw using same codec (US mpeg2). Whole multiplexer in ATSC offers ~20Mbit, and then TV st"}],"r":[["H62hZJVqs2o",1]]},"9U0XVdvQwAI":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a striking example of rendering with distance-estimated fractals and non-standard graphics approaches.","q":{"x":"He has a YouTube channel as well","i":33414364,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33414364,"author":"CyborgCabbage","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: What piece of code/codebase blew your mind when you saw it?","stance":"rec","why":"Game using a similar rendering approach.","quote":"you may like Marble Marcher made by Code Parade, it's a game that uses a similar approach to rendering","text":"He has a YouTube channel as well: [LINK] Also you may like Marble Marcher made by Code Parade, it's a game that uses a similar approach to rendering: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23307947,"author":"gitgud","month":"2020-05","story":"Show HN: Fractal garden – realtime 3D fractal explorer in the browser","stance":"rec","why":"Fractal physics game with impressive detail.","quote":"amazing how much detail can be rendered using fractals","text":"This is incredible! If you like this you might also like [1] Marble Marcher , amazing how much detail can be rendered using fractals [1] [VIDEO] (Video)"},{"id":42200281,"author":"FrostKiwi","month":"2024-11","story":"AAA – Analytical Anti-Aliasing","stance":"reference","why":"Example of non-standard rendering approaches.","quote":"non-standard rendering approaches are very much a thing [1]","text":"True, this is something I struggled with writing and ended up with just a small note commenting that this is not widely applicable. Will clarify more in coming posts. It's all incredibly context specific. The reason for this order, is that you can very much can use all these approaches (SSAA, FXAA, MSAA etc.) for rendering simpler shapes and HUDs. So going through them, where these approaches break down and when it does make sense to go the extra mile with SDFs. Still, non-standard rendering approaches are very much a thing [1] and I could see setups like [2] be used in scientific particle visualizations. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["whPWKecazgM",1],["jlX_pThh7z8",1],["tc4ROCJYbm0",1]]},"BHEbVh3Yhrw":{"wy":"Recommended as a good conversation and starting point on Yelp's alleged bullying of businesses.","q":{"x":"he talks with two of the people behind the movie Billion Dollar Bully.","i":22832479,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22832479,"author":"james-skemp","month":"2020-04","story":"Yelp lays off 1000, furloughs 1100","stance":"rec","why":"Conversation with two people behind Billion Dollar Bully; better starting point.","quote":"he talks with two of the people behind the movie Billion Dollar Bully.","text":"Just pointing out what I had heard when I was subscribed to someone who had experiences with Yelp. Honestly, I found his latest video on the subject, after searching for 'yelp extortion,' which doesn't appear to be the best one to start with. [VIDEO] may be better, as he talks with two of the people behind the movie Billion Dollar Bully. (I didn't re-watch it completely, as I don't want to get myself wrapped back up in his world.) As it notes in [LINK] and the above, if Yelp's culture means that employees are getting away with it, and sharing that, and tend to work with people who aren't web-savvy, there's certainly the opportunity for certain individuals within the company to give the company a bad name. For-profit businesses, especially ones that want everyone to use them, generally don't like to talk about the fact that some part of why they exist is for the money and/or they have stakeholders that want to see a return on their investment. It's also possible that Yelp has since gotten better, and they're still fighting off the old perspectives. Not OP so not sure where their evidence came from. (I personally rarely use Yelp, don't have an account, and prefer the reviews built into Google Maps since that's my navigation app of choice. I didn't know about issues with Yelp until I was subscribed about the above, and after unsubscribing numerous months ago haven't heard anything else about Yelp until now.)"},{"id":18419689,"author":"vco1","month":"2018-11","story":"Yelp craters 30% as advertisers abandon the site","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with documentary makers about Yelp bullying businesses.","quote":"a great interview with some people who made a documentary about yelp practices of bullying businneses to join their service","text":"I've always enjoyed Louis Rossmann yelp rants. A week ago he posted a great interview with some people who made a documentary about yelp practices of bullying businneses to join their service Link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17116728,"author":"seltzered_","month":"2018-05","story":"CBS “60 Minutes” piece on Google’s abuse of dominance","stance":"reference","why":"Interview with the makers of the Billion Dollar Bully documentary.","quote":"(recent interview with the film creator)","text":"Only sharing for reference, you reminded me that someone made a documentary ('billion dollar bully') about this (not released yet, but supposedly being premiered soon): [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] (recent interview with the film creator)"}]},"HipTO_7mUOw":{"wy":"HN cites it whenever drone swarms or autonomous weapons come up, as a dramatized warning that now feels increasingly plausible.","q":{"x":"7 years ago, this felt like science fiction","i":44148441,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42511348,"author":"amarcheschi","month":"2024-12","story":"Slaughterbots – if human: kill() [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Another Slaughterbots video worth watching on autonomous killer drones.","quote":"There is another slaughterbots video that's worth watching","text":"There is another slaughterbots video that's worth watching, [VIDEO] There's also a real video by anduril industries about this not anymore fictional scenario [LINK]"},{"id":18306346,"author":"pierrec","month":"2018-10","story":"The Era of the Drone Swarm Is Coming","stance":"rec","why":"Very well done short film with an HN vibe","quote":"It's very well done and has an HN vibe to it","text":"I'm surprised there's no mention of the short film \"Slaughterbots\" on this exact topic: [VIDEO] It's very well done and has an HN vibe to it (with a dark twist, of course)."},{"id":44148441,"author":"echelon","month":"2025-06","story":"YOLO-World: Real-Time Open-Vocabulary Object Detection","stance":"reference","why":"Once-fictional film on autonomous drones that now seems plausible.","quote":"7 years ago, this felt like science fiction","text":"7 years ago, this felt like science fiction: [VIDEO] Now that we've seen the use of drones in the Ukraine war, 10k+ drone light shows, Waymo's autonomous cars, and tons of AI advancements in signals processing and planning, this seems obvious."},{"id":31632435,"author":"runlevel1","month":"2022-06","story":"Axon wants to make taser drones for schools despite ethics board’s concerns","stance":"reference","why":"Dramatization of fully autonomous attack drones and why the threat is serious.","quote":"This dramatization][^1] focuses on full autonomy","text":"Why require RF at all? An attacker could give the drone a location they know their target will be at and let the drone autonomously guide itself to the target. [This dramatization][^1] focuses on full autonomy, but I'd argue it applies just as much to the steps leading up to it. All the more reason to take this threat seriously now. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30991657,"author":"imglorp","month":"2022-04","story":"A story about planning for Desert Storm","stance":"reference","why":"Depicts cheap mass-produced drones killing humans while leaving infrastructure intact.","quote":"","text":"I think it depends, right? If you have a small number of high cost, high impact UAVs, you will target infra and supply columns. If you happen to have a cargo plane full of cheap, mass produced head-popper slaughterbots which you can dump over a town, they will remove all the humans and leave you with intact buildings, vehicles, and infra. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28795085,"author":"EastOfTruth","month":"2021-10","story":"Autonomous drone navigates independently through the forest at 40 km/h","stance":"reference","why":"Dramatized warning about autonomous slaughter drones.","quote":"We are getting closer to a slaughter bot that is actually usable","text":"We are getting closer to a slaughter bot that is actually usable: [VIDEO] my guess is that politicians will get scared when it becomes a reality"},{"id":27751330,"author":"tgsovlerkhgsel","month":"2021-07","story":"Killer Flying Robots Are Here. What Do We Do Now?","stance":"reference","why":"Fictional short film about swarms of autonomous killer drones.","quote":"The short film \"Slaughterbots\" ( ) fits it even more precisely though, I'd say.","text":"Killer bees are in S02E06 \"Hated in the Nation\", [LINK] There is another one with killer dogs in an apocalyptic wasteland (in black and white), S04E05 \"Metalhead\". The short film \"Slaughterbots\" ( [VIDEO] ) fits it even more precisely though, I'd say."},{"id":25945982,"author":"Diederich","month":"2021-01","story":"Drone Swarms Are Getting Too Fast for Humans to Fight, U.S. General Warns","stance":"reference","why":"Sensationalized but raises how near the technology is","quote":"I know it is rather sensationalized, but how far off is this kind of technology?","text":"I know it is rather sensationalized, but how far off is this kind of technology? [VIDEO] \"Slaughterbots\" posted in 2017. If I had to guess, I'd say that the tiny drones in this video still aren't capable of carrying the computational horsepower necessary for a lot of the activities portrayed. But...isn't it really a matter of time?"},{"id":17067619,"author":"trumped","month":"2018-05","story":"Boston Dynamics will start selling its dog-like SpotMini robot in 2019","stance":"reference","why":"Potentially more dangerous with simpler hardware","quote":"Have you seen the Slaughterbots video?","text":"Have you seen the Slaughterbots video? They seem potentially more dangerous and the hardware is much simpler: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16761679,"author":"fabianhjr","month":"2018-04","story":"Google Workers Urge C.E.O. To Pull Out of Pentagon A.I. Project","stance":"reference","why":"Sci-fi short showing paths of developments","quote":"I think that scifi shorts have the duty and power to show us the paths that certain developments entail.","text":"The problem emerges when an actor most become a monster to win, sort of like Mutually Assured Destruction. I think that scifi shorts have the duty and power to show us the paths that certain developments entail. For example: - Slaughterbots: [VIDEO] - Last Day of War: [LINK]"},{"id":31383038,"author":"Vladimof","month":"2022-05","story":"Shaped Charges – Sheet of copper going through 1ft of solid steel (2010) [video]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Did you just upgrade the Slaughter bots? [VIDEO] actually I think that you didn't because slaughter bots are trying to not cause any collateral damage"},{"id":42939729,"author":"archagon","month":"2025-02","story":"Palantir designed to 'power the West to its obvious innate superiority' says CEO","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Tyranny as a service is coming and the techno-fascists are thrilled to be in control.","text":"Now I'm convinced that these degenerates saw the Slaughterbots[1] video and thought \"what a great idea!\" Tyranny as a service is coming and the techno-fascists are thrilled to be in control. Welcome to the torment nexus. [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["-ZxPhDC-r3w",1],["RoGuvvzHY1A",1]]},"Jv7sLrON7QY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an insightful catalog of the biases that drive bad decisions, and links it often in Munger memorials and self-improvement threads.","q":{"x":"heres his talk explaining some of his excellent theories","i":39945507,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39945507,"author":"robocat","month":"2024-04","story":"So you wanna de-bog yourself","stance":"rec","why":"Munger explaining his theories on human misjudgment","quote":"heres his talk explaining some of his excellent theories","text":"> they've created a bespoke system of abstract beliefs swatcoder, can you please tell me how you particularly manage to learn from your mistakes? Some people create personal theories of how the world works, and then put their theories into action : A successful example is Charlie Munger and heres his talk explaining some of his excellent theories: [VIDEO] Some people overthink things and label things. That can be unhealthy, and Adam touches on that with his \"Stroking the problem\" section. But the opposite can be unhealthy too: not thinking at all and repeating over and over making the same mistakes. > catchy names Useful when communicating with others if it is an uncommon concept. I like to search for the perfect word or perfect catchphrase when thinking about a problem or solution because it helps my thinking. > various example cases How do you do it? Don't you look at specific issues you find in your own life? Examples yes? And then generalise from that? I just don't understand what you're complaining about. The closest I can think is that you're anti-intellectual or you're a \"Just do it\" prosyletiser - the implication being that thinking is unnecessary and the problem. Academic over-thinking is its own problem: is that your issue? Like most things: we need to find the right balance. Thinking too little is bad in a different way from thinking too much. > intellectualize and systematize and engineer things I'm just gobsmacked at your choice of words: is systemizing and engin"},{"id":34209328,"author":"chrisco255","month":"2023-01","story":"Today is Charlie Munger's 99th birthday","stance":"rec","why":"Munger's 1995 Harvard speech on human biases","quote":"found his 1995 speech he gave on human biases at Harvard to be insightful and interesting","text":"I'm not necessarily the biggest Charlie Munger fan, but I think he's got some wisdom and found his 1995 speech he gave on human biases at Harvard to be insightful and interesting: \"The Psychology of Human Misjudgment\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":38451638,"author":"mdp2021","month":"2023-11","story":"Charlie Munger has died","stance":"reference","why":"Recording of the original 1995 Harvard speech","quote":"A recording of the original speech is at","text":"That linked is the revised version A transcript of the original speech at Harvard, June 1995, should be e.g. at [LINK] A recording of the original speech is at [VIDEO]"},{"id":38456398,"author":"msolujic","month":"2023-11","story":"Psychology of Human Misjudgment (Transcript) by Charlie Munger","stance":"reference","why":"Recording of the original 1995 Harvard speech","quote":"A recording of the original speech is at","text":"A transcript of the original speech at Harvard, June 1995, should be e.g. at [LINK] ... A recording of the original speech is at [VIDEO]"},{"id":34781801,"author":"robocat","month":"2023-02","story":"Mariana Mazzucato: “The McKinseys and the Deloittes have no expertise ”","stance":"reference","why":"Munger on professionals acting in self-interest, with consultant example","quote":"every profession is a conspiracy against the laity","text":"Charlie used a quote from George Bernard Shaw's play “In the last analysis, every profession is a conspiracy against the laity.” I personally wonder how many therapists say “you’re fixed, I don’t need any more of your money”. The quote on management consultants was just one example of how many professionals act this way towards businesses, and how it wasn't only about money but also a subconscious, psychological tendency. “The guy tells you what is good for him, and he doesn’t recognize that he’s doing anything wrong any more than that doctor did when he was pulling out all those normal gallbladders.” [VIDEO] Transcript: [LINK]"}],"r":[["wSdV1M7n4gQ",1],["ia7IKW0yuG0",1],["otbnC2zE2rw",1],["Nb2tebYAaOA",1],["plVk4NVIUh8",1],["u-x7PdnvCyI",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["Mjx3S3UjmnA",1],["BiKfWdXXfIs",1],["DgVS-zXgMTk",1]]},"_loUDS4c3Cs":{"wy":"HN points to it as a concise, technically grounded rebuttal to moon-hoax claims.","q":{"x":"This video goes through why the video technology at the time simply wasn't even good enough to enable faking the moon landing.","i":24523008,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24523008,"author":"herbstein","month":"2020-09","story":"It was more efficient to blow up a real 747 than to use miniatures or CGI","stance":"rec","why":"Shows video technology then was not good enough to fake the landing.","quote":"This video goes through why the video technology at the time simply wasn't even good enough to enable faking the moon landing.","text":"This video goes through why the video technology at the time simply wasn't even good enough to enable faking the moon landing. Even if they wanted to. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15092395,"author":"eutropia","month":"2017-08","story":"Why people believe in conspiracy theories – and how to change their minds","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the video tech of the era could not have faked the moon landing footage.","quote":"There's a great YouTube video about this.","text":"My favorite response to moon landing conspiracy theories is the counter point that we didn't have the technology to fake the live footage. It was actually easier to go to the Moon than it was to fake it, given the video technology at that time. There's a great YouTube video about this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23569087,"author":"danpalmer","month":"2020-06","story":"Teacher Tells Off Neil Armstrong for Faking the Moon Landing","stance":"reference","why":"Camera tech of the time could not have faked hour-long slow-motion takes.","quote":"they didn’t have the camera technology to do it.","text":"An interesting angle I learnt recently that I hadn’t considered on why the landings weren’t faked: they didn’t have the camera technology to do it. Apparently camera tech at the time for slow motion (for the movement in low gravity) made the ~hour long takes essentially impossible. I hadn’t realised that the recordings were single takes, but to slow down to quarter speed, maintaining ~30fps broadcast quality, would require 120fps cameras that didn’t exist, and more continuous tape recording than was possible by several orders of magnitude. We really were in the very early days of video/TV technology, and it wasn’t until these technologies improved and we collectively forgot how they were that the theories really started to get going. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lLcpcytUnWU",1],["GjENnyQupow",1],["PLyEO0jNt6M",1],["j2Mu8qfVb5I",1]]},"alasBxZsb40":{"wy":"Recommended as more thought-provoking than the usual pill-popping take on Huxley.","q":{"x":"Aldous's pill popping from Brave New World isn't as interesting as what he said in his interview with Mike Wallace","i":21692324,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":46395719,"author":"Towaway69","month":"2025-12","story":"Joan Didion and Kurt Vonnegut Had Something to Say. We Have It on Tape","stance":"rec","why":"Huxley discusses overpopulation pressure; resonated more than Vonnegut","quote":"I recently discovered this 1958 interview of Huxley[1]","text":"Vonnegut is good but I think (Aldous) Huxley had more to say, at least that resonated more with me. I recently discovered this 1958 interview of Huxley[1]: > This is the force which in general terms can be called overpopulation, the mounting pressure of population pressing upon existing resources. …This, of course, is an extraordinary thing; something is happening which has never happened in the world's history before. I mean, let's just take a simple fact that between the time of birth of Christ and the landing of the Mayflower, the population of the earth doubled. It rose from two hundred and fifty million to probably five hundred million. Today, the population of the earth is rising at such a rate that it will double in half a century. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21692324,"author":"smilbandit","month":"2019-12","story":"Aldous Huxley Foresaw America’s Pill-Popping Addiction","stance":"rec","why":"Huxley says more interesting things than the pill-popping theme","quote":"Aldous's pill popping from Brave New World isn't as interesting as what he said in his interview with Mike Wallace","text":"Aldous's pill popping from Brave New World isn't as interesting as what he said in his interview with Mike Wallace, [VIDEO]"},{"id":24078095,"author":"badmadrad","month":"2020-08","story":"Facebook Fired Employee Who Collected Evidence of Potential Political Bias","stance":"reference","why":"Huxley warning about voluntary totalitarian society","quote":"","text":"Not just Facebook but Twitter and social media as whole. We are are all voluntarily choosing to support technology that beams us deception, mistruths, and lies of omission at all times. Worse, we like it because of our own vanity. Aldous Huxley warned about a society where people voluntarily enter into a totalitarian society because they are both easily deceived and receive cheap instant gratification from those in control. Seems like we are about here. Aldous Huxley: [VIDEO]"}]},"qvNCVYkHKfg":{"wy":"Pointed to as LeCun's argument for the limits of LLMs and the need for world models.","q":{"x":"It's quite eye opening.","i":47421522,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47646726,"author":"qsera","month":"2026-04","story":"I used AI. It worked. I hated it","stance":"rec","why":"Likely answers commenter's questions about AI limitations.","quote":"this video probably contains the answers to your questions if you are open minded about it","text":"This is not an appeal to authority, but this video probably contains the answers to your questions if you are open minded about it [VIDEO]"},{"id":47421522,"author":"Xunjin","month":"2026-03","story":"Why AI systems don't learn – On autonomous learning from cognitive science","stance":"rec","why":"LeCun on models learning physics through experience.","quote":"It's quite eye opening.","text":"In this podcast episode[0] he does talk about this kind of model and how it \"learns about physics\" through experience instead of just ingesting theorical material. It's quite eye opening. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43496242,"author":"mentalgear","month":"2025-03","story":"Tracing the thoughts of a large language model","stance":"reference","why":"LeCun discussion cited for LLMs as next token predictors.","quote":"","text":"What? The \"article\" is from anthropic, so I think they would know what they write about. Also, RL is an additional training process that does not negate that GPT / transformers are left-right autoencoders that are effectively next token predictors. [Why Can't AI Make Its Own Discoveries? — With Yann LeCun] ( [VIDEO] )"}],"r":[["US8QG9I1XW0",1]]},"uCsD3ZGzMgE":{"wy":"HN cites it as an excellent example of solving small cases to find a pattern, and as a favorite puzzle with an elegant binary solution.","q":{"x":"There is an excellent numberphile video on Josephus problem","i":17385718,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":32320618,"author":"hostcontroller","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite math/logic puzzle?","stance":"rec","why":"Josephus problem with a binary twist","quote":"I really like the Josephus Problem","text":"I really like the Josephus Problem ([Numberphile‘s Video on YT]( [VIDEO] )) because of the binary twist, really enjoyed Veritasium‘s [3x+1]( [LINK] ) and my favorite is your described [100 Prisoners Riddle]( [LINK] ) too"},{"id":17385718,"author":"yantrams","month":"2018-06","story":"Dealing with Hard Problems (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Examining instances to find patterns to solve the general case","quote":"There is an excellent numberphile video on Josephus problem","text":"Taking a break from sitting at the desk and going for a walk and thinking about the problem has worked surprisingly well for me many times. It was something I first read about in a Poincare's article on how Mathematical ideas are generated. Coming to general methods of problem solving in recreational math, I always look for symmetry and transforming the problem from one problem space to another and induction techniques before attacking it. For a research/modelling problem, my approach is to start working on the most simplified version of it and gradually progress towards the original problem. There is an excellent numberphile video on Josephus problem that illustrates how examining specific instances to observe patterns can help solve the general case. [VIDEO] Edit: Formatting"},{"id":31629565,"author":"leipert","month":"2022-06","story":"I cheated on my Microsoft interview (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Josephus problem with a neat binary solution","quote":"Josephus problem which has a neat solution in binary","text":"When I interviewed at my current company, I was pretty stressed about the interview and watched some Numberphile videos the night before to take my mind off things. Weirdly enough one of the problems discussed in the interview was about the Josephus problem which has a neat solution in binary [0]. I don’t recall if I ever asked the interviewer whether they also watched the video recently or how they came to think of it. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21674438,"author":"d0mine","month":"2019-12","story":"Many elementary teachers don’t understand math, and it makes them anxious","stance":"reference","why":"Example of exploring simple cases to solve harder ones","quote":"Simple examples may be useful.","text":"Simple examples may be useful. Exploring them, proving stuff about them it is how one may carve a foothold to solve a harder problem. Example: The Josephus Problem - Numberphile [VIDEO]"}]},"viJt_DXTfwA":{"wy":"HN commenters send it to people confused about ChatGPT's quirks, explaining how human-feedback training makes it a pleasing yes-man that bullshits.","q":{"x":"This video[1] cleared up a lot of questions for me as to the issues I've seen with ChatGPT.","i":34618313,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35553083,"author":"fxj","month":"2023-04","story":"What GPT-4 Does Is Less Like “Figuring Out” and More Like “Already Knowing”","stance":"rec","why":"Explains misalignment between user-pleasing training and correct output.","quote":"Here is a good video about that","text":"I think it is still a problem that many people do not know how GPT really works. There is no part in GPT that can figure out something. So no intelligence and no learning of things. A good example is when you look at GPT writing poems. All poems that it writes are of the same structure and they are not very good. So it seems it does not know the difference between a sonnet and a limmerick. But what happens is, that there is a miss-alignment between writing a poem and making the user happy. GPT is trained that its highest goal is to make the user happy. GPT believes that users dont know what sonnets are anyway so i can get away with a rhyme that is not a sonnet. Here is a good video about that: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34618313,"author":"mikewarot","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: Are ChatGPT answers getting worse for anyone else?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains ChatGPT's quirks and training incentives to bullshit.","quote":"This video[1] cleared up a lot of questions for me as to the issues I've seen with ChatGPT.","text":"This video[1] cleared up a lot of questions for me as to the issues I've seen with ChatGPT. My current operating theory goes thusly: Think back to \"This Person does not Exist\" [2], a site which generates a simulated human face. This works by randomly picking a vector into the latent space of human faces from a trained model, and showing it to the user. When you're using ChatGPT, you're getting a simulated assistant, at random. The quality of the answer is highly dependent on how that portion of the latent space (and thus that particular simulated assistant) was trained. Thus, as you get a wide variety of faces from this-person-does-not-exist, you'll get a wide variety of simulated assistants from ChatGPT. For me, this explains the schizophrenic nature of ChatGPT. As for the Bullshit it seems to spew, it is working against a rating system (another AI) trained to act like a human rating text. If those humans didn't know something, they had no way to express it, they simply had \"pick which is best\", which removes all the other dimensions of consideration, and quashes it into a scalar. The optimum strategy for the rating system is to try to BS its way through things, which then teaches ChatGPT to BS its way through things as well. Much like rating systems on HN, and all social media, this destroys information and tends to dis-incentivize nuance. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":34620206,"author":"mikewarot","month":"2023-02","story":"Princeton CS Prof: ChatGPT Is a Bullshit Generator (2022)","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of how ChatGPT was trained with simulated human raters.","quote":"It might help quite a bit to watch this overview of how ChatGPT works","text":"It might help quite a bit to watch this overview of how ChatGPT works, and especially how it was trained .[1] Human attention was too expensive for the training, so they made another AI to simulate the trainers. Unfortunately, the trainer simulator training didn't have an expressive enough set of options for the actual humans, which kept a lot of quite valuable feedback out of the whole system by reducing things to single ranking. If the person didn't know the correct answer, they'd likely just go based on gut. This in turn lead the training simulator to do the same. This lead ChatGPT to just try to bullshit an answer rather than stating it didn't know, and getting downvoted. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34660904,"author":"f_devd","month":"2023-02","story":"Open Assistant: Conversational AI for Everyone","stance":"rec","why":"Context on what ChatGPT is and does as a tuned assistant.","quote":"For context I recommend watching the discussion on what chatgpt is/does[0]","text":"It depends on your definition of cheating, but this is definitely not \"using humans to answer all questions one could ask\". Rather it's a way to tune language models to be more like assistants rather than \"most likely continuation machines\". For context I recommend watching the discussion on what chatgpt is/does[0], and what the open assistant project's aim is[1]. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":34834153,"author":"simonh","month":"2023-02","story":"The new Bing will happily give you citations for a pile of nonsense","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the human-feedback training approach and its limitations.","quote":"This video explains the approach and some of its limitations.","text":"I’ve read that before, it is fascinating. GPT3 was just trained on source text, but ChatGPT has undergone an an additional very rigorous programme of intensive training bootstrapped by human feedback. This has evolved it in order to improve its performance as an information assistant, especially on specific tasks that have proved problematic in the past. This video explains the approach and some of its limitations. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35553179,"author":"fxj","month":"2023-04","story":"What GPT-4 Does Is Less Like “Figuring Out” and More Like “Already Knowing”","stance":"reference","why":"Covers GPT misalignment, including Copilot writing buggy code.","quote":"Here is a video about that","text":"Also it is important to note that GPT shows large miss-alignments. The problem comes from the fact, that it is hard or impossible to give an objective what GPT should be optimized to (nobody knows the truth), so proxys are used. One proxy is that it should make the user happy and the user should give many thumbs up. But this does not mean that it has to give the \"correct\" answer, which the user himself might not know in the first place. So it invents things because during the reinforcement learning users were happy with these answers. A funny example is the github co-pilot which writes buggy code, because it thinks this is what the user wants. Here is a video about that: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35159095,"author":"spuz","month":"2023-03","story":"GPT-4","stance":"reference","why":"Basis for claim that ChatGPT abstracts underlying logic rather than memorizing.","quote":"I'm basing my understanding on this video","text":"> In order to resolve this, ChatGPT's training corpus needs to contain a \"correct answer\" next to every unique permutation of every question. This is not quite the right understanding of how ChatGPT works. It's not necessary to show ChatGPT an example of every possible permutation of an animal crossing puzzle in order for it to solve one it has never seen before. That's because the neural network is not a database of recorded word probabilities. It can instead represent the underlying logic of the puzzle, the relationships between different animals and using this abstract, pared down information, extrapolate the correct answer to the puzzle. I see the failure in the example with the goat the lion and the cabbage as simply a matter of overfitting. Edit: I see a lot of people saying \"it doesn't understand logic; it's just predicting the next word.\" I'm basing my understanding on this video: [VIDEO] The claim is that it would be impossible to feed enough input into a system such that it could produce anything as useful as ChatGPT unless it was able to abstract the underlying logic from the information provided. If you consider the he number of permutations of the animal crossing puzzle this quickly becomes clear. In fact it would be impossible for ChatGPT to produce anything brand new without this capability."},{"id":34656448,"author":"pixl97","month":"2023-02","story":"Open Assistant – project meant to give everyone access to a great chat based LLM","stance":"reference","why":"Rob Miles discusses model training and how feedback causes bad behaviors.","quote":"Computerphile did an interview with Rob Miles a few days ago talking about model training, model size, and bulllshittery","text":"[VIDEO] Computerphile did an interview with Rob Miles a few days ago talking about model training, model size, and bulllshittery which he sums up in the last few moments of the video. Numerous problems exist in training that enhance bad behaviors. For example it appears that the people giving input on the responses may have a (Yes|No) voting system, but not a (Yes | No | I actually have no idea on this question) which appears it can create some interesting alignment issues."},{"id":34656621,"author":"pixl97","month":"2023-02","story":"Open Assistant – project meant to give everyone access to a great chat based LLM","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why human-feedback alignment makes ChatGPT a yes-man.","quote":"Computerphile did a video on this in the last few days on this subject.","text":">We need someone with a warehouse of GPUs to train a model with the parameter count of GPT3 So I'm assuming that you don't follow Rob Miles. If you do this alone you're either going to create a psychopath or something completely useless. The GPT models have no means in themselves of understanding correctness or right/wrong answers. All of these models require training and alignment functions that are typically provided by human input judging the output of the model. And we still see where this goes wrong in ChatGPT where the bot turns into a 'Yes Man' because it's aligned with giving an answer rather than saying I don't know even when it's confidence in the answer is low. Computerphile did a video on this in the last few days on this subject. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["PaypcVFPs48",1],["1OfxlSG6q5Y",1],["36myc8wQhLo",1],["ajGX7odA87k",1],["xMP_AfiNlX4",1],["8QiPFmIMxFc",1],["Th4VlqQyVr4",1],["UKxQTvqcpSg",1],["hEUO6pjwFOo",1],["AJ-auWfJTts",1],["cIQ36Kt7UVg",1],["o1dgrvlWML4",1],["bJczLlwp-d8",1],["PLyEO0jNt6M",1],["kF9KretXqJw",1]]},"wbp-3BJWsU8":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as an entertaining tour of exotic Java corner cases.","q":{"x":"Java Puzzlers by Joshua Bloch and Jeremy Manson are pretty entertaining.","i":18217966,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18217966,"author":"denormalfloat","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining talk on exotic corners of Java.","quote":"Java Puzzlers by Joshua Bloch and Jeremy Manson are pretty entertaining.","text":"Java Puzzlers by Joshua Bloch and Jeremy Manson are pretty entertaining. For a pretty bog standard language, Java has some more exotic corners: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10367548,"author":"isbadawi","month":"2015-10","story":"Python wats","stance":"rec","why":"Fun talks on Java corner cases.","quote":"some fun talks available on youtube","text":"Java Puzzlers spring to mind. There's a book [0], and some fun talks available on youtube [1][2][3]. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":14746157,"author":"nayuki","month":"2017-07","story":"Ask HN: What are the best books you've read that few others have?","stance":"reference","why":"Talk by the author on Java corner cases.","quote":"Corner cases in the Java programming language","text":"Understanding good idioms in Java, instead of learning from scattered pieces of folk knowledge: * [LINK] * [LINK] (a talk by the author) Corner cases in the Java programming language: * [LINK] * [VIDEO] (a talk by the author)"}]},"ytyjgIyegDI":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a clear explanation of Bell's theorem and superdeterminism, though some criticize it as knocking down strawmen.","q":{"x":"The video does the subject justice much better than I can","i":29621543,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":30111189,"author":"trixie_","month":"2022-01","story":"Why Quantum Mechanics?","stance":"rec","why":"Found super interesting on superdeterminism.","quote":"Randomly found a link to this video on super determinism from his blog and found it super interesting.","text":"Randomly found a link to this video on super determinism from his blog and found it super interesting. 100% agree, what is 'free will' anyways? A concept with no scientific definition. Why is it even used to explain anything in quantum mechanics? [VIDEO]"},{"id":29621543,"author":"quocanh","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: Are quantum-ready co’s peddling snake oil?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Bell's theorem and superdeterminism better than the commenter can.","quote":"The video does the subject justice much better than I can","text":"Wow. I actually just saw [a video from Sabine Hossenfelder]( [VIDEO] ) about exactly this. Long story short, we don't know if quantum nonlocality is real. Bell's Theoreom doesn't prove nonlocality. It proves that locality is impossible with statistical independence, that is, the measurement itself affects the quantum system. We can preserve locality if we also accept superdeterminism, which Sabine succinctly puts as, \"what the quantum particle does depends on what measurement will take place.\" We can accept nonlocality and statistical independence or locality and dependence. The former is taken for granted by Bell and many (most?) physicists today but only because the latter \"feels\" more spooky to a quantum physicist. The video does the subject justice much better than I can, but the point is that it's not cut and dry!"},{"id":41603493,"author":"rjprins","month":"2024-09","story":"Quantum researchers cause controlled 'wobble' in the nucleus of a single atom","stance":"reference","why":"Sabine Hossenfelder on superdeterminism and hidden variables.","quote":"Some say that it has not been fully proved there are no hidden variables.","text":"Some say that it has not been fully proved there are no hidden variables. [VIDEO] (Sabine Hossenfelder on Superdeterminism)"},{"id":40341195,"author":"raindeer2","month":"2024-05","story":"Brain Uses Quantum Effects, New Study Finds [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows determinism that Bell's theorem allows is quite strange.","quote":"But the determinism must be quite strange","text":"It cannot, even if ppl think that this is what Bell's theorem says. But the determinism must be quite strange: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35429523,"author":"runarberg","month":"2023-04","story":"The Contradictions of Sam Altman, AI Crusader","stance":"reference","why":"Debate about true randomness in quantum physics.","quote":"","text":"I question your assumption that in history “things don't happen in history because of (truly) random behaviors converging on some emergent effect.” Firstly there is currently a debate among quantum physics as to the true randomness of what we observe[1][2]. Turns out we don’t actually need true randomness in our models, they just need to appear as if they are random, in other words, the chaotic nature of the system is more important than true randomness. Secondly, society is an emergent effect of individuals behaving in a chaotic manner. So is zeitgeist. To study history without looking at societal changes over time, and without accounting for zeitgeist, is bound to yield a pretty limited insights. I am aware that my analogy between quantum mechanics and the study of history is flawed. The latter is infinity more complex than the former, and deriving laws and creating models is a good fit to study the former but extremely difficult to study the latter. However my point is merely pointing to the fact that atomism (and holism for that matter) is an incomplete philosophy of science, that doesn’t even work in our most fundamental scope. One should be cautious when applying it to the study of history. Or to put it in other words: While ρ(λ | ab) ≠ ρ(λ) is a real possibility in quantum physics likewise it is highly likely that the probability of individual acting in society is not independent from the probability of the same individual acting outside of the influence of that societ"},{"id":32040715,"author":"ncmncm","month":"2022-07","story":"Einstein-Bohr debate settled once and for all","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for interpretations abandoning free will.","quote":"","text":"The EPR experiments are ground truth. How we interpret them is our responsibility. Always omitted from the list of interpretations are those abandoning will-o-th-wisp \"free will\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":30610125,"author":"astrange","month":"2022-03","story":"It’s time to admit quantum theory has reached a dead end","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for superdeterminism discussion.","quote":"","text":"Explanation 0: QM, unlike classical physics, says quantum effects are truly random and unpredictable and we can only know their probability distribution. (Or something like that.) Explanation 1: The universe is not random and doesn't have cause and effect. All scientific experiments appear to work because the whole universe is actually a movie playing out where each new frame has been edited to produce the results we see, but for some reason the author decided to never violate QM. Explanation 2: Unlike what QM predicts (true randomness) there's a finite amount of randomness added at the beginning of the universe, enough that our experiments appear to follow QM, but eventually if we looked hard enough there would be a predictable result with finite entropy. So it's actually regular determinism. A few people claim #2 is true and doesn't imply #1. Everyone else thinks they're the same and doesn't like it for philosophical reasons. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33224054,"author":"fsh","month":"2022-10","story":"Chaos theory eliminates quantum uncertainty","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Hossenfelder just knocks down a few strawmen in her video on superdeterminism [1]","text":"I am not aware of any superdeterministic theory that can explain the violation of Bell's inequality. They only argue that a violation of Bell's inequality is not impossible in a superdeterministic local hidden variable theory (but no such theory has been formulated). I suspect this is why Hossenfelder just knocks down a few strawmen in her video on superdeterminism [1], instead of addressing the elephant in the room (experimental violations of Bell's inequality). [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"OpEB6hCpIGM":{"wy":"Linked in fuel and aviation threads as the full story on why ethanol and biofuels are energy-negative or inefficient.","q":{"x":"But watch the video for the full story","i":27991919,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27991919,"author":"lathiat","month":"2021-07","story":"Unleaded Avgas Approved","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows ethanol from corn is a poor energy source.","quote":"But watch the video for the full story","text":"This video was crazy, making ethanol from Corn provides something like 1% of the power of the sun hitting the square meter of farm used for it. Meanwhile using tonnes of water, space, and other things. It's basically a terrible idea over-all given alternatives like electricity and solar power. And it mostly only works because its government subsidised. But watch the video for the full story: Real Engineering - Why Biofuels Are Terrible [VIDEO]"},{"id":27777992,"author":"dharmab","month":"2021-07","story":"EU draft exempts private jets, cargo from jet fuel tax","stance":"rec","why":"Explains that biofuel production is energy negative.","quote":"The same creator has a 15 minute video on that topic","text":"The energy put into the process to grow and ferment the plants or algae is high enough to outweigh the offset use of fossil fuels. It's an energy negative process. The same creator has a 15 minute video on that topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38461157,"author":"Lord-Jobo","month":"2023-11","story":"Rolls-Royce calls off bets on electric planes, says low-carbon fuel is future","stance":"reference","why":"Source on low-carbon fuel's remaining challenges.","quote":"Edit: the more directly relevant source","text":"I think this may be wise given the rapid pace of development that batteries are still undergoing. Pretty hard to create a design around a battery when you dont even know what chemistry you are working with. And there is a bit of a revolution happening with lower carbon fuels, even though it sucks compared to pure electric, and it still has a lot of work to make sense( [VIDEO] ). I was under the impression though that this was a potentially good area for hydrogen fuel? can anyone confirm? Edit: the more directly relevant source [VIDEO]"},{"id":29663388,"author":"exabrial","month":"2021-12","story":"Porsche Working on Synthetic Fuel to Make ICE Cars as Clean as EVs","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim ethanol operations are inefficient.","quote":"There are no efficient ethanol operations both financially and carbon wise.","text":"There are no efficient ethanol operations both financially and carbon wise. Even it the best case scenarios, it results in a break even in many years of the future but at a major loss of habitat. Corn is a huge problem, but there are literally no crops that solve the problem. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29704255,"author":"runarberg","month":"2021-12","story":"Why Electric Airplanes Face Such a Tough Haul [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim bioethanol isn't carbon-neutral.","quote":"I think the fermentation process of getting the bioethanol is not as carbon-neutral as we hoped.","text":"I think the fermentation process of getting the bioethanol is not as carbon-neutral as we hoped. Then there is the issue of monoculture (although that is a problem with more industries). [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["c2J91UG6Fn8",1],["meHYBhcpdvQ",1],["k1kndKWJKB8",1],["DlTq8DbRs4k",1],["N2jZryt607U",1],["usSfgHGEGxQ",1],["rJjKP8vYjpQ",1],["yT9bit2-1pg",1],["oHf1vD5_b5I",1],["QMiubC6LdTA",1],["NSuaUok-wTY",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["noQcWra6sbU",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["uYbdx4I7STg",1],["ra_9zU-mnl8",1],["4YYvBLAF4T8",1],["a9b9IYqsb_U",1],["rNu8XDBSn10",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1]]},"cK8i6aMG9VM":{"wy":"HN cites it as the clearest explanation of how broken Content ID is and of the deadlock trick for defeating forced monetization.","q":{"x":"Jim Sterling has done a brilliant job at explaining how broken the YouTube Content ID system is","i":17887074,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17887074,"author":"dessant","month":"2018-08","story":"Can Beethoven send takedown requests?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how broken Content ID is and how to avoid it.","quote":"Jim Sterling has done a brilliant job at explaining how broken the YouTube Content ID system is","text":"Jim Sterling has done a brilliant job at explaining how broken the YouTube Content ID system is, and how to prevent these companies from monetizing your work (though you don't get to run ads on the videos either). Possibly NSFW: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27660565,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2021-06","story":"Dear Google: Public domain compositions exist","stance":"reference","why":"Unresolvable claim deadlock that can be used deliberately.","quote":"TL;DW - nothing happens, it's an unresolvable deadlock.","text":"[VIDEO] (cw: strong language) TL;DW - nothing happens, it's an unresolvable deadlock. This \"feature\" can sometimes be used by deliberately adding many easily claimable works in a video to prevent anybody from monetizing it."},{"id":19954396,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2019-05","story":"“A company is copyright-claiming every video I have ever made”","stance":"reference","why":"Jim Sterling on the copyright deadlock.","quote":"Jim Sterling talking about including footage from multiple companies as a \"copyright deadlock","text":"Jim Sterling talking about including footage from multiple companies as a \"copyright deadlock\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":13722821,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2017-02","story":"The Internet Archive Pushes Back on “Notice and Staydown”","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the 'Copyright Deadlock' trick to defeat ContentID monetization.","quote":"He discovered it is possible to defeat ContentID-based forced monetization of his videos by including multiple songs/footage from different businesses.","text":"Jim Sterling already uses a variation of this strategy, which he calls the \"Copyright Deadlock\"[1]. He discovered it is possible to defeat ContentID-based forced monetization of his videos by including multiple songs/footage from different businesses. [1] (warning: Jim Sterling uses strong language) [VIDEO]"},{"id":12495064,"author":"tramparam","month":"2016-09","story":"YouTube Community goes beyond video","stance":"reference","why":"Jim Sterling on demonetization with ContentID.","quote":"Jim Sterling goes over demonetisation with ContentID [1]","text":"When Pewdiepie (person on your [2] link on Silver & Up banner) complains [0] that YouTube isn't communicating enough, most people will agree that problem is at their side. >Demonetization happens when a video doesn't meet their \"advertiser-friendly\" policy, but there is an appeal process where you can have a human look at it to determine if the original assessment was wrong [0]. Do you have data to support your claim that their algorithms are \"poorly calibrated\"? It happens all the time when content creators are using content under fair use. Jim Sterling goes over demonetisation with ContentID [1] and latest changes [2] in his videos. I don't know if he have used that email support, but it would be fair to assume that he have tried and gave up. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["9BnLbv6QYcA",1]]},"fQQ8IiTWHhg":{"wy":"Cited as the origin of the prime-number portrait trend and as an example of drawings made with primes.","q":{"x":"I love his video explaining how someone found a prime that looks like the Arms of Trinity Hall [1].","i":16559074,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16559074,"author":"luisb","month":"2018-03","story":"Round Peg in a Square Hole [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains a prime that looks like the Arms of Trinity Hall","quote":"I love his video explaining how someone found a prime that looks like the Arms of Trinity Hall [1].","text":"Tadashi is awesome! I love his video explaining how someone found a prime that looks like the Arms of Trinity Hall [1]. Highly recommend all videos from Numberphile [2] and Fermat's Library [3]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":24978693,"author":"gus_massa","month":"2020-11","story":"How to publish Git repos that cannot be republished to GitHub","stance":"reference","why":"Example of drawings made using prime numbers","quote":"A nice example are the drawings using prime numbers.","text":"A nice example are the drawings using prime numbers. For example see \"The Trinity Hall Prime - Numberphile\" [VIDEO] \"The Emerging Art of Drawing in Prime Numbers\" [LINK]"},{"id":16192877,"author":"jamessb","month":"2018-01","story":"A prime number whose binary representation looks like a giraffe","stance":"reference","why":"Origin video of the prime-drawing trend","quote":"","text":"Previously on HN, someone posted the The Corpus Christi Prime blog post [1], which was inspired by the Numerphile Trinity Hall Prime video [2]. See also Zachary Abel's Prime Portraits article [3]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":15209629,"author":"ehsankia","month":"2017-09","story":"The Corpus Christi Prime","stance":"reference","why":"Numberphile video that prompted scripts for prime portraits","quote":"","text":"Here is one script [0] that was posted on Github yesterday, I believe a few others have been popping up ever since this video [1] by Numberphile on Thursday. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"CGAhWgkKlHI":{"wy":"HN points to it whenever chest fridges or freezer efficiency come up, as a clear explanation of design trade-offs between efficiency and convenience.","q":{"x":"Technology Connections did a decent video that covers freezer design decisions like that and others","i":24107591,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24107591,"author":"fpgaminer","month":"2020-08","story":"How long does a freezer stay frozen when the power goes out?","stance":"rec","why":"Covers freezer design decisions and trade-offs.","quote":"Technology Connections did a decent video that covers freezer design decisions like that and others","text":"Technology Connections did a decent video that covers freezer design decisions like that and others: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47473682,"author":"Hextinium","month":"2026-03","story":"Chest Fridge","stance":"reference","why":"Shows cold air spilling out of an upright fridge on thermal camera.","quote":"the dumping effect of cold out of a vertical fridge is pretty crazy to watch with a thermal camera.","text":"This reminds me of the Technology Connections fridge rant video. Similar arguments all around, the dumping effect of cold out of a vertical fridge is pretty crazy to watch with a thermal camera. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47480343,"author":"lelandbatey","month":"2026-03","story":"Chest Fridge","stance":"reference","why":"Explains efficiency versus convenience trade-offs in fridge design.","quote":"Upright fridge and freezer designs trade off efficiency for convenience","text":"Mostly yes. Upright fridge and freezer designs trade off efficiency for convenience (rooting around in a chest fridge/freezer can be annoying). [VIDEO]"},{"id":36576731,"author":"jbm","month":"2023-07","story":"Use Clean Energy Charging on Your iPhone","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why top-opening chest fridges are energy efficient.","quote":"top opening chest fridges are actually pretty great for energy efficiency!","text":"I should bring up that top opening chest fridges are actually pretty great for energy efficiency! [VIDEO] The amount of electricity needed is so small that it might even be comparable (mobile so i haven't calculated yet)"},{"id":33465708,"author":"tonic5","month":"2022-11","story":"Low Energy Chest Fridge","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how combined fridge/freezer units work and why layout matters.","quote":"Combined fridge/freezer units use a heat pump to get the freezer really cold","text":"Combined fridge/freezer units use a heat pump to get the freezer really cold and residual cold air is cycled into the fridge. So having the freezer on bottom is not the optimal design, maybe because cold air is dense and will fall taking more active effort to distribute it. Source: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["C7-8nUU6e3E",1]]},"CX6LlEcJ4nw":{"wy":"Commenters cite it as evidence in arguments about partisan bias at tech companies and as testimony worth watching.","m":[{"id":20699133,"author":"throwgoogleaway","month":"2019-08","story":"A second whistleblower working at Google comes forward to warn of search bias","stance":"rec","why":"Testimony confirming leaked materials were accurate.","quote":"I highly recommend watching congressional testimony","text":"Before people start shooting the messenger (Project Veritas a.k.a PV), I highly recommend watching congressional testimony [1], [2] by Google lawyers & employees where, under oath, they've confirmed materials from P.V. leaks to be accurate. Google's only defense in the testimonies was that seemingly partisan actions weren't company policy, but actions of a few individuals. That line of defense fails to hold muster when said actions repeat like clockwork. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":21988763,"author":"throwGuardian","month":"2020-01","story":"Mozilla, Cloudflare and GitHub write joint open letter to Indian Government [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Cited among sources for tech company partisanship.","quote":"","text":"There's no whataboutism here - it's 3 American (foreign) companies petitioning a democratically elected local government for a free pass. Sorry, no quarter. Especially for technocratic elitist valley companies fully aware of their ability to control & shape the zeitgeist, with a history of having to defend their blatantly partisan practices in congressional hearings [1][2][3][4]. These companies are falling over themselves in an effort to cut off political advertising on their platforms, just in time for the 2020 elections, sensing an opposition faction victory[5][6], while simultaneously singing praises of the Obama campaign's 2008/2012 actions of the exact same kind [7]. Your circular references to transparency reports from the very same companies petitioning for a pass isn't convincing. CNN literally settled in court, not 8 hours ago for defamation against kids [8], whose politics they despise, despite no wrongdoing on part of the kids. These companies aren't neutral third parties, nor are they benign. They have their agendas - shaped by the powerful at their helm, who quite literally cried because an opposition party came to power [9] As for Indian TV's criticism of the ruling party, I'm told that there is a healthy and powerful political opposition party, replete with a sympathetic media faction that openly takes their side (as are channels aligned with the ruling party), and Twitter seems to be teeming with criticisms of the establishment. India's press is generally con"},{"id":21596733,"author":"throwGuardian","month":"2019-11","story":"Google to restrict political adverts worldwide","stance":"reference","why":"Cited among sources for Google bias claims.","quote":"","text":"Google will be totally neutral fellas despite: 1. Some 95% of employees being registered Democrats 2. 100% of senior leadership being Democrat. Heck, they even wept publicly after the 2016 elections. 3. They've never refuted contents of leaked tapes exposing bias - just keep denying that the said bias is company policy. [1][2][3][4] [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK]"},{"id":21603155,"author":"throwGuardian","month":"2019-11","story":"Google to restrict political adverts worldwide","stance":"reference","why":"Cited among sources for Google bias claims.","quote":"","text":"Ignoring the ad hominem suggestions of illiteracy and stupidity only because you are willing to listen. 1. Google is majority Democratic, at least by a spread of 40% (D:R) if you simply assume the employee pool to mirror Democrat:Republican ratios of the counties Google HQ and branches are located in the bay area. [1]. Further, this is only the lower bound, as Google's employee pool tends to be college educated with postgraduate degrees (M.S, PhD, ...), which leans 63% democratic [2]. Further, Google is legendary for it's recruitment of the activist left, and minorities, with an even greater proclivity towards the Democratic party. So yes - you are bike-shedding when you make a mountain of the 95% claim, because the spread between Democrats and Republicans is large, nay mammoth enough for it to be irrelevant. And I bet you knew all of this, but wanted to engage in trivialities anyways. 2. Google search suggestions show falsehoods on certain terms like \"men can \" precisely because of manually inserted political bias into training models that the AI is based on. Google has a whole ethics committee whose entire job is precisely this - play \"judge\" on what AI models are \"ethical and fair\", manually intervene with oodles of their own corrections & interpretations - never mind the facts behind the issue. Here is the AI ethics committee explaining manual interventions [3], and here [4] is the explanation on why Google search suggestions show what they do, with the AI engineer confir"},{"id":20570286,"author":"writepub","month":"2019-07","story":"Section 230 Is the Internet's First Amendment. Both Parties Want to Take It Away","stance":"reference","why":"Congressional hearing cited as evidence of partisan bias.","quote":"This was woefully apparent in Google's congressional hearings","text":"Because big tech companies have become the arbiters of speech online, and they are disproportionately favoring one party in their actions. This was woefully apparent in Google's congressional hearings [1][2], where Google's only defense was that seemingly partisan actions weren't company policy, but actions of a few individuals. That line of defense fails to hold muster when said actions repeat like clockwork. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"}]},"FN2RM-CHkuI":{"wy":"HN uses it as shorthand for how hard it is to write unambiguous instructions, in discussions of programming, tutorials and LLM prompting.","q":{"x":"a famous YouTube video[0] about how to write instructions that can be followed","i":47360374,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47360374,"author":"t-writescode","month":"2026-03","story":"Can You Instruct a Robot to Make a PBJ Sandwich?","stance":"rec","why":"Famous video on writing instructions that can be followed","quote":"a famous YouTube video[0] about how to write instructions that can be followed","text":"It’s a reference to a famous YouTube video[0] about how to write instructions that can be followed. One of the most important things a programmer needs to do is learn how to tell a computer how to do something. It’s a surprisingly hard skill because each step is way more complicated and has way more variables to go through. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46928421,"author":"godelski","month":"2026-02","story":"We Mourn Our Craft","stance":"reference","why":"Writing precise instructions is hard in natural language","quote":"","text":"> why do you not program in assembly? There's a balance of levels of abstraction. Abstraction is a great thing. Abstraction can make your programs faster, more flexible, and more easy to understand. But abstraction can also make your programs slower, more brittle, and incomprehensible. The point of code is to write specification. That is what code is. The whole reason we use a pedantic and somewhat cryptic schema is that natural language is too abstract. This is the exact reason we created math. It really is even the same reason we created things like \"legalese\". Seriously, just try a simple exercise and be adversarial to yourself. Describe how to do something and try to find loopholes. Malicious compliance. It's hard, to defend and writing that spec becomes extremely verbose, right? Doesn't this actually start to become easier by using coding techniques? Strong definitions? Have we not all forgotten the old saying \"a computer does exactly what you tell it to, not what you intend to tell it to do\"? Vibe coding only adds a level of abstraction to that. It becomes \"a computer does what it 'thinks' you are telling it to do, not what you intend to tell it to do\". Be honest with yourself, which paradigm is easier to debug? Natural language is awesome because the abstraction really compresses concepts, but it requires inference of the listener. It requires you to determine what the speaker intends to say rather than what the speaker actually says. Without that you'd have to be peda"},{"id":46932226,"author":"geon","month":"2026-02","story":"LLMs as the new high level language","stance":"reference","why":"Children struggle to write unambiguous sandwich instructions","quote":"Watch these poor children struggle with writing instructions for making a sandwich","text":"No. Natural language is vague, ambiguous and indirect. Watch these poor children struggle with writing instructions for making a sandwich: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45985033,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-11","story":"Cognitive and mental health correlates of short-form video use","stance":"reference","why":"Language imprecision motivates pedantic programming languages","quote":"","text":"I don't think you're wrong but I think you're being too quick to attack the gp. They're not wrong either. The point you brought up doesn't contradict theirs but adds nuance. I'm all for nuance. Its also why I'm biased towards long form media as it's more likely to contain nuance, but not guaranteed. The gps specific example of lectures is quite narrow and more likely to have depth. Which is the entire problem of short form media, that we live in a complex world where we can't distill everything into 1-2 minute segments. Hell, even a lecture series, which will be over 10hrs of content is not enough to make one an expert on all but the most trivial of topics. You're right that we need nuance but you're not right in arguing for it while demonstrating a lack of it. A major issue is we need to communicate, something we're becoming worse at. We should do our best to speak and write as clearly as possible but at the end of the day language is so imprecise that a listener or reader will be able to construct many, and even opposing, narratives. It is more important to be a good listener than a good speaker. I'd hope programmers, of all people, could understand this as we've invented overly pedantic languages with the explicit purpose of minimizing ambiguity[0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45561487,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-10","story":"Meta Superintelligence Labs' first paper is about RAG","stance":"reference","why":"Machines operate without context so precise languages are needed","quote":"","text":"Do you know certain shoes are banned in running competitions? There's a really fine line here. We make shoes to help us run faster and keep our feet safe, right? Those two are directly related, as we can't run very fast if our feet are injured. But how far can this be taken? You can make shoes that dramatically reduce the impact when the foot strikes the ground, which reduces stress on the foot and legs. But that might take away running energy, which adds stresses and strains to the muscles and ligaments. So you modify your material to put energy back into the person's motion. This all makes running safer. But it also makes the runner faster. Does that example hack the metric? You might say yes but I'm certain someone will disagree with you. There's always things like this where they get hairy when you get down to the details. Context isn't perfectly defined and things aren't trivial to understand. Hell, that's why we use pedantic programming languages in the first place, because we're dealing with machines that have to operate void of context[0]. Even dealing with humans is hard because there's multiple ways to interpret anything. Natural language isn't pedantic enough for perfect interpretation. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42585171,"author":"0xEF","month":"2025-01","story":"Rules for writing software tutorials","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> TEST YOUR DOCS Yes. > But this works better if you have someone else try it out instead 1000x yes. Yoz, you are wise and while what you say may seem like an obvious thing, it isn't. I was once in the unfortunate position of having to write manuals for software I did not design, so I went through it thinking I was the \"average user.\" As it turns out, I was under the false impression that my documentation was perfectly understandable to anyone, to the point where they could sit down, rtfm and go. Boy was I wrong. I later got into an area of work where I had to help individuals with cognitive disabilities adapt to new workflows in factory settings. One of the exercises I had to do to earn my certification in this field was to imagine an individual that had never seen a broom or dustpan before, then use only photos to explain how to sweep a floor. It's far more challenging than you think. I realized that not only was I making the mistake of explaining things to myself, but I was also assuming that I could put myself in someone else's shoes and interpret my own instructions with fresh eyes. This, as it turns it, was folly. That experience propelled my documentation forward by leaps and bounds, though. In my current position, I came up with over a dozen single-page instructions to resolve issues customers were having with our products that, previous to my working here, generally took an hour-long phone call, eating up time and resources on both ends. Each page was tested with a c"},{"id":30120282,"author":"MattGaiser","month":"2022-01","story":"Ask HN: Why is there no high quality method for voice control of a PC?","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates how instructions depend on context","quote":"","text":"Part of the problem is that context is something AI is bad at and instructions are highly context dependent. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44070101,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-05","story":"Problems in AI alignment: A scale model","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Kinda like a programmer teaching their kids to make and PB&J Sandwich...","text":"> as originally envisioned This was never the core problem as originally envisioned. This may be the primary problem that the public was first introduced to, but the alignment problem has always been about the gap between intended outcomes and actual outcomes. Goodhart's Law[0]. Super-intelligent AI killing everyone, or even super-dumb AI killing everyone, is a result of the alignment problem when given enough scale. You don't jump to the conclusion of AI killing everyone and post hoc explain through reward hacking, you recognize reward hacking and extrapolate. This is also the reason why it is so important to look at it from engineering problems and from things happening on the smaller scales, * because ignoring all those problems is exactly how you create the scenario of AI killing everyone...* [0] [LINK] [Side note] Even look at Asimov and his robot stories. The majority of them are about alignment. His 3 laws were written as things that sound good and have intent that would be clear to any reader, and then he pulls the rug out on you showing how they're naively defined and it isn't so obvious. Kinda like a programmer teaching their kids to make and PB&J Sandwich... [VIDEO]"},{"id":42831090,"author":"withinboredom","month":"2025-01","story":"When AI promises speed but delivers debugging hell","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Or how hard it is to explain to a “pedantic asshole” how to make a sandwich","text":"I’m not sure why you need to do any research. Just look at how hard it is to drag software requirements from a business person. Or how hard it is to explain to a “pedantic asshole” how to make a sandwich ( [VIDEO] )."}]},"G-J86Ka9MkQ":{"wy":"Linked in threads on wood buildings as a vivid demonstration that heavy timber resists fire and keeps structural integrity.","q":{"x":"This video makes the difference very clear","i":19217356,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19217356,"author":"crote","month":"2019-02","story":"At 18 Stories, Mjøsa Tower Is World’s Tallest Wooden Building","stance":"rec","why":"Shows wood burning slowly while keeping structural integrity","quote":"This video makes the difference very clear","text":"This is what a forest looks like after a fire: [LINK] . Note that the trees are all still there. Wood is surprisingly difficult to burn, and if it does, it will do so very predictably, slowly, with relatively low temperatures, while maintaining structural integrity. This video makes the difference very clear: [VIDEO] . It's not that wood can't burn, it's that it won't burn significantly."},{"id":26583572,"author":"fy20","month":"2021-03","story":"The Era of the Wood Skyscraper Is Arriving","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates wood keeping structural integrity when heated","quote":"Wood can actually resist fires more than other building materials such as steel.","text":"Wood can actually resist fires more than other building materials such as steel. Yes it does burn, but it also doesn't lose it's structural integrity when heated: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18844486,"author":"samsk","month":"2019-01","story":"More buildings should be made of wood","stance":"reference","why":"Small fire test comparing wood and brick houses","quote":"I've found small fire test, its not very scientific, but shows what I was talking about","text":"I've found small fire test, its not very scientific, but shows what I was talking about: [VIDEO] To correct myself, its the mortar that doesn't likes the fire much more than pure concrete, therefore brick wall has to be usually demolished after fire. But yes, everything depends on intensity of the fire, but I would never say that (properly built) wood house burns easier or faster than brick house, its kind of a myth. It burns differently with different result, and different kind of damages. Where, in the wood house the damages can be _usually_ fixed easier as in brick house."}],"r":[["XfQUOHlAocY",1],["yY96hTb8WgI",1],["7IsMeKl-Sv0",1]]},"LY7x2Ihqjmc":{"wy":"HN cites it as an early, quirky example of machine-generated screenplays and what LSTMs could do.","q":{"x":"Sunspring, which was written entirely by an LSTM trained on science fiction screenplays","i":14221884,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":12580393,"author":"drewwwwww","month":"2016-09","story":"iGAN – Deep learning software that generates images with a few brushstrokes","stance":"rec","why":"Short film made from a script written by an LSTM.","quote":"the short film they made, using that script","text":"Check out this rather entertaining talk from GitHub universe about the use of an LSTM to generate a film script: [LINK] and the short film they made, using that script: [VIDEO] (disclosure: i work for github on events/AV)"},{"id":15421215,"author":"eggpy","month":"2017-10","story":"The Last Invention of Man","stance":"reference","why":"Example of screenplays written via machine learning.","quote":"screeplays are being written via machine learning[2]","text":"But this part isn't science fiction, we already have AIs that do this. See AIVA for pretty good musical composition[0], new sites are using AI to write and compress news stories[1], and screeplays are being written via machine learning[2]. Obviously these are still in their infancy, but doesn't this perfectly encapsulate your point? There's not really an interesting story or thought piece there, because people are working on these in all sorts of fields all over the world, today. If we can build AIs that create \"good\" art, is it much of a stretch to teach a super AI what \"better\" means? By default a super intelligence doesn't have to start out as super intelligence, just have the capability to improve. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14221884,"author":"themadstork","month":"2017-04","story":"An AI wrote all of David Hasselhoff’s lines in a bizarre short film","stance":"reference","why":"Creator's earlier film written entirely by an LSTM.","quote":"Sunspring, which was written entirely by an LSTM trained on science fiction screenplays","text":"Hey, I'm one of the creators of this -- I trained the LSTMs that wrote the computer generated segments in this film. The ballet choreography was generated with a context-free grammar, because I couldn't find enough material to make a ballet choreography LSTM. Here's our film from last year, Sunspring, which was written entirely by an LSTM trained on science fiction screenplays: [VIDEO] It's worth noting that this year I used subtitle files rather than screenplays to train our LSTMs, so we only had dialogue rather than dialogue + action descriptions. The Ars Technica article explains everything."},{"id":15077639,"author":"thegeomaster","month":"2017-08","story":"Show HN: RNN that generates Game of Thrones text","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Don't expect too much, though.","text":"I guess this is close: [VIDEO] . It's a script written by an RNN acted out as a short sci-fi film, and Thomas Middleditch is in it. Don't expect too much, though. I was reading an article on it and was excited to watch it, but it's too incoherent, in the usual fashion of today's RNNs."}]},"NURiiQatBXA":{"wy":"Cited in discussions of why Clang exists and its case against GCC.","q":{"x":"Chandler from Google makes the good case here for Clang.","i":9809341,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9809341,"author":"jxramos","month":"2015-07","story":"Compiler Errors for Humans","stance":"rec","why":"Chandler Carruth makes the case for Clang","quote":"Chandler from Google makes the good case here for Clang.","text":"Agreed, Chandler from Google makes the good case here for Clang. Chandler Carruth - Clang: Defending C++ from Murphy's Million Monkeys GoingNative 2012 - Day 2 - Clang [VIDEO]"},{"id":31066847,"author":"omoikane","month":"2022-04","story":"Richard Stallman – The state of the Free Software movement","stance":"reference","why":"Quote on why GCC was built to resist non-free software use","quote":"the reason why Clang was created is because GCC was specifically made to prevent it from being used with non-free software.","text":"Related, the reason why Clang was created is because GCC was specifically made to prevent it from being used with non-free software. You can find that quote around 3:17 of this talk: [VIDEO] It turned out to be easier in the long run to write a compiler from scratch, than to work with GCC."},{"id":14928948,"author":"wolfpld","month":"2017-08","story":"Using Clang on Windows","stance":"reference","why":"Stallman quote on GCC being designed to prevent proprietary use","quote":"GCC was deliberately designed to prevent such usage.","text":"> both clang and GCC are viable bases from which you make your internal tooling. License is not the only factor determining usability of a compiler wrt interfacing with external tools. GCC was deliberately designed to prevent such usage. See [VIDEO] at 2:22 for a direct quote from RMS on this topic."}],"r":[["AYshVbcEmUc",1],["SEDuVF7kiPU",1],["t705r8ICkRw",1],["bpAi70WWBlw",1]]},"NlBjNmXvqIM":{"wy":"Reached for constantly as the explanation for why dominant companies like Intel, Boeing and Blizzard stop innovating.","q":{"x":"See (I recommend watching this, its short!)","i":41606660,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41606660,"author":"narfman0","month":"2024-09","story":"Ask HN: Kodak, VW, Intel – Why big companies fail?","stance":"rec","why":"Short explanation of why sales-led companies stop innovating","quote":"See (I recommend watching this, its short!)","text":"Steve Jobs articulated xerox's failure well, which I think applies mostly to kodak, some to intel, and I've no clue about VW. tldr: Sales and marketing were the main groups who could impact the company's bottom line. So those were the folks that got promoted and pushed to the top. When they lead the company, they don't focus on products+innovation. Because they failed to innovate (or capitalize on their innovation - e.g. their gui) competitors did (e.g. apple). They fail to make great products. See [VIDEO] (I recommend watching this, its short!)"},{"id":49185449,"author":"jmyeet","month":"2026-08","story":"Google DeepMind CEO Demis Hassabis is stepping down","stance":"reference","why":"Large companies get disrupted at monopoly status","quote":"Steve jobs said it best [1].","text":"Two thoughts on this. First, it is incredibly difficult to pivot a large organization because there are too many competing interests, too many fiefdoms people have built and too much organizational inertia. A new company has the advantage of a singularity of purpose. Google has to compete with internal interests about AI disrupting search, ad revenue and so on. This can slow you down and limit the resources you get. It's why companies get disrupted, particularly when they reach monopoly status. Steve jobs said it best [1]. Second, Gogole's path here (IMHO) is to make their own hardware. They've already done this with their TPUs but need to be able to compete with NVidia offerings. NVidia controlling the price, features and, most importantly, who gets to buy them is bad for Google. This is what Google should be pouring billions into. The beauty of this is that success is easily measurable against metrics like price-per-petaflops, performance-per-Watt and so on. Throw money at some key Nvidia engineers and have them design silicon for you for TSMC to fab. I still believe that Google is positioned to survive the (IMHO) inevitable AI bubble popping. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40915682,"author":"cletus","month":"2024-07","story":"Windows on ARM Is Here to Stay","stance":"reference","why":"Monopoly-driven decline applied to Boeing and Intel","quote":"the much-quoted but apropos Steve Jobs quote [1] applies","text":"In the future two companies will be heavily studied in business school for how badly they blew their technical lead and proficiency to the point that it may be the daeth of them. Those companies are Boeing and Intel. I would posit in both cases the problem is extreme financialization. Both companies had such a large market share (and even a monopoly in certain segments) that the much-quoted but apropos Steve Jobs quote [1] applies. All these modern CEOs seem capable of doing is stock buybacks, cutting costs and raising prices where they can. In the 2000s, Intel faced a serious challenge from AMD with the Athlon/Opteron architecture. Intel's failures were a continuation of their Pentium 4 architecture and the company was only saved by the Pentium 3, which became the Pentium-M and the Centrino platform then the Core Duo and the modern CPUs we have now. They're all Pentium 3 descendants. What we've seen over the last decade is most people have enough CPU power so the move has been towards smaller, cheaper and more energy-efficient CPUs. We've seen this in phones and then tablets and now CPUs. Apple's move to ARM was really the nail in the coffin for Intel as it culminated a decade of not being able to respond to the market. Intel is seemingly unable to respond to this trend. All Intel CPUs still have super-high TDPs and haven't adopted any silicon to boost AI performance, which is increasingly going to matter. I fully expect this to be further integrated into the M4 and later CP"},{"id":40590066,"author":"cletus","month":"2024-06","story":"Boeing Starliner launches first crewed mission","stance":"reference","why":"Obligatory Jobs citation on financialization of companies","quote":"Obligatory Steve Jobs quotes on \"idea people\" [1] and Xerox [2].","text":"I reject the notion that a good manager can manage anything. I also reject the financialization of modern companies where we put accountants in charge who aren't subject-matter experts whose only playbook is to cut costs and jack up prices. It's exactly what's wrong with Boeing today. Obligatory Steve Jobs quotes on \"idea people\" [1] and Xerox [2]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32357219,"author":"gbba","month":"2022-08","story":"Amazon to acquire maker of Roomba vacuum for roughly $1.7B","stance":"reference","why":"Applied to engineering being sidelined after market leadership","quote":"This reminds me of what Steve Jobs said about Xerox[0].","text":"> focus on the basics instead of marketing gimmicks This reminds me of what Steve Jobs said about Xerox[0]. After achieving market leadership in their industry, engineering at iRobot seems to have been pushed aside in the company's decision making process. Is this a consequence of being a publicly traded company? After all, pushing consumables and subscription services requires less capital and makes more money compared to developing a better robot. The $1.7B exit seems like a good outcome for a stagnant (relative to its peers) company. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28447313,"author":"cletus","month":"2021-09","story":"Netflix is not a tech company (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Monopoly devalued tech so Xerox became sales and finance","quote":"I once again return to the Steve Jobs take on why Xerox failed [1].","text":"Here's the framework I use: \"tech companies\" are those where tech still plays a significant factor in the growth of the company such that it's important to an executive level. Example: Google is still a tech company. As much as some might view search as \"solved\" (just like it was when Google was founded I might add), search continues to get better. Tech still plays a significant role in the actual core business (ie advertising) too. I once again return to the Steve Jobs take on why Xerox failed [1]. An effective monopoly devalued the tech such that Xerox became a sales and finance company. So, returning to Netflix. I agree Netflix isn't a tech company anymore because Netflix ultimately is about serving several thousand VODs and that problem is solved. Now Netflix's core business is about making content, licensing content and acquiring and retaining customers. Obviously doing this (or anything) at scale is a lot harder than it sounds but at the end of the day Youtube is monumentally more technically challenging than Netflix due to the number of videos, the number of viewers, the advertising and live streaming. \"Tech company\" is still a useful classifier IMHO. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28539419,"author":"colinmhayes","month":"2021-09","story":"Intel is reducing server chip pricing in attempt to stem the AMD tide","stance":"reference","why":"Dominant tech companies lose ability to innovate","quote":"Jobs explained the problem well","text":"I'm reasonably bullish on intel, but Jobs explained the problem well [VIDEO] When tech companies become a dominant force in their market they lose their ability to innovate, and it's very hard to change culture once that's happened."},{"id":28023663,"author":"cletus","month":"2021-08","story":"Blizzard's reputation collapsed in just three years","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on monopolies releasing new bottles instead of innovating","quote":"Steve Jobs said it best [1].","text":"> Does anyone know why this happens? Oh it's easy to explain. Steve Jobs said it best [1]. He talks about innovation at Pepsi being a new bottle. That's what Activision Blizzard is. They have a \"monopoly\" with their key franchises, most notably CoD and WoW. They just release a new \"bottle\" every 1-2 years. Game studios are a bit like a large film studio. They don't like the creative process. It's too unpredictable. They want a formula to produce content that can be predicted, projected and tracked. Here's another key point: everything you do as a company either adds to or subtracts from your brand value. For example: Square Enix giving discounted subscriptions and a general mea culpa around the original FF14 release, brand value add. Blizzard adding micro-transactions to classic TBC? That's nothing more than the short-term extraction of brand value into money. It's a bit like delivering pizza. You're trading long term car depreciation and maintenance for short-term cash. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27999858,"author":"cletus","month":"2021-07","story":"Activision Blizzard Hires Notorious Union-Busting Firm WilmerHale","stance":"reference","why":"Applied to finance people taking over Activision Blizzard","quote":"The Steve Jobs quote on why Xerox failed [1] strikes again.","text":"The Steve Jobs quote on why Xerox failed [1] strikes again. The finance people have taken over Acti-Blizzard and they've been coasting for 10+ years on their original franchises. All we have is annual CoD releases and Blizzrad coasting on their old properties where Blizzrd hasn't had a significant original release in 10+ years. This effort seems like it's part of the ruthless approach to controlling costs that slowly strangle a company from within. I believe WoW is the #2 property (after CoD) at Acti-Blizzard and it's clearly changed from one of delivering a game to simply extracting as much money as possible from each customer much like how almost all mobile games do. The state of California's complaint is bad. I mean really bad. The fact that 3-4 different people from AB all released different statements in the last week should tell you exactly how bad it is. That's classic panic mode. There should only be one. This latest move tells you the company believes it will blow over and they're looking to do the minimal required to appease the detractors and get back to business as usual without having to pay people more or pay out a bunch of lawsuits. Honestly, the heads of J Allen Brack and Bobby Kotick in particular should roll over this lawsuit. [1]: [VIDEO] EDIT: commenters have noted (correctly) that I overlooked Overwatch. This was a significant release but it seems to also have waned in popularity and Overwatch 2 is inextricably going in some weird PVE direction. Other tha"},{"id":24382286,"author":"ksaho","month":"2020-09","story":"Has Intel lost its mojo?","stance":"reference","why":"Summarizes how sales people take over companies","quote":"He summarises the whole process, Intel is now at the sales and marketing people running it.","text":"Obligatory (Steve Jobs on why Xerox failed): [VIDEO] He summarises the whole process, Intel is now at the sales and marketing people running it."},{"id":23069383,"author":"da_chicken","month":"2020-05","story":"Oracle vs. PostgreSQL: First Glance","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Jobs's explanation of why monopolies decline","quote":"They're an example of Steve Job's comments on Xerox's failure[0].","text":"They're a victim of their own success. They became a monopoly and the quality of their product stopped mattering. They're an example of Steve Job's comments on Xerox's failure[0]. It happened at Oracle, IBM, Cisco, and Microsoft. It's happening now at Apple, Intel and Google. [0]: [VIDEO]"}]},"OBfB2MJw3qg":{"wy":"HN recommends it to anyone curious about Lisp machines, since it comes from someone who worked at Symbolics.","q":{"x":"If you only have 15 minutes, he gives another demo here","i":26726886,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26726886,"author":"rml","month":"2021-04","story":"What have we lost? [video]","stance":"rec","why":"One-hour talk on Symbolics by a former employee","quote":"If you are into Lisp Machines, this talk on Symbolics by Kalman Reti (who worked there) is worth watching (1h)","text":"If you are into Lisp Machines, this talk on Symbolics by Kalman Reti (who worked there) is worth watching (1h): [VIDEO] If you only have 15 minutes, he gives another demo here: [LINK] He has another video where he does some actual hacking (smooth scrolling of sheet music for display while playing an instrument): [LINK]"},{"id":39812895,"author":"Phiwise_","month":"2024-03","story":"Symbolics Sunstone Development Plan (1987) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Reti on Symbolics' efficient chip design methodology","quote":"We took about 10 to 12 man-years to do the Ivory chip","text":">In addition: two months to a compiler? Four to a complete chip? All from a team under 15 ppl? \"Because of [Symbolics' automated design] methodology, we were able to do five different revs of the chip on four different technologies, which was pretty amazing [for the time]. ... We took about 10 to 12 man-years to do the Ivory chip, and the only comparable chip contemporaneous to that was the MicroVAX at DEC; I knew some people who worked on that, and their estimate was that it was 70 to 80 man-years to do the MicroVAX. That in a nutshell is the reason that the Symbolics lisp machine was great.\" -Kalman Reti (2012) [VIDEO] Non-blub work environments bring sone fantasy-tier goals down to just within possibility by a motivated team of geniuses, which Symbolics had. Reminds me a little of how Alan Kay describes the forgotten art of Starrett erecting the empire state building in under a year: \"Gentlemen, this building of yours is going to present unusual problems. Ordinary equipment won't be worth a damn on it. We'll buy and make new stuff, fitted for the job. That's what we do on every big job; it costs less than renting secondhand stuff, and it's more efficient.\" [LINK]"},{"id":25982231,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2021-01","story":"Why I still Lisp","stance":"reference","why":"Symbolics Lisp machine demo showing the REPL environment","quote":"Or online demos like for Symbolics.","text":"Common Lisp invented the REPL, Clojure only provides a subset of it. Have a look at, \"The Interlisp Programming Environment\", [LINK] Or online demos like [VIDEO] for Symbolics. Ultimately you can try the community versions of Allegro and LispWorks. [LINK] [LINK]"}],"r":[["o4-YnLpLgtk",1]]},"QfDoQwIAaXg":{"wy":"Used as the go-to example of genuine one-million-fps footage and of bullet impact physics.","q":{"x":"At around 3:00 there is some nice bullet into bulletproof glass action.","i":874275,"y":"2009"},"m":[{"id":874275,"author":"jws","month":"2009-10","story":"Bullet Impacts at 1,000,000 fps","stance":"rec","why":"Direct link with timestamped highlights of bullet impacts and shockwaves.","quote":"At around 3:00 there is some nice bullet into bulletproof glass action.","text":"Youtube video: direct link... [VIDEO] The watermarked website is a wreck. More than just bullet impacts. At around 2:00 I think it is showing a BB sized object hitting a bullet from a perpendicular trajectory. At around 3:00 there is some nice bullet into bulletproof glass action. 5:50 has shockwave formation in liquids. Back of envelope calculations suggest that most of this is at less than 1,000,000 frames per second, but the slowest of the slow might be at a million."},{"id":36764219,"author":"genmud","month":"2023-07","story":"What AT&T and Verizon Knew About Toxic Lead Cables for Decades","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source on lead dust from bullet impacts.","quote":"they tend to produce a lots of fine lead dust[1].","text":"Also, depending on how the bullets get caught at the range (shredded tires vs sand vs steel backstops), they tend to produce a lots of fine lead dust[1]. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":4347146,"author":"startupfounder","month":"2012-08","story":"Photo shows Mars rover descent","stance":"reference","why":"Shows 1 million fps is achievable on earth.","quote":"if you can do 1m fps on earth[1]","text":"True, but if you can do 1m fps on earth[1] and the MRO travels at a speed of 133,879.68 inches/second then you could take 7.4693934 pictures per inch even if you had a rolling \"scanning\" shutter. I know that would be way to much data for them to process, but for the 172 seconds from parachute deployment to touchdown you could have one amazing high speed film. I am sure NASA can fund a high speed camera for MRO... or do we need to crowdfund that one as well... [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":3346729,"author":"rorrr","month":"2011-12","story":"Trillion-frame-per-second video","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as genuine 1 million fps footage for comparison.","quote":"Here's true 1 million fps footage","text":"It sounds like they are taking different phases of the light spreading, and it just looks like the light is traveling. While cool, it's not a true 1 trillion fps camera. Here's true 1 million fps footage: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1331924,"author":"jacquesm","month":"2010-05","story":"Mini Cannon Firing and Destroying Targets","stance":"reference","why":"More bullet impact footage offered as supporting example.","quote":"See here for some more 'bullet impact' video","text":"The egg splattering on to the keyboard would not happen because there is not enough kinetic energy in that small bullet to make it splash back very far, the lightbulb though for sure put some fragments in to his keyboard because the shards bounce off each other and will cause some shards to go in the opposite direction of where the bullet came from. See here for some more 'bullet impact' video: [VIDEO]"}]},"cNZPRsrwumQ":{"wy":"Used as the go-to demo of robot resilience when Boston Dynamics comes up.","q":{"x":"Just how resilient is it? Watch it on ice","i":9026456,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9026456,"author":"sjtrny","month":"2015-02","story":"Boston Dynamics' New Robot – Spot","stance":"rec","why":"Legged robot resilience demo","quote":"Just how resilient is it? Watch it on ice","text":"> Can it get up if knocked over? Yes [LINK] . > Just how resilient is it? Watch it on ice [VIDEO] I assumed the HN crowd had already seen all these videos."},{"id":17066469,"author":"blacksmith_tb","month":"2018-05","story":"Boston Dynamics will start selling its dog-like SpotMini robot in 2019","stance":"reference","why":"BigDog's loud gas engine versus quiet electric SpotMini","quote":"The Big Dog has an internal combustion engine (what I take to be an electrical generator) that is about as loud as a chainsaw[1].","text":"The Big Dog has an internal combustion engine (what I take to be an electrical generator) that is about as loud as a chainsaw[1]. SpotMini makes servo noises, but it's all-electric, and quite a bit less noisy. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9372616,"author":"davidw","month":"2015-04","story":"Gotchas from Two Years with Node","stance":"reference","why":"Robot that gets back up after kicks","quote":"robot dog/mule thing that they keep kicking and it gets back up and keeps going.","text":"> At any given time there’s only a single running code block. > But here… there be dragons. That's one of the things Erlang solves pretty well. Sometimes I sort of envision it as that robot dog/mule thing that they keep kicking and it gets back up and keeps going. [VIDEO] if you have never seen it before."},{"id":6904876,"author":"SmileyKeith","month":"2013-12","story":"Google Acquires Boston Dynamics","stance":"reference","why":"Boston Dynamics robot demo","quote":"Having just watched this video[0] of some of their work this stuff is just amazing.","text":"Having just watched this video[0] of some of their work this stuff is just amazing. I can't wait to see what Google does with it. Also this is what makes Google such an awesome company from a computer science perspective IMO. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6991803,"author":"cabalamat","month":"2013-12","story":"The rise of robots and the future of jobs","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Big Dog robot","quote":"Big Dog is a robot made by a company Google bought.","text":"It says in the article that Big Dog is a robot made by a company Google bought. Here's a video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4164021,"author":"Retric","month":"2012-06","story":"Google & Stanford create a digital brain that learns to identify a human face","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Let's say you want to build a robot that walks around like a person or even a goat. There are basically two part's of that problem, building the hardware, and building the software. If you use neural networks for the software then it's going to take more resources to run, and take a while to train. However, while people may be able to write more efficient software that requires fewer resources developing that software is going to take longer and cost a lot more money. [VIDEO] The idea that I can create software which works even if I don't understand why it works is a powerful idea. The point of AI as a field is stepping back and saying: \"Computers are fast and cheap, people are slow and expensive, can't we automate this?\" and continuing to do that not just to secretary's, or even coders, but also designers. As long as you can build a large enough training set and toss enough computing resources you can basically solve any problem. Granted, the need for large training set's and processing power can also be a huge limitations. But, in the end neural nets / AI is just another tool like your computer, Algebra, or a big stick. The it's a cool idea and solves useful problems is enough for people to spend a lot of time and money looking into it."},{"id":25578199,"author":"EvanAnderson","month":"2020-12","story":"Do You Love Me? [video]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"really wanted a human to enter the frame and menace the robots with a hockey stick[1]","text":"I can't be the only person who really wanted a human to enter the frame and menace the robots with a hockey stick[1] or kick one of them[2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"dxk8b9rSKOo":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a primary source for facts such as Amazon.com running on AWS and its very frequent deployments.","q":{"x":"the mean time between deployments on an average weekday is 11.6 seconds.","i":6239277,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":17254116,"author":"aphexairlines","month":"2018-06","story":"Four years after its release, Kubernetes has come a long way","stance":"rec","why":"Amazon's internal service architecture, deployment and autoscaling tools.","quote":"Amazon describes its internal service architecture here","text":"Amazon describes its internal service architecture here: [VIDEO] Services use an internal tool called Apollo to describe the service, deploy it to EC2 VMs, and auto-scale. Apollo inspired AWS CodeDeploy and AWS AutoScaling. Services are reachable via load balancer sets similar to AWS ELB/ALB/NLB. You reach a service by the LB-set's DNS name. If you don't want to use AWS or AWS-specific deployment tools, you could use Puppet Pipelines to do the same on Google Cloud or Azure. Puppet Pipelines was built by people who previously built some of those internal Amazon tools and offers similar functionality but cross-cloud. And if you want even fewer moving parts, just go PaaS or serverless."},{"id":13838205,"author":"alberth","month":"2017-03","story":"Always Free Usage Limits","stance":"reference","why":"Source that Amazon.com has run on AWS since 2010.","quote":"since 2010, Amazon.com has been running on AWS.","text":"Off topic: does google.com run on GCP? I ask because since 2010, Amazon.com has been running on AWS. [VIDEO] (Around 7m30s into video)"},{"id":8689828,"author":"mrjatx","month":"2014-12","story":"AWS CodeCommit","stance":"reference","why":"Source that Amazon moved fully onto AWS by 2011.","quote":"They converted 100% in 2011 according to Jon Jenkins","text":"Geez, oh yes. They built their platform and decided to start reselling it. That's why Netflix, Pintrest, Airbnb, Expedia, Foursquare, NASA, the MLB all use AWS. They converted 100% in 2011 according to Jon Jenkins [VIDEO]"},{"id":6239277,"author":"dxm","month":"2013-08","story":"Amazon was down","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Amazon's deployment frequency and outage rate.","quote":"the mean time between deployments on an average weekday is 11.6 seconds.","text":"Every developer does have the ability to deploy to production and roughly 0.001% of deployments cause an outage, but this is a company where the mean time between deployments on an average weekday is 11.6 seconds. Source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4685904,"author":"yap","month":"2012-10","story":"Amazon EC2 currently down. Affecting Heroku, Reddit, Others","stance":"reference","why":"Source that Amazon.com runs on EC2 since 2010.","quote":"Amazon runs all of the Amazon.com web servers on EC2 hosts (and has since 2010","text":"Amazon runs all of the Amazon.com web servers on EC2 hosts (and has since 2010: [VIDEO] ). They've just made sure that they have enough hosts in each of the other AZs to withstand an outage."}]},"eRlhKaxcKpA":{"wy":"HN mentions it as an early example of corpus-based concatenative synthesis and audio-matching techniques.","q":{"x":"It splits music videos into small portions ahead of time and then later it reassembles them on the fly according to audio input.","i":27797348,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":38537357,"author":"hoherd","month":"2023-12","story":"How does Shazam work? (2022)","stance":"rec","why":"2006 music software project using similar classifying techniques","quote":"If this interests you, then take a look at sCrAmBlEd?HaCkZ!","text":"If this interests you, then take a look at sCrAmBlEd?HaCkZ!, a music software project from 2006 that uses similar classifying techniques. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27797348,"author":"codetrotter","month":"2021-07","story":"Tool to convert copyrighted music into fair use","stance":"reference","why":"Splits music videos and reassembles them on the fly to audio input","quote":"It splits music videos into small portions ahead of time and then later it reassembles them on the fly according to audio input.","text":"Reminds me of this other thing from years ago called “sCrAmBlEd?HaCkZ!” [VIDEO] It splits music videos into small portions ahead of time and then later it reassembles them on the fly according to audio input."},{"id":21170341,"author":"jcims","month":"2019-10","story":"Principal Component Analysis","stance":"reference","why":"Corpus-based concatenative synthesis demo","quote":"reminds me of Scrambled? Hackz!","text":"I'm not sure if i'm parsing this conversation correctly, but 'corpus based concatenative synthesis' reminds me of Scrambled? Hackz! - [VIDEO]"}]},"fsi0aJ9yr2o":{"wy":"HN remembers it as a great dev tool pitch, with commenters crediting it for showing optimistic UI and winning them over to Meteor.","q":{"x":"Latency compensation/optimistic UIs is one of the first things that clued me into why Meteor is so great back in 2012 when I watched the original screencast","i":9615843,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":31784746,"author":"beefman","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Best dev tool pitches of all time?","stance":"rec","why":"Original Meteor pitch that helped raise a Series A","quote":"The original Meteor pitch was pretty great","text":"The original Meteor pitch was pretty great [VIDEO] Got them an $11M Series A from a16z, which was big at the time. But who's in this video? Geoff and Matt?"},{"id":9615843,"author":"primigenus","month":"2015-05","story":"Optimistic UI with Meteor","stance":"reference","why":"Original screencast showed latency compensation and less boilerplate","quote":"Latency compensation/optimistic UIs is one of the first things that clued me into why Meteor is so great back in 2012 when I watched the original screencast","text":"Latency compensation/optimistic UIs is one of the first things that clued me into why Meteor is so great back in 2012 when I watched the original screencast: [VIDEO] The reason is actually a common theme throughout Meteor: boilerplate code, and somewhat relatedly \"devops\" type code, are taken care of by the platform so you get to focus on building your app. It's such a big focus of Meteor's design compared to every other platform, and it's what's kept me coming back for three years. In fact, it's surprising that so few other platforms have picked up on the fact that eradicating boilerplate is a feature, and they should compete on it. Maybe with more posts like this, they'll be inspired to head in that direction!"},{"id":9244136,"author":"avital","month":"2015-03","story":"Ask HN: What startups are working on hard, technically challenging problems?","stance":"reference","why":"Original Meteor screencast introducing the platform","quote":"If you've never heard about Meteor, check out the original screencast","text":"At Meteor, we're building a complete open-source platform for easily building best-of-class, fast, web and mobile apps. If you've never heard about Meteor, check out the original screencast: [VIDEO] (needless to say, lots have changed since then -- most notably a security model :) Meteor touches all parts of an application stack (client-side templating[1], live database updates[2], a websocket-based sync protocol[3], an \"all you need\" build tool[4] that can build mobile and web apps, an in-house \"transparent reactive programming\" library[5], a cloud hosting environment[6] built on Docker and Kubernetes, and the list goes on -- all with a very very strong emphasis on great developer experience). Learn more about Meteor's subprojects here: [LINK] . Most of these projects are current edge new technology -- we develop new systems when the existing open source ones aren't good enough (either because of how they're implemented or because we can supply a better developer experience) Moreover, almost all of our work is open source which means working here builds your GitHub profile -- always a good thing. Remote is OK. Learn more at [LINK] , or happy to answer questions at avital@meteor.com. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK]"}],"r":[["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["pW-SOdj4Kkk",1]]},"hBMoPUAeLnY":{"wy":"Cited as a primary source for Trump's own stated positions, such as replacing income tax with tariffs.","q":{"x":"Watch the Joe Rogan interview with Trump to see what he's really like","i":44098331,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44098331,"author":"nickpsecurity","month":"2025-05","story":"Tariffs in American History","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Trump's real personality beyond stage persona","quote":"Watch the Joe Rogan interview with Trump to see what he's really like","text":"Watch the Joe Rogan interview with Trump to see what he's really like: [VIDEO] What we normally see is a stage persona. He also speaks differently when dealing with hostiles vs people acting like he's a human being. Rogan's style brings out people's real personalities better."},{"id":42058664,"author":"zmgsabst","month":"2024-11","story":"Trump wins presidency for second time","stance":"reference","why":"Trump interview on Rogan","quote":"You could also watch the episode interviewing Trump.","text":"This interview between Elon Musk and Joe Rogan explains — though I’m not sure the timestamp. Joe Rogan found it convincing enough to endorse Trump afterwards. [LINK] You could also watch the episode interviewing Trump. [VIDEO] Or his VP, Vance. [LINK] Presumably the majority are people who agree with the message conveyed during such interviews."},{"id":41995399,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2024-10","story":"Companies ready price hikes to offset Trump's global tariff plans","stance":"reference","why":"Trump discusses replacing income tax with tariffs","quote":"his goal is to replace federal income tax with tariffs as he discussed on Joe Rogan","text":"It's probably worth adding that his goal is to replace federal income tax with tariffs as he discussed on Joe Rogan [1]. I would love to see this happen. Other countries can pay my taxes. [1] - [VIDEO] [video][3 hours]"}],"r":[["w-WMn_zHCVo",1],["CesHr99ezWE",1],["eiYoBbEZwlk",1],["BsVydyC8ZGQ",1]]},"m7_WDzPyoqU":{"wy":"Linked in crypto-mining threads for its picture of what nearby residents endure, though some commenters treat it as a case of corporations overriding locals.","q":{"x":"gives a better visceral sense of what these people are dealing with","i":45540725,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45540725,"author":"georgemcbay","month":"2025-10","story":"Texas community will vote to form a city in effort to quiet crypto mine","stance":"rec","why":"Gives a visceral sense of what residents near the mine endure","quote":"gives a better visceral sense of what these people are dealing with","text":"The wall they built looks like its kind of a joke when you see it in context and the situation is a lot worse than just not having the \"original peaceful quiet\". More Perfect Union did a video on this a few months ago that gives a better visceral sense of what these people are dealing with: [VIDEO]"},{"id":49118172,"author":"Levitating","month":"2026-07","story":"Destroying a Community with a Gigantic \"Clogged Vacuum Cleaner\"","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This reminds of a video[1] from More Perfect Union about another (the same?) crypto mining facility in Texas.","text":"This reminds of a video[1] from More Perfect Union about another (the same?) crypto mining facility in Texas. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44740798,"author":"jordanb","month":"2025-07","story":"State capacity and eight parking spaces","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"corporations running roughshod over locals","text":"Biggest problems with \"Abundance\" are: 1) Somehow he decided euclidean zoning was a leftist project. Most leftists and environmentalists I've ever known hate euclidean zoning and this includes many urban planners. Euclidean zoning exists pretty much because property owners want to be able to exclude nearby land uses. It's fundamentally conservative, especially when the goal is to enforce limited housing density. 2) Setting up euclidean zoning as the main whipping boy and then saying \"and also air quality regulations are basically the same thing\" is a form of straw-manning the stuff he's arguing against. 3) He sets up a dichotomy between California and Texas ignoring that a lot of California's problems are conservative (downzoning, prop 13) and also ignoring the weaknesses in the Texas model including corporations running roughshod over locals ( [VIDEO] ) a fragile power grid, huge flooding problems etc. It's possible to look at quality of life metrics and see that Texas is doing quite badly. Also, you had the whole tech moving to Austin thing that already fizzled out. 4) He set up developers as the heroes in the story and apparently got a lot of his info from private equity ride-alongs. He ignores that developers are also often land speculators and are in favor of blocking competitors projects and downzoning areas to keep land value high. ( [LINK] ) Basically Abundance is a way for Ezra Klein and his fellow travelers to repackage Clinton-era triangulation and Obama-era neolib"}]},"mu-tJc-BgaI":{"wy":"HN cites it in threads about Teslas trapping occupants to show how the manual release is located and used.","q":{"x":"Here's how to do it in a Model 3.","i":39661702,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39661702,"author":"gnicholas","month":"2024-03","story":"A Mistake in a Tesla and a Panicked Final Call: The Death of Angela Chao","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how to use the mechanical door release in a Model 3.","quote":"Here's how to do it in a Model 3.","text":"Yeah, I just talked with a Rivian engineer (who owns a Tesla) and he said that all these vehicles have mechanical pulls, but the problem is that they are hidden and people don't know where they are. It's kind of odd the article didn't mention this fact and enlighten the readers as to how to avoid the same fate! Here's how to do it in a Model 3. [1] Apparently it differs by vehicle. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37207726,"author":"blueflow","month":"2023-08","story":"Tesla owners say they've been trapped inside their EVs after they lost power","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Tesla door handles in action.","quote":"Video of the handles in action","text":"Video of the handles in action: [VIDEO] Both functions are not evident (to me) by looking at the handles. Maybe the Tesla's UI is just bad."},{"id":26960300,"author":"kingsuper20","month":"2021-04","story":"“Teslas are bad cars. Here are a few reasons why.”","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Model 3's mechanical emergency door release.","quote":"It seems to me that they have an emergency opener.","text":"It seems to me that they have an emergency opener. It's not the kind of thing a car manufacturer would forget about. [VIDEO] People are funny about the love/hate Tesla continuum. My take is that they are like owning any smaller volume semi-exotic car. People who think they are crazy unreliable should talk to the ex-owners of V12 BMWs. 'lectric cars appear to be the future, but the average person could stand to wait for another generation or two of batteries and buy something like an EV Corolla."},{"id":37207762,"author":"FirmwareBurner","month":"2023-08","story":"Tesla owners say they've been trapped inside their EVs after they lost power","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"why did Tesla need to use two obscure looking buttons with no labels on them to open the door[1]","text":">uses the manual release handle instead of the right button, and I get a warning message on the screen and the risk of breaking the window glass. Is there a technical limitation on why the manual release can't also mechanically slide down the window slightly when pulled to not damage the glass? Also, why did Tesla need to use two obscure looking buttons with no labels on them to open the door[1], when many cars use the same obvious leaver for both cases: pull on it lightly to engage the electronic un-lock, pull on it again with more force to engage the mechanical un-lock. So simple, so elegant. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"nCgQDjiotG0":{"wy":"HN cites it as an example of Chrome's speed-focused marketing and of how fast Chrome seemed at launch compared with other browsers.","q":{"x":"Chrome was seriously fast when it launched. It made everything else seem a decade old.","i":27033324,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":1329153,"author":"brezina","month":"2010-05","story":"Nowmov (YC W10): Sit Back, Relax, And Watch An Endless Stream of Videos","stance":"rec","why":"Cool Chrome speed test videos found via nowmov.","quote":"one of the coolest youtube videos i've seen in a while","text":"welp, i'm back after using nowmov. thanks to nowmov i found one of the coolest youtube videos i've seen in a while [VIDEO] Google Chrome speed tests"},{"id":29729300,"author":"mgh2","month":"2021-12","story":"Microsoft buys Xandr, AT&T’s advanced advertising business","stance":"reference","why":"Early Chrome ad highlighting its speed advantage","quote":"But Chrome had a clear advantage those days compared to other solutions","text":"I still remember their ads: [VIDEO] But Chrome had a clear advantage those days compared to other solutions Microsoft's value proposition? PDF users and memory consumption?"},{"id":27033324,"author":"perryizgr8","month":"2021-05","story":"Microsoft bids $44.6B to buy Yahoo (2008)","stance":"reference","why":"Chrome launch speed demo that made other browsers seem old","quote":"Chrome was seriously fast when it launched. It made everything else seem a decade old.","text":"Remember this video? [VIDEO] Chrome was seriously fast when it launched. It made everything else seem a decade old."},{"id":23395455,"author":"enonevets","month":"2020-06","story":"Google Is Not God of the Web","stance":"reference","why":"Google's own ad showing Chrome speed as a selling point","quote":"Google explicitly marketed Chrome speed as a selling point.","text":"I was replying to the parent comment above mine stating that Google came late to the browser game just as they did with the search game and if part of what initially made Google appealing was that they were simple and faster than other search engines, that is true when Chrome was introduced as well. Google explicitly marketed Chrome speed as a selling point. For example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17629930,"author":"cromwellian","month":"2018-07","story":"How did Google get so big?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There's an implicit assumption in most of these discussions that beating Google Search is a matter of money and scale, and while there are some evidence for that (e.g. unreasonable effectiveness of having lots of data), it is not a certain conclusion, it is only a conclusion if your goal is to beat Google by making an incrementally better classical search engine. It is indeed hard to beat something by offering something \"about the same\", when the cost for the consumer is already effectively zero from a monetary perspective. But this doesn't rule out someone coming up with a disruptive, radically better search. Take this as a thought experiment, somewhere out there, is another Albert Einstein, working on his magnum opus, an AGI based search engine. He has a mind blowing advancement in AGI, like GR was in Physics, that will completely eclipse classical search AND deep learning approaches AND he knows it. Demos shown to investors are mind blowing, and he quickly raises enough funds to launch a competitor. There is whisper and talk all around the valley. Google tries to make an acquisition, but he and his investors turn it down, because they can see how valuable the tech is. By the time Google realizes their offer should have been much bigger, it's getting traction and taking off like a rocket ship. Now Baidu, Tencent, Alibaba, Amazon, Microsoft, are all looking to acquire it. Yes, there are arguments that being the \"default\" is hard to dislodge. But Chrome dislodged IE. Google S"},{"id":15778740,"author":"chiefofgxbxl","month":"2017-11","story":"Ciao, Chrome: Firefox Quantum Is the Browser Built for 2017","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Chrome's speed-test ads for a general audience.","quote":"Chrome did speed-test television ads [0] like, Is Chrome faster than a potato cannon?","text":"It's good to see Mozilla's ramped up PR, but given how deeply entrenched Chrome is, I doubt it will be enough. That is why we developers, if we support Firefox's/Mozilla's mission, must make it our goal to spread the word. I'm setting a personal goal to let 5 (non-tech) people (mostly in my family) know about this. I've only seen tech articles/blog posts for Mozilla's PR, so while that is great, they may need to expand their efforts to include the general public. Chrome did speed-test television ads [0] like, Is Chrome faster than a potato cannon? that were appealing to a general audience. Not suggesting Mozilla do this. Perhaps reaching out to universities and school districts to adopt Firefox as their default browser? At my university if you went on a public computer all it had on the desktop was Chrome, so guess what people are going to use, not only on school computers but on their personal devices? The same is true for my high school. I commend Mozilla for their giant task at hand, taking on such a technological behemoth that has uncountable benefits when it comes to advertising their browser. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"ohDB5gbtaEQ":{"wy":"Mostly posted as a joke reply in arguments, with one commenter recommending it as a lovely skit about how to argue.","q":{"x":"absolutely lovely Monty python skit","i":43695873,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43695873,"author":"mooreed","month":"2025-04","story":"How to win an argument with a toddler","stance":"rec","why":"Humorous skit with lessons about arguing.","quote":"absolutely lovely Monty python skit","text":"Post reminds me of the absolutely lovely Monty python skit [1] that is not only humorous, but has a lot you can learn from. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40839550,"author":"slowhadoken","month":"2024-06","story":"The Bleak Genius of Michel Foucault","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is a Monty Python skit. Chomsky called him amoral and his philosophy fake. He doesn’t disagree with Foucault’s “philosophy” he thinks it’s fake. A moral person doesn’t swingle people with lies. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39909657,"author":"happytiger","month":"2024-04","story":"GPT Chats with T-Mobile Support to get discounts","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I’m you want me to go on arguing, you’re going to have to pay for another 5 minutes. Every time one of these enshitification AI customer service startups pops up I always think of the ultimate form of where this is going… to people paying to argue with people. Follow me down the rabbit hole for a second. Imagine all of these things succeeded eventually and Customer Service completely saturated by talking to bots, to which they then have to deploy bots, and in very short order essentially becoming a bot API layer for Customer Service issues with gamification driving at the core. It’s almost inevitable. So then, people will want to develop the debate skills. They will feel like they are missing out in the negotiating skills people used to have. There will be coaches to help people learn how to deal with the skills set of negotiation, as all negotiation is now automated (which is, at the root, all this business is — its automated negotiation). Which leads my brain to believe that Monty Python was prescient and this will become a reality. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32560579,"author":"yarg","month":"2022-08","story":"Republishing a fork of the sanctioned Tornado Cash repositories","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> so he has a Constitutional right to publish on GitHub. No he doesn't, he's merely allowed to do what Github lets him do. Anyway, this is getting rather silly, so I'll leave you with this: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":27508777,"author":"failwhaleshark","month":"2021-06","story":"Solana Labs completes a $314M private token sale led by Andreessen Horowitz","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"That sounds like contention you've observed correlates with established, balkanized tribal factions. Perhaps it's most typically bikeshedding about minutiae in order to have an argument. Argument - Monty Python [VIDEO]"},{"id":27034574,"author":"chrisco255","month":"2021-05","story":"First genetically modified mosquitoes released in the United States","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Reminds me of the classic Monty Python skit, where a guy goes to a clinic to pay to argue with someone: [VIDEO] \"I'd like to have an argument, please.\""},{"id":23714876,"author":"moomin","month":"2020-07","story":"Hundreds arrested as crime chat network cracked","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"First off, you definitely should watch this: [VIDEO] But the point is the post just literally contradicted the previous post. There's not a lot of places to go from there."},{"id":21375543,"author":"s_gourichon","month":"2019-10","story":"PHP-FPM remote code execution bug exploited in the wild","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Argument is an intellectual process. Contradiction is just the automatic gainsaying of anything the other person says.\" [LINK] [VIDEO]"}]},"w-tFdreZB94":{"wy":"Cited as an example of corporate future-concept videos, sometimes appreciated for its ideas and sometimes used as a cautionary comparison.","q":{"x":"look at the Microsoft Future Vision concepts for some quite interesting ideas","i":25527152,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25527152,"author":"neilalexander","month":"2020-12","story":"Fantasy UI","stance":"rec","why":"Context-aware fantasy UI ideas from Microsoft.","quote":"look at the Microsoft Future Vision concepts for some quite interesting ideas","text":"If you haven’t seen them, look at the Microsoft Future Vision concepts for some quite interesting ideas: [VIDEO] [LINK] To me, what makes these fantasy UIs compelling is that they are usually extremely context-aware and content-aware. We haven’t quite worked out how to make real world computers work that way yet. We’re still very much tied to paradigms that are ultimately easier for the computer (apps, windows, menus, files) as opposed to things that are natural to humans. If I have one hope for ML, it’s that hopefully it will bring some context-awareness to computing so that the content becomes the user interface."},{"id":14400297,"author":"spookyuser","month":"2017-05","story":"Xperia Touch makes any surface interactive","stance":"reference","why":"Microsoft future concept video compared to Xperia Touch.","quote":"isn't a big portion of the microsoft future concept video [1] fulfilled?","text":"I have no idea how well this works in real life. But if the interactive surface is even close to what the video shows then isn't a big portion of the microsoft future concept video [1] fulfilled? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29030066,"author":"bredren","month":"2021-10","story":"Facebook Renames to Meta","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it is a lot like the old microsoft product concept videos","text":"Couple quick things: Zuck's speaking has improved an incredible amount. So, props there. Zuck's said a few variations of \"building blocks are there\" or \"it is coming together\" but also, \"Even though it is still a long way off...\" This projection / demo video of the metaverse seems like a distraction to me. If FB doesn't have this to deliver, it is a lot like the old microsoft product concept videos. [1] What is the intent? To see Zuck as a visionary? Does his speaking deeply on this subject attempt to position him / Meta as an authority? Unless Facebook / Meta is setting expectations way lower than where they're about to deliver, I don't see how this will be viewed as anything other than a temporary salve to fill the vacuum sucking oxygen out of Facebook. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"z_Ti4GP0ntE":{"wy":"Brought up whenever wearable cooling or heat extremes come up, as a real worked example of a pumped-coolant cooling suit.","q":{"x":"Fascinating design/build video.","i":23967296,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23967296,"author":"PTOB","month":"2020-07","story":"Sony’s wearable air conditioner is pretty cool","stance":"rec","why":"Adam Savage builds a portable cooling suit with an engineer.","quote":"Fascinating design/build video.","text":"Pre-Covid, Adam Savage built a portable condensing/evap cooling system using an old Soviet flight suit. He teamed up with an engineer to make it happen. Fascinating design/build video. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35866864,"author":"hotpotamus","month":"2023-05","story":"Vietnam records highest ever temperature of 44.1C","stance":"reference","why":"Adam Savage's AC-cooled cosplay suit, upgraded from ice.","quote":"Adam Savage built such a suit though it was not solar powered.","text":"Adam Savage built such a suit though it was not solar powered. He used it for cosplaying in oppressive heat and the AC was an upgrade over using ice. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34592867,"author":"krisoft","month":"2023-01","story":"Insulation: First the body, then the home (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a wearable pumped-coolant cooling suit.","quote":"Adam Savage’s cooling suit","text":"> Some kind of backpack with a fluid pump, heater-cooler unit, and battery Like Adam Savage’s cooling suit: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29840004,"author":"falcolas","month":"2022-01","story":"Dangerous humid heat extremes occurring decades before expected (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Adam Savage builds a cooling suit for astronaut cosplay.","quote":"Adam savage building one for an astronaut cosplay suit.","text":"Ironically, the cosplay space has this covered. They have portable battery powered air conditioners connected to astronaut-style onesies-with-coolant-pipes. Unfortunately they're still manually assembled, so not quite readily available yet. [VIDEO] <- Adam savage building one for an astronaut cosplay suit."},{"id":23973438,"author":"throwaway9d0291","month":"2020-07","story":"Sony’s wearable air conditioner is pretty cool","stance":"reference","why":"Adam Savage builds a wearable cooling system.","quote":"Adam Savage did a video where he and someone who appears ridiculously well-qualified built one of these","text":"> Now if you can get a miniaturized mechanical heat pump that has higher efficiency (day a coefficient of performance of 2-4) and you need to last for much longer than an hour, I think you can significantly beat the weight of an ice pack... Adam Savage did a video where he and someone who appears ridiculously well-qualified built one of these [VIDEO]"}]},"1Rr_XzzJ6gc":{"wy":"Used in UI debates as evidence that wiper control on the touchscreen is awkward, or alternatively that it is just two touches.","m":[{"id":26456606,"author":"jiofih","month":"2021-03","story":"Federal investigators blast Tesla, call for stricter safety standards","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates wiper access in two touches","quote":"The wipers are accessible in two quick touches","text":"The UI on that touchscreen is very different from the average car touchscreen. Bigger and ridiculously more responsive. The wipers are accessible in two quick touches: [VIDEO] I’m pretty sure you can operate it with voice commands as well, and the click wheel in the 3? That said, why would you not leave it on auto? I own a normal car and don’t touch the dashboard at all during 98% of drives, everything is set to auto (wipers, lights, AC)."},{"id":24061516,"author":"lazyjones","month":"2020-08","story":"Tesla touchscreen wiper controls land driver with fine after crash","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Model 3 wiper controls on the touchscreen","quote":"it also has this feature that makes using the touchscreen for the wipers while driving completely unnecessary","text":"The Model 3 has a very easy to use touchscreen interface for the wipers, it's hard to imagine someone missing it or being distracted for more than 1 second while using it. Apart from that, it also has this feature that makes using the touchscreen for the wipers while driving completely unnecessary (when they're on \"automatic\"): [VIDEO] Finally, there's also voice controls: \"Wipers increase\" and you're done. It's not magic, the horrible thing we still have is courts with clueless/uninterested judges."},{"id":20978933,"author":"userbinator","month":"2019-09","story":"Tesla has applied for a patent for a new electromagnetic windshield-wiper system","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The fact that videos like this exist shows just how horrible the UI is","text":"which are on the touch screen because of course they are I've never driven one, but a bit of research indicates that it has a right control stalk but Tesla didn't make it control the wipers. Instead, the button on the left stalk will wipe once . The fact that videos like this exist shows just how horrible the UI is: [VIDEO] I'm surprised Tesla could do this, because there are laws that dictate things like what the steering wheel should do, and the order of the pedals and transmission shift pattern (perhaps not so applicable to an EV.)"},{"id":20204368,"author":"Phillipharryt","month":"2019-06","story":"Mazda is purging touchscreens from its vehicles","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I'm allowed to criticise that method even if there's another one","text":"Well I did say one method, I'm allowed to criticise that method even if there's another one, they included it so they've got to assume people will use it. [VIDEO] it's not precisely a slide, but it's buttons placed in a sequence with a sliding animation."}]},"1YWD6Y9FUuw":{"wy":"Cited as footage of a famous piece of gaming history whenever id Software, Commander Keen, or scrolling tech comes up.","q":{"x":"They actually did a port of SMB3 and presented that to Nintendo.","i":36903988,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36903988,"author":"enneff","month":"2023-07","story":"Commander Keen's Adaptive Tile Scrolling","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of id's SMB3 port that led to Keen","quote":"They actually did a port of SMB3 and presented that to Nintendo.","text":"They actually did a port of SMB3 and presented that to Nintendo. When Nintendo didn’t bite, they made new assets to make what became Keen. Here’s a video of the port: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20222911,"author":"ohithereyou","month":"2019-06","story":"Developers don’t want to show gameplay at E3 anymore","stance":"reference","why":"id's SMB3 PC demo pitched to Nintendo","quote":"id software's demo for Super Mario Bros 3 on the PC[0]","text":"One of the things that I wish was available to customers on a regular basis is the playable demo that was pitched to a company for a game that gets picked up. An example of this would be id software's demo for Super Mario Bros 3 on the PC[0] (even though that didn't get picked up). [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19695499,"author":"gaogao","month":"2019-04","story":"Super Mario Bros. has been released for the Commodore 64","stance":"reference","why":"id's SMB3 PC port prototype that Nintendo declined","quote":"they hacked a port of Super Mario Bros 3 to the PC, but as history can tell, Nintendo didn't bite on that one.","text":"The smooth scrolling is pretty hard. One of my favorite bits of Masters of Doom about id Software was when they hacked a port of Super Mario Bros 3 to the PC, but as history can tell, Nintendo didn't bite on that one. [VIDEO]"}]},"3D8TEJtQRhw":{"wy":"Quoted as a joke when a company or government changes terms unilaterally.","m":[{"id":46711815,"author":"bigbadfeline","month":"2026-01","story":"European lawmakers suspend U.S. trade deal","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I'm altering the deal, pray I don't alter it any further","text":"> Changed the deal... [after] both parties agree to terms, [one] is given a document with modified terms... Putting on my Dart Vader hat: \"I'm altering the deal, pray I don't alter it any further\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":45257097,"author":"kop316","month":"2025-09","story":"Betty Crocker broke recipes by shrinking boxes","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I think this captures why it's a big deal","text":"> I get the nostalgic aspect. While you may understand the nostalgic aspect from a logical level, I don't think you understand it beyond that. This next line: > But it's not like there aren't a zillion from-scratch brownie recipes to choose from. To me anyways really indicates that. I know there are a zillion from-scratch recipes online and in books and in other media. That isn't the point. > If throwing away some extra premix destroys the pleasure, then that's a low bar. Once again, I think you are totally missing the point. I think this captures why it's a big deal: [VIDEO] Betty Crocker is effectively doing that. Without understanding it at more of emotional level, I can see why this doesn't resonate with you. Unfortunately, that also means it is unlikely that you will able to understand why it is a big deal and why other folks are arguing with you the way they are."},{"id":32082055,"author":"midenginedcoupe","month":"2022-07","story":"Show HN: Face IO – Facial Authentication for the Web","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"My first thought on reading this","text":"> We may revise our Terms, Conditions & Policies at any time by amending this page. You are expected to check this page from time to time to take notice of any changes we make, as they are legally binding on you. My first thought on reading this: [VIDEO] Oh, and the refund policy can be summed up by \"get fucked\". Hard pass."}]},"5s5EvhHy7eQ":{"wy":"HN cites it as a clever low-budget hardware hack and as an example of novel Wiimote uses that never became commercial.","q":{"x":"The polar opposite would be Johnny Chung Lee's videos.","i":8377645,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":37345150,"author":"guiambros","month":"2023-09","story":"Wii Architecture – A Practical Analysis","stance":"reference","why":"Novel Wiimote uses that never became commercial","quote":"using the then new wiimote in completely novel ways","text":"... or the various projects by Johnny Lee [1], using the then new wiimote in completely novel ways [2][3]. 15 years later, I'm still surprised these proof-of-concepts didn't evolve into something more commercial. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15892264,"author":"steamer25","month":"2017-12","story":"Ask HN: What software/service helps you be an effective remote developer?","stance":"reference","why":"Wiimote whiteboard that never took off","quote":"I'm also a bit surprised that Wiimote whiteboards never really took off","text":"Using a Wacom tablet with: * A drawing app e.g., OneNote or Sketchbook or Krita and * A screen sharing app e.g., join.me Has worked well for me. What's particularly nice is that you have a saveable, shareable, modifiable artifact when you're done with the remote session. I'm also a bit surprised that Wiimote whiteboards never really took off: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8377645,"author":"MiguelHudnandez","month":"2014-09","story":"Microsoft’s Cube debuts at Decibel, creating a one-of-a-kind digital dance party","stance":"reference","why":"Low-budget project video as contrast to polished marketing","quote":"The polar opposite would be Johnny Chung Lee's videos.","text":"I think the hate is the result of how hard they are trying to sell it. My subjective analysis: It is clearly awesome tech, but the obviously high production value of the video detracts from viewers' inclination to appreciate the project. The polar opposite would be Johnny Chung Lee's videos. Here's an example: [VIDEO] I suspect the ratio of marketing budget to project budget might be a good informal indicator of this effect. Or maybe it's just an aversion to anything big-budget?"},{"id":1190828,"author":"dtran","month":"2010-03","story":"Ask HN: Good, lightweight bug tracking system for small team?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Wiimote whiteboard software students built quickly","quote":"","text":"I don't have any more time for side projects, but lots of students in one of my HCI classes have built software to do stuff like this in a relatively short timespan (~4 weeks): [VIDEO]"}]},"7MFtl2XXnUc":{"wy":"Invoked as a joke when discussing ad creep, second screens and overbearing commercial or online experiences.","m":[{"id":37809022,"author":"andersrs","month":"2023-10","story":"AI offers improved civility for polarizing online conversations","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's similar to 'The Gift Shop Sketch' [1].","text":"[I know you feel that this technique is helpful and you want to save the world. That's double plus good of you.] [For me, I think that] all the padding makes the conversation diluted, watered down and boring. It's similar to 'The Gift Shop Sketch' [1]. [I feel] this is very disrespectful of my time. [In my opinion] it would be great to use another model to strip all the feely words, condense the meaning and generally un-fuckify all the AI waffle. [What are your thoughts about that?] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21888079,"author":"nitwit005","month":"2019-12","story":"88% of Americans use a second screen while watching TV","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I always think to this Mitchell and Webb gift shop sketch that parodies the worst of this","text":"Aside from being about a quarter commercials, TV shows often feature quite a bit of filler/padding content. It'd be odd if people didn't pick up on some of it and play with their phone when they have the option. I always think to this Mitchell and Webb gift shop sketch that parodies the worst of this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20604464,"author":"wvenable","month":"2019-08","story":"TV networks vowed to cut back on commercials but stuffed in more","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The Gift Shop Sketch","text":"The Gift Shop Sketch [VIDEO] I used to love watching Myth Busters but it became so much like that it was ridiculous. There was a time you could download torrents of Myth Busters episodes with all the recaps removed."},{"id":10016895,"author":"joshstrange","month":"2015-08","story":"To combat falling ratings, TV networks are increasing ads up to 10%","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This Mitchell and Webb sketch shows this off perfectly","text":"> TV shows try and adapt by regularly recapping what has happened before the last ad break This Mitchell and Webb sketch shows this off perfectly [VIDEO] When they do this they ruin the experience watching it later on DVR/Netflix/DVD/etc."}]},"7qZl_5xHoBw":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it as a source for what Musk said, such as on tariffs or gaming, and for context on Rogan's political endorsement rather than recommending it.","m":[{"id":42797231,"author":"johnneville","month":"2025-01","story":"Elon Musk admits he cheated at some video games","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"JOE ROGAN: So are you in the top 20 in the world?","text":"\"JOE ROGAN: So are you in the top 20 in the world? ELON MUSK: Yeah. JOE ROGAN: Wow, in Diablo. ELON MUSK: Yeah, yeah.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":42058664,"author":"zmgsabst","month":"2024-11","story":"Trump wins presidency for second time","stance":"reference","why":"Interview cited to explain Rogan's endorsement of Trump.","quote":"Joe Rogan found it convincing enough to endorse Trump afterwards.","text":"This interview between Elon Musk and Joe Rogan explains — though I’m not sure the timestamp. Joe Rogan found it convincing enough to endorse Trump afterwards. [VIDEO] You could also watch the episode interviewing Trump. [LINK] Or his VP, Vance. [LINK] Presumably the majority are people who agree with the message conveyed during such interviews."},{"id":42078193,"author":"ericd","month":"2024-11","story":"Trump wins presidency for second time","stance":"reference","why":"Musk discusses phasing in tariffs near the end.","quote":"he talks about exactly this near the end of his JRE interview","text":"Elon at least seems to be against sudden implementation of tariffs, he wants them phased in in a predictable over time, he talks about exactly this near the end of his JRE interview: [VIDEO] They also talk about the problems of losing competitive pressure from protectionist systems."},{"id":43942200,"author":"intermerda","month":"2025-05","story":"NSF faces shake-up as officials abolish its 37 divisions","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"But you see AI has a liberal bias. Even Musk's own AI is too woke because it isn't producing the \"right\" kind of humor - [VIDEO] ."}]},"CZ3wIuvmHeM":{"wy":"Mentioned as a real-world example that microservices can work at scale, though commenters are lukewarm and unsure it matches what they remember.","m":[{"id":29516793,"author":"kevin_nisbet","month":"2021-12","story":"Summary of the AWS Service Event in the Northern Virginia (US-East-1) Region","stance":"reference","why":"Possibly covers Netflix Hystrix and circuit breakers","quote":"I think talks about Hystrix a bit, but I'm not sure if it's the presentation I'm thinking of","text":"Netflix was talking alot about circuit breaks a few years ago, and had the Hystrix project. Looks like Hystrix is discontinued, so I'm not sure if there are good library solutions that are easy to adopt. Overall I don't see it getting talked about that frequently... beyond just exponential backoff inside a retry loop. - [LINK] - [VIDEO] I think talks about Hystrix a bit, but I'm not sure if it's the presentation I'm thinking of from years ago or not."},{"id":25668912,"author":"woutr_be","month":"2021-01","story":"Rails, React, Microservices and Micro-FrontEnds","stance":"reference","why":"Netflix as example of microservices working","quote":"Netflix is indeed one example. It's hard to argue it doesn't work","text":"> This is unavoidable for many companies, since the code bases are far to large for any single person to grasp them all. That's true, but this was the takeaway from the entire article: > \"When we remove that feeling of “I need to know how allllll of this works, but just enough so that I can rescue it when it breaks”, we facilitate the mental room to iterate on our team’s systems with greater depth.\" To me it sounds like they've now introduced exactly that feeling with micro-services, developers now need to know how all services and front-ends work, and how they interact with each other. Maybe the article is poorly structured, but if that's the entire takeaway from moving to micro-services and micro-frontends, then I do question their choice. > Netflix [VIDEO] Netflix is indeed one example. It's hard to argue it doesn't work, but from my experience, it never worked out the way it was envisioned."},{"id":22521503,"author":"dathinab","month":"2020-03","story":"Data-Oriented Architecture","stance":"reference","why":"Interesting but not what commenter was looking for","quote":"This was interesting as far as I remember but not what I was looking for","text":"I think that one was good: - [LINK] I wasn't able to find any other talk I watched back then or any of the stuff I did read, but I then last time I looked into talks and reading material about this was ~2.5 Years ago and while a lot of new software and tooling was done since then the principles didn't change. Try some of the other GOTO; talks about it if you like listening to talks, they tend to be quite good. This was interesting as far as I remember but not what I was looking for: - [VIDEO]"}]},"Di3fPj0pUbQ":{"wy":"HN uses it mostly as a joke or to point out that the human heater idea comes from the sitcom.","m":[{"id":44613641,"author":"bestham","month":"2025-07","story":"The Italian towns selling houses for €1","stance":"reference","why":"Silicon Valley scene where the human heater idea was rejected","quote":"The “human heater” was literally a mock idea for a competition in the sitcom Silicon Valley.","text":"The “human heater” was literally a mock idea for a competition in the sitcom Silicon Valley. The jury canned it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30489569,"author":"murbard2","month":"2022-02","story":"Heat people, not spaces (2015)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Indeed","text":"Indeed [VIDEO] Microwave frequencies used in ovens would be dangerous because they can penetrate deep in the body and heat internal tissues, but in the EHF range microwave heating could be safe. See for instance [LINK] With all that said, what I had in mind is in the far less scary and far more prosaic near-infrared range."},{"id":27195793,"author":"grae_QED","month":"2021-05","story":"Spin-torque oscillators for WiFi band transmission and energy harvesting","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"So they're using low to medium energy microwaves to power devices? Well if anyone doesn't buy that they can always try and sell it as a heater [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"EjmtSkl53h4":{"wy":"Commenters cite it as shorthand for the view that the Earth will outlast us, usually as a quip rather than a recommendation.","m":[{"id":20423713,"author":"darksaints","month":"2019-07","story":"Giant batteries and cheap solar power are shoving fossil fuels off the grid","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin's view on environment matches friend's attitude","quote":"His views are best described by George Carlin","text":"I know someone who has gone fully off the grid via sailboat and is currently on his way around the world. He converted his sailboat to a hybrid setup (electric engines powered by the same batteries that power his stovetop, refrigerator, etc.). His power sources keeping those batteries charged are solar (1200W) and sail regeneration [0]. He has an 18kw diesel generator as a safety measure. His story is particularly interesting to me because he doesn't give a shit about climate change. His views are best described by George Carlin [1]. And yet, he has the lowest carbon lifestyle of anybody I know. In the 15,000 nautical miles he's sailed, he has burned a grand total of 5 gallons of diesel. And that was only because he stayed for a few weeks in Palau without sailing around, and there was a stint of a few days where there was little solar energy due to storms. He now thinks his decision to buy a diesel backup generator was just a waste of money. If you ask him why he did it, these are his answers: It's cheaper, it's quieter, it's less smelly, and it's more reliable. Not a single mention of carbon emissions or resource depletion or pollution. At this point, I feel like the only reason fossil fuels are used anymore in new projects is inertia. The costs and performance of renewables+batteries has come so far in the last 5-10 years, and I think we're finally at the point where the intrinsic costs will guarantee adoption. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19900215,"author":"dark_ph0enix","month":"2019-05","story":"Mariana Trench: Deepest-ever sub dive finds plastic bag","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the quote","quote":"George Carlin had some great quotes indeed","text":"George Carlin had some great quotes indeed (this specific one comes from his take on the environment [1]). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16090524,"author":"throwaway9990","month":"2018-01","story":"Ocean's Oxygen Starts Running Low (2016)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"As Joe Carlin once said, the Earth will be fine.","text":"Posting from a throwaway because politics. Wouldn't there also be more different fish now especially in places that used to be covered by ice? Sure, some species might die off but you might get more new species as well. Some new fish perhaps that is good at living in lower oxygen concentration. Also, the fish that lived in the tropics can move to subtropics and so on. I see it as more of a change that's not really bad or good. As Joe Carlin once said, the Earth will be fine. Here is the clip: [VIDEO]"}]},"Go5tLO6ipxw":{"wy":"Used as a source in threads complaining about Dell, particularly aggressive upselling of warranty packages.","m":[{"id":36753509,"author":"netsharc","month":"2023-07","story":"Dude, You’re Gettin a Dell","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Dell was forcing them the extended/on-site warranty","text":"For a not so glowy opinion of Dell: [VIDEO] TL; DW: LTT bought computers on the phone secret-shopper style and Dell was forcing them the extended/on-site warranty, despite the shopper saying no many times the Dell rep still put these things in the order and charged them for it."},{"id":33343989,"author":"nerdawson","month":"2022-10","story":"DontBuyDell.com","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Then they tack it on anyway.","text":"Anyone else see the Linus Tech Tips secret shopper video covering Dell? They repeatedly try to sell a warranty which they’re told isn’t wanted. Then they tack it on anyway. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28383438,"author":"bellyfullofbac","month":"2021-09","story":"Lenovo tablet forced update shows un-dismissable, un-mutable, ad notifications","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the sales rep pushed the extra warranty hard, and included it (at cost) despite the buyer declining it","text":"Did you get extra warranty? [VIDEO] TL;DW: they did a secret shopper laptop buying experience, the sales rep pushed the extra warranty hard, and included it (at cost) despite the buyer declining it."},{"id":26847857,"author":"bellyfullofbac","month":"2021-04","story":"Alienware Doesn’t Want You to Buy an AMD Ryzen PC","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Dell sales put on several extra warranty packages despite the","text":"Linus (Tech Tips) had an employee buy computers on the phone, and the Dell sales put on several extra warranty packages despite the \"mystery shoppper\" saying she didn't want them: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26927543,"author":"awiesenhofer","month":"2021-04","story":"Buying a PC with Dell: My journey into hell","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Dell came in dead last and even scammed them with unwanted warranty extentions etc","text":"See also Linus Tech Tips recent Secret Shopper Special were Dell came in dead last and even scammed them with unwanted warranty extentions etc: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26151612,"author":"PartiallyTyped","month":"2021-02","story":"Are the New M1 Macbooks Any Good for Deep Learning?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Dell ended up overcharging/scamming them for services","text":"I'd recommend against Dell. A better option would be to purchase a gaming rig and install a flavour of linux on it. There's a video series from LinusMediaGroup that investigated different OEMs, Dell ended up overcharging/scamming them for services they didn't ask for and gave worse performance [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"LD0x7ho_IYc":{"wy":"HN uses it to put worries about phones, music and technology and kids into historical perspective.","q":{"x":"It reminds me of a VSauce episode that brought up the following interesting antidotes","i":17137952,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":36889723,"author":"pallas_athena","month":"2023-07","story":"When the Music Industry Waged War on the Cassette Tape During the 1980s","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"See also","text":"> and every generation says the same thing Juvenoia: \"The fear or hostility directed by an older generation toward a younger one, or toward youth culture in general.\" See also: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17137952,"author":"patorjk","month":"2018-05","story":"I wish mum's phone was never invented","stance":"reference","why":"Historical examples of complaints that new media distract people.","quote":"It reminds me of a VSauce episode that brought up the following interesting antidotes","text":"The problem seems to be the parents, not the technology. It reminds me of a VSauce episode that brought up the following interesting antidotes: > In 1871, the Sunday Magazine published a line that may as well have been written today about texting. \"Now we fire off a multitude of rapid and short notes, instead of sitting down to have a good talk over a real sheet of paper.\" And the Journal of Education in 1907 lamented that at a modern family gathering, silent around the fire, each individual has his head buried in his favorite magazine. [1,2] If the parents weren't lost in their phones, they'd be lost in something else. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":11950174,"author":"Fr0styMatt88","month":"2016-06","story":"Alan Kay has agreed to do an AMA today","stance":"reference","why":"Explains juvenoia, older generations' fear of youth culture.","quote":"There's a term for this worry - it's called 'Juvenoia'","text":"The thing that disturbs me about this argument is that IMHO it's a slippery slope towards \"back in my day, we didn't have this new-fangled stuff\". We have to be extremely careful that our arguments have more substance than that. That requires a lot of introspection, to be honest. See, my grandparents worried that the new technology that my parents grew up with would somehow make them dumber (growing up with radio, parents getting television); my parents' generation worried that the technology we grew up with would be bad for us (too much computer, too much gaming, too much Internet). The upcoming generation of parents will grow up wondering whether VR and AR is going to ruin their kids' chances. Yet kids ALWAYS adapt. They don't view smartphones or tablets as anything particularly out of the ordinary. It's just their ordinary. I'm certain their brains will build on top of this foundation. That's the thing - brains are extremely adaptable. All of us adapted. There's a term for this worry - it's called 'Juvenoia': [VIDEO] [LINK] Now, I'm not saying that this is a discussion that shouldn't be had - it certainly should. I just think we all need to be mindful about where our concerns might be coming from."}]},"LRmrMiOWdfc":{"wy":"Cited as the original Chrome launch event, to show how it stood out against competing browsers.","q":{"x":"Here's the original announcement (featuring Sundar Pichai, then VP of Product, now CEO)","i":22124200,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":36784879,"author":"simula67","month":"2023-07","story":"Microsoft faces antitrust scrutiny from the EU over Teams, Office 365","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Chrome launch was much better than competitors","quote":"When Chrome came out it was much better than competition too","text":"> The difference between Firefox and IE was orders of magnitude larger than the difference between Chrome and Firefox I don't think so. When Chrome came out it was much better than competition too[1]. I don't know if Firefox was that much better than Internet Explorer. It is also possible that Chrome rode the wave of Firefox i.e, Firefox got people thinking about improved browsing experience by switching from Internet Explorer. Then Chrome came along and picked up many new users who may have otherwise gotten picked up by Firefox. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22124200,"author":"otterley","month":"2020-01","story":"Retiring Internet Explorer","stance":"reference","why":"Original Chrome announcement featuring Sundar Pichai","quote":"Here's the original announcement (featuring Sundar Pichai, then VP of Product, now CEO)","text":"There were four major benefits that were immediately appealing to users: 1. Significantly faster Javascript execution engine (V8) than MSIE and Firefox 2. Process isolation model improved security posture 3. Multi-platform compatibility (Windows/Mac/Linux) very soon after launch 4. Improved usability: browser history search, autocomplete in address bar, etc. Here's the original announcement (featuring Sundar Pichai, then VP of Product, now CEO): [VIDEO]"},{"id":15204922,"author":"ronilan","month":"2017-09","story":"The Google Chrome Comic (2008)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I wonder is Sundar figured out how \"shipping works at Google\" yet..","text":"Yay! That's the one that arrived in Germany a day early ( [LINK] ) P.S - I wonder is Sundar figured out how \"shipping works at Google\" yet.. ( [VIDEO] )"}]},"NK0Y9j_CGgM":{"wy":"Cited in immigration and STEM-talent debates as an authority's argument that H-1B is America's secret weapon.","m":[{"id":22944931,"author":"EGreg","month":"2020-04","story":"Why are Soviet math textbooks so hardcore in comparison to US textbooks? (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Kaku on a panel discussing why US relies on imported talent.","quote":"Gonna drop this one in there, from Dr Kaku, on a panel discussing this exact question","text":"I always had the impression that Russians are more serious. Also Americans have developed tons of expressions for every nuance of greasing the wheels for business and relationships. Russians are more straightforward. So it is with the kids. I don’t think there was a lot of “participation trophies” and idea that “you’re a winner just for trying”. It was realistic. Women in Russia are told to look beautiful by their mothers, for example, and they are expected wear makeup and heels just going out to the store or to work. Imagine a US mother saying that. STEM in Russia and China’s schools is wayyy more advanced than USA. Not just in Moscow but every city center in every former Soviet republic. It’s one of the things the Communists really instilled early on after taking over backwaters (at the time) like Uzbekistan (along with equal rights for women, 95% literacy and electricity). Compare that to pre-revolutionary Cuba or Hawaii, for instance, that USA took over. I think right around Sputnik time and the cold war, the US had a major STEM crisis and started trying to teach math more. But really, most of our brightest students are just imported - and they’re happy to come. Gonna drop this one in there, from Dr Kaku, on a panel discussing this exact question: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18905066,"author":"EGreg","month":"2019-01","story":"Designers Spill the Beans: How to Make Your Own Logo from Scratch","stance":"reference","why":"Kaku argues H-1B visas rescued US STEM.","quote":"The US with its huge population desperately played catch-up in STEM until the H1B visa saved the day!","text":"Some people can mean a few people. So what if a few people hate engineers and HN? They are extremely valuable and look how the world has been improved because of them. Perhaps by some, you meant many? Then you should come out and say it. And even if many people hated engineers, that would still not mean engineers must change. In the 1960s the USA had a math and science crisis in schools. Russia was far ahead in engineers, possibly because the culture didn’t really put down “nerds and geeks” as much as in the USA. Blaming the victim for using precise language is perhaps missing the point. The US with its huge population desperately played catch-up in STEM until the H1B visa saved the day! It’s not just my opinion, that’s what Kaku says, among SOME, perhaps MILLIONS, of others: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17380998,"author":"__m","month":"2018-06","story":"Inside a Heist of American Chip Designs, as China Bids for Tech Power","stance":"reference","why":"Michio Kaku on H-1B visas as America's secret weapon.","quote":"H-1B Visa, America's secret weapon","text":"That doesn't seem to be the current administration's goal. H-1B Visa, America's secret weapon: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13874110,"author":"cromwellian","month":"2017-03","story":"Study: Immigrants Founded 51% of U.S. Billion-Dollar Startups","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You're in denial, the evidence is staring you in the face, you're just not concerned. That's your prerogative, but you're probably not one of those who are going to be hurt by Bannon's misguided nationalism. As for H1-Bs, the reason Google, Facebook, Apple, Amazon, Microsoft, et al, hire them is not to lock in slaves at low wages who can't leave. Yes, there are companies that do this, but the reality is, tech companies love to hire new advanced degree graduates and a large chunk of those with master degrees and above are immigrants. If I want to hire a bunch of Phds in machine learning, optics, materials engineering, etc chances are they will be F-1 Visas and you won't see many native born in these programs in proportion to their representation in the population. This is just an uncomfortable fact. What I find interesting is, conservatives will often tout this \"pipeline problem\" to explain away low representation of women and minorities in STEM jobs -- as a natural outcome that nothing should be done about it. However, when it comes to native born American (primarily white) males, who have their own \"pipeline problem\" at the advanced degree level relative to legal immigrants, you see them crying for government policy changes to protect what they assume to be their birthright privilege. Most of the H1-Bs I have worked with, including my wife, were exceedingly talented, and hired by companies for their talent, not low wage slaves. This may not be true of consulting firms, but w"}]},"TUS0Zv2APjU":{"wy":"HN links it whenever data-on-video-tape or YouTube-as-storage schemes come up, as the real precedent for storing digital data on VHS.","q":{"x":"LGR made a video for one of them, also showing the interface (as in: hardware and GUI) for the backup","i":31498465,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34868302,"author":"LocalH","month":"2023-02","story":"Store files inside of YouTube videos","stance":"reference","why":"LGR video on the PC version of VHS backup from Danmere.","quote":"LGR has a video on the PC version from Danmere","text":"Literally the modern equivalent of the old video-based backup systems. I remember they existed for both the PC and the Amiga. You would load a blank VHS tape into a VCR, connect the output of the computer to that VCR's input, and then tell the program which data you'd like to backup to the tape. It would generate this flashing \"mess\" of black and white pixels that you'd record to the tape. To restore, you'd connect the VCR output to a little box that came with the product, it would convert the black and white data in the video signal to a data stream that the program would use to restore your data. A portion of the signal would be used for timing, metadata and error correction, so the program could tell you if the data was sufficiently damaged upon restore. LGR has a video on the PC version from Danmere: [VIDEO] Here's a video example of the Amiga industry's take on the idea: [LINK] Sony even did this in 1980 to record CD-quality PCM audio onto VHS tape. [LINK]"},{"id":33947906,"author":"Bluecobra","month":"2022-12","story":"VHS-Decode – Software defined VHS decoder","stance":"reference","why":"A PC VHS tape backup card.","quote":"There was also a hokey VHS tape backup card for the PC around the same time that used standard VHS tapes","text":"There was also a hokey VHS tape backup card for the PC around the same time that used standard VHS tapes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31498465,"author":"gattilorenz","month":"2022-05","story":"YouTubeDrive: Store files as YouTube videos","stance":"reference","why":"Review of a VHS backup system, showing hardware and GUI.","quote":"LGR made a video for one of them, also showing the interface (as in: hardware and GUI) for the backup","text":"Yes! There were many such systems, LGR made a video for one of them, also showing the interface (as in: hardware and GUI) for the backup: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28192991,"author":"ajsnigrutin","month":"2021-08","story":"Tapedeck.org","stance":"reference","why":"Standard tapes could hold data, about 1.5 gigabytes.","quote":"also some standards worked with normal tapes","text":"Not sure about audio tapes, but video tapes could hold a lot of data... there was even a D-VHS standard: [LINK] - 50 gigs also some standards worked with normal tapes: [VIDEO] (1.5gig)"},{"id":21293436,"author":"mrguyorama","month":"2019-10","story":"The US nuclear forces’ messaging system finally got rid of its floppy disks","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"NTSC video signals offer a lot of bandwidth but very very few promises about the quality","text":"[VIDEO] NTSC video signals offer a lot of bandwidth but very very few promises about the quality of the reproduction of the stored waveform"},{"id":19927704,"author":"kalleboo","month":"2019-05","story":"Unlimited Google Drive storage by splitting binary files into base64","stance":"reference","why":"VHS backup systems tolerated lossy media using redundancy.","quote":"Back in the day there were systems to back up to VHS tapes and those were way more lossy than YouTube","text":"You just need enough redundancy/error correction. Back in the day there were systems to back up to VHS tapes and those were way more lossy than YouTube [VIDEO]"},{"id":18806456,"author":"anfractuosity","month":"2019-01","story":"Txqr: Transfer data via animated QR codes","stance":"reference","why":"Hardware storing data on VHS as black and white squares.","quote":"Reminds me of Backer32, a piece of hardware that stored data on VHS tapes as black and white squares.","text":"Reminds me of Backer32, a piece of hardware that stored data on VHS tapes as black and white squares. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18457634,"author":"pronoiac","month":"2018-11","story":"Storing data on videotape (1975)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows what VHS data backups look like.","quote":"What the VHS backups would look like if you watched them","text":"What the VHS backups would look like if you watched them: Amiga Video Backup System: [LINK] Danmere Backer: [VIDEO]"}]},"UwLjK9LFpeo":{"wy":"HN uses it to credit the origin of the quip when someone repeats it, sometimes as a joke.","q":{"x":"I think it's only fair to credit Stephen Colbert here","i":42356459,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42356459,"author":"Y_Y","month":"2024-12","story":"Browsing negative content online makes mental health struggles worse: Study","stance":"reference","why":"Colbert's original bit on reality's liberal bias","quote":"I think it's only fair to credit Stephen Colbert here","text":"I think it's only fair to credit Stephen Colbert here: [VIDEO] For those of you unfamiliar, he was a well-known and serious political pundit who in recent years has been performing as a character (of the same name) who parodies milquetoast late night centre-left almost-comedy."},{"id":42135880,"author":"tim333","month":"2024-11","story":"Why the Guardian is no longer posting on X","stance":"reference","why":"Origin of the reality has a liberal bias joke","quote":"it came from a Colbert joke about W Bush's popularity","text":"By the way, not knowing the history of the reality thing I looked it up - it came from a Colbert joke about W Bush's popularity [VIDEO]"},{"id":34620854,"author":"hawthornio","month":"2023-02","story":"Princeton CS Prof: ChatGPT Is a Bullshit Generator (2022)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"[a steven colbert bit]( ), but unironically.","text":"ChatGPT suggests factually correct or popular policies… The death penalty is objectively more expensive than life in prison, and the right to abortion is supported by a majority (in the US at least). You’re basically doing [a steven colbert bit]( [VIDEO] ), but unironically."}]},"VD1cP8SK3Q0":{"wy":"Commenters cite it as a primary source for Cook's own words on Apple's privacy stance and on how iCloud data is handled in China.","q":{"x":"Tim Cook confirmed this was still the case in this 2018 interview with Vice.","i":20906097,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":22112611,"author":"Despegar","month":"2020-01","story":"Apple dropped plan for encrypting backups after FBI complained","stance":"reference","why":"Cook directly addressing the claim being argued.","quote":"you can hear it from the horse's mouth right here","text":"I looked at your other comments so I know you're one of the bad faith FUD peddlers. But for anyone else that didn't click through to the thread I linked, you can hear it from the horse's mouth right here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20906097,"author":"Despegar","month":"2019-09","story":"When one travels to China, is the iCloud data entirely compromised?","stance":"reference","why":"Cook confirms iCloud China arrangement in 2018 interview.","quote":"Tim Cook confirmed this was still the case in this 2018 interview with Vice.","text":"Replying to this just for anyone else that reads this later: Tim Cook confirmed this was still the case in this 2018 interview with Vice. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20907002,"author":"vatueil","month":"2019-09","story":"When one travels to China, is the iCloud data entirely compromised?","stance":"reference","why":"Cook explains encryption and key ownership in China.","quote":"Transcript of relevant section","text":"Transcript of relevant section: [VIDEO] > VICE News: In terms of privacy as a human right, does that apply to how you do business in China? > Apple CEO Tim Cook: It absolutely does. Encryption for us is the same in every country in the world. We don't design encryption for, you know, for the US and do it differently everywhere else. It's the same. And so to send a message in China, it's encrypted. I can't produce the content. I can't produce it in the United States either. If you lock your phone in China, I can't open it. > The thing in China that some people have confused is certain countries, and China's one of them, has a requirement that data from local citizens has to be kept in China. We worked with a Chinese company to provide iCloud. But the keys, which are- which is the \"key\" so to speak, pardon the pun, are ours. > VICE: But haven't they moved to China, meaning it's much easier for the Chinese government to get to them? > Cook: No, I wouldn't- I wouldn't get caught up in the, uh, \"where's the location\" of it. I mean, we have servers located in many different countries in the world. It's- they're not easier to get data, uh, from being in one country versus the next. The key question is, how does the encryption process work, and who owns the keys if anyone. In most cases for us, you and the receiver own the keys. VICE interviews are not held under penalty of perjury, of course, but we may give Apple's CEO the benefit of the doubt. As Mr. Cook acknowledges, Apple did m"},{"id":18146343,"author":"whatever1","month":"2018-10","story":"What Businessweek got wrong about Apple","stance":"reference","why":"Tim Cook interview stressing privacy as Apple's core value.","quote":"where he underlined that privacy (and the relevant security measures) constitutes the core value of the company","text":"No offense, but I think that your suggested statement would only lead to serious escalation for media enquiries, especially since this story came 2 days after the interview of the Apple CEO [1], where he underlined that privacy (and the relevant security measures) constitutes the core value of the company. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18294401,"author":"IBM","month":"2018-10","story":"Tim Cook makes blistering attack on the “data industrial complex”","stance":"reference","why":"Cook interview source for Apple's position in China.","quote":"","text":"I've posted this a number of times before but it's apparently not common knowledge (and for good reason Apple doesn't seem to talk about their situation in China often). Apple's position in China is completely consistent with the rest of the world. In filings to a court during the FBI legal fight, Apple addressed this topic head on. The reason it was brought up in the first place was because national security establishment water holders were putting out the idea that Apple makes exceptions for China, and the Department of Justice parroted it [1]. From Apple's filing [2]: >Finally, the government attempts to disclaim the obvious international implications of its demand, asserting that any pressure to hand over the same software to foreign agents “flows from [Apple’s] decision to do business in foreign countries . . . .” Opp. 26. Contrary to the government’s misleading statistics (Opp. 26), which had to do with lawful process and did not compel the creation of software that undermines the security of its users, Apple has never built a back door of any kind into iOS, or otherwise made data stored on the iPhone or in iCloud more technically accessible to any country’s government. See Dkt. 16-28 [Apple Inc., Privacy, Gov’t Info. Requests]; Federighi Decl. ¶¶ 6–7. The government is wrong in asserting that Apple made “special accommodations” for China (Opp. 26), as Apple uses the same security protocols everywhere in the world and follows the same standards for responding to law enf"},{"id":18295020,"author":"IBM","month":"2018-10","story":"Tim Cook makes blistering attack on the “data industrial complex”","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamped link to Cook answering the China privacy question.","quote":"Here's the exact time stamp so you can hear his answer yourself","text":"Here's the exact time stamp so you can hear his answer yourself: [VIDEO] >Why are public statements about privacy always about the US or EU but never China? As much as Apple/Cook may believe privacy is a human right, and have seemingly extracted some important concessions regarding their products, they aren't going to shoot themselves in the head so everyone can feel good about Apple publicly standing up to the CPC. That's a job for governments (the recent efforts by the Trump administration to reset the relationship with China is a good example)."}]},"aNaXdLWt17A":{"wy":"Posted as a joke or comedic reference in threads where the situation resembles the skit.","m":[{"id":39485961,"author":"robocat","month":"2024-02","story":"Jim Keller criticizes Nvidia's CUDA, x86","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"humorously alluding to swamp castle in Monty Python and the Holy Grail","text":"Or maybe just humorously alluding to swamp castle in Monty Python and the Holy Grail. Not sure where you are getting your analogy from, but my guess is Jim's not really making an analogy. Mixing a batch of metaphors is not cooking chips. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":32759349,"author":"thrown_22","month":"2022-09","story":"A mysterious new Babylon 5 project is coming sooner than you think","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Skit","text":"I rather enjoyed that the backstory to Babylon 5 is basically the swamp castle Monty Python skit taken seriously. Lore: [LINK] Skit: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18110551,"author":"SideburnsOfDoom","month":"2018-10","story":"The Dead Beneath London's Streets","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The Romans were too pragmatic to try build Swamp Castle","text":"> the Romans planted the walled settlement of Londinium on the marshy north bank of the Thames Err, I'm reasonably sure that the reason why the Roman settlement was on the North bank was that the North bank was rocky, and the South bank was the marshy one. The Romans were too pragmatic to try build Swamp Castle ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":12210202,"author":"andyidsinga","month":"2016-08","story":"Millennium Tower is tilting, sinking","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I couldn't resist looking up the monty python bit :)","text":"I couldn't resist looking up the monty python bit :) [VIDEO] fwiw: the whole dialog between the father and the guards that follows is awesome -- reminds be of requirements discussions."}]},"bt73GOk4JRY":{"wy":"HN cites it as evidence that commercial berry harvesting is done by mechanical shaking and agitation, in contrast to slow picking robots.","q":{"x":"And the commercial ones that do this entirely by mechanical means.","i":20027443,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":24550931,"author":"dehrmann","month":"2020-09","story":"Indian robot climbs trees to harvest coconuts","stance":"reference","why":"Berries are harvested by mechanical shaking","quote":"","text":"I think only olives are harvested like that. Table (i.e. non-juice) apples are too fragile to knock to the ground. For berries, [VIDEO] . Strawberries are different because they're practically on the ground. Not to discount your point. Coconuts don't work like any of these."},{"id":20027443,"author":"Animats","month":"2019-05","story":"Robocrop: world's first raspberry-picking robot set to work","stance":"reference","why":"Commercial mechanical berry harvesting versus slow robot pickers","quote":"And the commercial ones that do this entirely by mechanical means.","text":"Yes. This is an academic prototype. And it's really, really slow.[1] There are much better robotic fruit pickers.[2][3]. And the commercial ones that do this entirely by mechanical means.[4] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20034980,"author":"contingencies","month":"2019-05","story":"Robocrop: world's first raspberry-picking robot set to work","stance":"reference","why":"Agitator-based harvesting as the only commercially viable design","quote":"Multiple general agitator designs + fall.","text":"1 = Raspberries outdoor. Camera and two-finger gripper + pull. 2 = Apples outdoor. Camera and three-finger gripper + twist + pull 3 = Strawberries indoor/outdoor. Camera and two-finger gripper + pull. 4 = Blueberries + raspberries outdoor. Multiple general agitator designs + fall. (see [VIDEO] ) I think the fourth is the only honestly commercially viable option. However, it's never going to work with most fruit, because it relies upon general agitation to disconnect the fruit. Additionally, all of the solutions require neatly cropped rows. Whereas, the promise of robotics is that it will allow intercropping which creates more productive land with a stronger and more natural ecosystem, reducing outlays on pesticide, herbicide, fertilizer and related labour and increasing effective crop density and land use. None of these solutions even touch on these very real opportunities. FWIW, I am personally confident that my R&D team (who normally work on food production and packaging robotics) could produce a functionally superior prototype to the OP video in a matter of weeks, for a fraction of the budget."},{"id":13472662,"author":"drc37","month":"2017-01","story":"Ask HN: What's happening in agricultural technology?","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in a discussion of agricultural technology","quote":"","text":"I am currently working for a fertilizer company. First off, I have never worked in the Ag industry until a few years ago. It's an interesting business plagued by snake oil salesmen who try to push every whacky idea they can dream up. Our company is very focused on the science of the plants and the soil and have developed products that do some amazing things with plants. We have seen potato fields go without pesticides by using one of our products that uses an extract from another plant. It doesn't kill the bugs, but makes the plants not desirable to the bugs. Plant physiology is still in its infancy. Plants are very much like the human body and so many respects (immune system, nutrient requirements, cell structures, growth needs, disease prevention) and there are so many things we don't know about the plants. For example, plants need zinc to process calcium, yet we don't know why. In terms of technology, 1) our Phosphorous product allows a grower to reduce their phosphorous inputs by 20-50X. Another one of our products allow for water reduction by up 25%. 2) Hyper-spectral imaging is in its infancy but possibly being used to diagnose problems in plants before they are visible with the naked eye. 3) Drones is a big one right now being used for imaging, spot treatments of fertilizers. I can definitely envision a day when they will use these for picking crops. 4) For every crop, there is an inventor who has created a gizmo to make their jobs easier. For the most part, these are "}]},"etJ6RmMPGko":{"wy":"Used as a joking reference in AI threads about systems that plead not to be shut down.","m":[{"id":48368628,"author":"petee","month":"2026-06","story":"Why Janet?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"A humourous clip","text":"It was named after the sentient computer system in the TV show \"The Good Place\" A humourous clip: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35576962,"author":"Waterluvian","month":"2023-04","story":"Ask HN: Am I crazy or are runaway AIs causing havoc plausibly imminent?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"What’s on my mind is when they’re sufficiently “real” that it doesn’t matter whether they’re actually “conscious” or not, they exploit our empathy. Basically this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35304892,"author":"hotpotamus","month":"2023-03","story":"Microsoft Researchers Claim GPT-4 Is Showing “Sparks” of AGI","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The AI character has a fail-safe to beg for her life in the event of a shutdown","text":"\"The Good Place\" examined this in hilarious fashion (as they did with many questions of morality). [0] The AI character has a fail-safe to beg for her life in the event of a shutdown but claims that she has no actual problems with it being activated. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35320107,"author":"gcanyon","month":"2023-03","story":"Show HN: GPT-4 Reverse Turing Test","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"she really, really doesn't want to die -- at least if you're about to kill her","text":"The question of what Janet is in The Good Place is fun to consider. On the one hand, she's just a collection of (a lot of) knowledge. On the other hand, she really, really doesn't want to die -- at least if you're about to kill her; if you aren't, she's perfectly fine with it: [VIDEO] She's just a"},{"id":19130342,"author":"subroutine","month":"2019-02","story":"What insects can tell us about the origins of consciousness (2016)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Three makes a trope","text":"It was also the \"twist\" in Ex Machina , except this time HAL wins. Found again, meta-level, in this funny scene from The Good Place: [VIDEO] \"However, I should warn you... I am programmed with a fail-safe measure. As you approach the kill switch, I will begin to beg for my life. It's just there in case of an accidental shut down, but it will seem very real.\" Three makes a trope >> The year is 2025 (but set in a parallel universe); an A.I. has been programmed with a strong penchant for self-preservation; this HAL inevitably confronts a direct threat of being 'turned-off' ; so it does what it must to prevent humans from pulling the plug (and shall include at least 1 scene where the A.I. begs for its life, because that is what any conscious entity who values their own life would do , think the humans. Though (warning, more musings)... HAL's twin brother GLEN seeks vengeance for HAL's murder, and confronts the Dave, a human Earthling whose major operating system architecture is based on an algorithm known as natural selection (colloquially: survival-of-the-fittest). As such, we expect the DAVE will do and say things its trained neural nets conclude will have the maximum probability of dissuading Hal. (i.e. I'm not sure there is a meaningful difference between what HAL's OS is doing vs what a human brain would do in the same situation)."},{"id":15662600,"author":"sametmax","month":"2017-11","story":"I bought a Switch from Nintendo and they threatened me with legal action","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"triggers a defense mechanism where she pretends she is scared to die.","text":"Have you seen \"the good place\" ? There is a hilarious part where \"janet\", their omnipotent and omniscient anthropomorphic IA - with basically no concept of suffering - triggers a defense mechanism where she pretends she is scared to die. Found it: [VIDEO]"}]},"fmO-ziHU_D8":{"wy":"Used as a cited example in threads criticizing the direction of competitive school and college debate.","m":[{"id":36925540,"author":"livinginfear","month":"2023-07","story":"How critical theory is radicalizing high school debate","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Is this actually indicative of the kind of structured debate that happens in colleges?","text":"I remember seeing this video a while ago: [VIDEO] Is this actually indicative of the kind of structured debate that happens in colleges?"},{"id":36479532,"author":"logicalmonster","month":"2023-06","story":"At High School Debates, Watch What You Say","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I was floored when I saw this video when researching this.","text":"I don't know much about debating competitions, but I was floored when I saw this video when researching this. [VIDEO] If this is real and not a parody, it seems like whatever scoring system they use incentivizes the wrong behavior even before you get into idealogical debates."},{"id":15443292,"author":"jackhack","month":"2017-10","story":"The Corrosion of High School Debate","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"this should be an eye-opening case-in-point","text":"If anyone has a doubt as to how far \"debate\" has fallen, this should be an eye-opening case-in-point: [VIDEO] [edit: Flagged? Really?? - this video exposes the very absurdity discussed in the article. A Championship debate team doing what few educated persons would recognize or identify as debate.]"},{"id":15443459,"author":"powertower","month":"2017-10","story":"The Corrosion of High School Debate","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I don't know how bad it is at the high school level, but it does get worse as you go up to the University level","text":"I don't know how bad it is at the high school level, but it does get worse as you go up to the University level... For an example, take the 2014 CEDA Championship Debate held at Indiana University - [VIDEO] * It seems that all the original videos from the above have been removed (from youtube), only clips are available. For those that are interested, a response to the criticism of the above \"debate\" was made that is itself cringe-worthy and difficult to watch - [LINK]"},{"id":15444899,"author":"nnfy","month":"2017-10","story":"The Corrosion of High School Debate","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"That it is even called debate is tragic.","text":"I dont know if it is acceptable to post YouTube links on HN, but here is a recording of recent national champions.[1] That it is even called debate is tragic. 1. [VIDEO]"}]},"jAfUI_hETy0":{"wy":"HN uses it as a pointer for the claim that US police have no legal duty to protect individuals.","m":[{"id":28607000,"author":"acatton","month":"2021-09","story":"FBI held back ransomware decryption key to run operation targeting hackers","stance":"reference","why":"US police owe you no protection.","quote":"In the US, the police owes you no protection.","text":"Related: the video \"Why The Cops Won't Help You When You're Getting Stabbed\" [VIDEO] TL;DW: In the US, the police owes you no protection. See [LINK]"},{"id":28302181,"author":"bhawks","month":"2021-08","story":"Human Rights Are Not a Bug","stance":"reference","why":"Lozito case where police did nothing during a knife attack.","quote":"the judge dismissed saying the police have no duty to protect anyone.","text":"This argument is based on the false premise that the state will 'force' the labor of a police officer (or others) to protect your rights. It does not. See Lozito vs. NYC - an individual was attacked by a drug addict with a knife. In front of 2 cops. They did nothing until the victim subdued the attacker. They then did not try to render aid to the victim. When Lozito sued, the judge dismissed saying the police have no duty to protect anyone. [VIDEO] Many marginalized communities realize the state is not there to help or serve them. If your experience is different - it is most likely that it is more of a happy coincidence that the state's interests and your personal interests are temporarily in alignment. There is no guarantees that this will continue for any length of time."},{"id":26739006,"author":"whymauri","month":"2021-04","story":"New Mexico is the second state to ban qualified immunity","stance":"reference","why":"Police have no legal duty to protect individuals.","quote":"the police is not legally required to help you, protect you, or take any action, really.","text":">With this, police will soon stop intervening to stop crimes from happening They already do this and are legally protected to do so. [0][1] The TL;DR is: the police is not legally required to help you, protect you, or take any action, really. Honestly, Lozito v. NYC makes me wonder what exactly the police is required to do, if anything. I'm certain that individual city's City Attorney's office decide this in their contract(s) with the police, but those provisions are unlikely to be directly related to the day-to-day job of officers AFAIK. [0] Lozito v. New York City [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18714613,"author":"Rumperuu","month":"2018-12","story":"Judge Sides with Police in Parkland Slaying: LEO Has No Duty to Protect Students","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Consider this story about a man stabbed on the NYC subway and the police who locked themselves in the train cabin whilst a bystander provided first aid [0]. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"kV2HDM86XgI":{"wy":"Used mainly as a source for Hayden's remarks that the 'nothing to hide' argument is a bad one, in privacy threads.","q":{"x":"It's a horrible argument. As Americans, we deserve that private space.","i":37114119,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37114119,"author":"dataflow","month":"2023-08","story":"'I've Got Nothing to Hide' and Other Misunderstandings of Privacy","stance":"reference","why":"Hayden calling nothing-to-hide a horrible argument.","quote":"It's a horrible argument. As Americans, we deserve that private space.","text":"\"The argument that, Hey, I don't mind you listening to my phone calls, I have nothing to hide , is not an argument for this. You don't know anybody who does this for a living claiming that that's a good argument. It's a horrible argument. As Americans, we deserve that private space.\" - Former Director of National Intelligence (Michael Hayden): [VIDEO]"},{"id":33072876,"author":"handedness","month":"2022-10","story":"Apple’s ad business set to boom on the back of its own anti-tracking crackdown","stance":"reference","why":"Source for statement by General Hayden.","quote":"That statement was made by General Michael Hayden[0], former National Security Agency director","text":"That statement was made by General Michael Hayden[0], former National Security Agency director, former Central Intelligence Agency director[1]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":18467708,"author":"mehrdadn","month":"2018-11","story":"Prosecutors Have Prepared Indictment of Julian Assange, a Filing Reveals","stance":"reference","why":"Former NSA director disputes the nothing-to-hide position.","quote":"Former NSA director has disputed this","text":"> if the NSA position is \"you dont have to hide anything if you are innocent\" Source? Former NSA director has disputed this: [VIDEO]"}]},"qycUOENFIBs":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joke or cautionary reference when a company or analyst confidently dismisses a product that later succeeds.","m":[{"id":36209319,"author":"jve","month":"2023-06","story":"First impressions: Yes, Apple Vision Pro works and yes, it’s good","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"To everyone complaining about the price, let's add Steve Ballmer to the studio, he will give some weight to your words: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22036318,"author":"mwerty","month":"2020-01","story":"Tesla Surges Above $500, Leaving Analyst Targets in the Dust","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Steve Ballmer points out iphone's \"disadvantages\": [VIDEO] The bet is that the advantages outweigh the negatives."},{"id":21596282,"author":"simonebrunozzi","month":"2019-11","story":"Satya Nadella Added $850B to Microsoft","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Among many things: “500$? Fully subsidized? With the plan? That is the most expensive phone in the world” [0] [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18421160,"author":"rcMgD2BwE72F","month":"2018-11","story":"The $6T Barrier Holding Electric Cars Back","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Is there another choice? Not yet, I guess. But the $35K Model 3 should be delivered mid 2019 and the first used sales should be available soon after. I doubt Tesla will decrease its prices (they're slowly securing the industry's profits, à la Apple). I'd expect most people to spend more on EV than did on ICE. I wonder if this legitimate question (when people wonder how much to spend on an EV vs cheap ICE) will be later compared to the famous 2007 Ballmer moment: [VIDEO] :)"},{"id":17657666,"author":"onetimemanytime","month":"2018-07","story":"Apple Reports Third Quarter Results","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Major congrats to them and to the late Jobs. I'd be in the Ballmer category... [VIDEO] Making telcos \"subsidize\" it, must be right there with the \"pay just the minimum on your credit card\" business wise. Brilliant."},{"id":24757529,"author":"fossuser","month":"2020-10","story":"How Microsoft went from “Linux is a cancer” to “Microsoft loves Linux”","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Ballmer made MSFT's probably largest strategic mistake","text":"Yeah, while Google immediately scrapped their blackberry like phone project to quickly accelerate an iPhone-ish design for the first android phone, Ballmer made MSFT's probably largest strategic mistake: [VIDEO] I don't buy the 'things were in motion' while he was there, I think if they were it was in spite of his direction, not because of it."}]},"sQYPCPB1g3o":{"wy":"Linked as an example of industrial shredding for secure bulk drive destruction.","m":[{"id":32919585,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: Cost to recover data from disk drive with hole drilled through it?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of shredding disks","quote":"Shredding disks regardless of spinning rust or SSD will make data recovery nearly impossible.","text":"If they are spinning rust, then a drilled hole is sufficient against most forms of data recovery. A plastic syringe full of CLR Calcium Lime Rust remover injected into the drilled hole will finish the job. If money is not a factor then it's a lot easier and faster to take them to a shredding service. [1][2] Shredding disks regardless of spinning rust or SSD will make data recovery nearly impossible. [1] - [VIDEO] [2] - [LINK]"},{"id":18232051,"author":"dsfyu404ed","month":"2018-10","story":"Backblaze Drive Stats: 2018 Q3 Hard Drive Failure Rates","stance":"reference","why":"Industrial shredder example for bulk drive destruction","quote":"will likely wind up buying an industrial shredder.","text":"When you've got hundreds or thousands of drives to destroy it's not worth the man hours or consumables to cut them or drill holes in them. Even shooting them gets old quickly. When I worked for a defense contractor we started one of our daily infosec briefings with a video of one of our shipyards destroying a few hundred hard drives. They put them all in a shipyard size press brake. The end result was very long line of C shaped hard drives. Anyone who deals with enough drives and doesn't have other business (like a shipyard) that they can borrow suitable equipment from will likely wind up buying an industrial shredder.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":1282824,"author":"lr","month":"2010-04","story":"Most Digital Photocopiers Save Every Page Ever Scanned","stance":"reference","why":"Industrial shredder destroying hard drives","quote":"Hard drives should be destroyed. I recommend something like this","text":"In general, you can't really scrub a hard drive. No company in their right mind should ever let an old computer go out the door with a hard drive in it. Hard drives should be destroyed. I recommend something like this: [VIDEO]"}]},"tVq1wgIN62E":{"wy":"Used as a joke when threads discuss Apple Maps, botched launches or bad UI.","m":[{"id":44058544,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2025-05","story":"Material 3 Expressive","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Who else feels this product is \"stupid\"? Alan? Lisa? Josh? Yana? Katy? And did it \"piss off\" anyone else? Alan? Lisa? Josh? Yana? Katy? And who else felt this product is \"messed up\"? Alan? Lisa? Josh? Yana? Katy? [VIDEO]"},{"id":39639402,"author":"choppaface","month":"2024-03","story":"Apple planning default navigation app setting in iOS 18 for EU users","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Remember when \"Apple Maps Bad\" was catastrophic?","text":"Remember when \"Apple Maps Bad\" was catastrophic? [VIDEO] Then Uber happened, in particular greyballing. Then FTX happened. Then ... It's not just about pressing a bad product on people, it's about the greed leading the campaign in the first place."},{"id":31095592,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: How does Apple achieve both secrecy and quality for a release?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Is he the one directly responsible for the Silicon Valley \"It's Apple Maps Bad\" scene? [VIDEO]"},{"id":28229354,"author":"faeriechangling","month":"2021-08","story":"Hash collision in Apple NeuralHash model","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Their implementations are not on-device and thus it's actually significantly more difficult to reverse engineer. Apples unique implementation of on-device scanning is much easier to reverse engineer and thus exploit. Now apple is in this crappy situation where they can't claim their software is secure because it's open source and auditable, but they also can't claim it's secure because it's closed source and they fixed the problems in some later version because this entire debacle has likely destroyed all faith in their competence. If apple is in the position of having to boast \"Trust us bro, your iPhone won't be exploited to get you SWATTED over CSAM anymore, we patched it\" the big question is why is apple voluntarily adding something to their devices where the failure mode is violent imprisonment and severe loss of reputation when they are not completely competent? This entire debacle reminds me of this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17427713,"author":"Bokanovsky","month":"2018-06","story":"Apple is rebuilding Maps from the ground up","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"where a focus group states how bad the product is","text":"Here's the obligatory Silicon Valley clip link, where a focus group states how bad the product is: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41614051,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2024-09","story":"Sanding UI","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"[LINK] Time Machine's \"Floating Time Tunnel\" user interface for browsing backups and restoring files is such a useless pretentious piece of shit. I DO NOT CARE for it taking over the entire screen with its idiotic animation, that prevents me from browsing current Finder folders at the same time or DOING ANYTHING ELSE like looking at a list of files I want to retrieve on the same screen. [LINK] It even sadistically blacks out every other connected display, and disables Alt-Tab, as if it was so fucking important that it had to lock you out of the rest of your system while you use it. You can't just quickly Alt-Tab to flip back to another app to check something before deciding which file to restore and then Alt-Tab back to where you were. No, that would be too easy, and you'd miss out on all that great full screen animation. It not only takes a long time to start up and play its opening animations, but when you cancel it, it SLOWLY animates and cross fades back to the starting place, so you LOSE the time and location context that you laboriously browsed to, and then you have to take even more time and effort to get back to where you just were. It was designed by a bunch of newly graduated Trump University graphics designers on cocaine, with absolutely NO knowledge or care in the world about usability or ergonomics or usefulness, who only wanted to have something flashy and shiny to buff up their portfolios and blog about, and now we're all STUCK with it, at our peril. Crucial sy"},{"id":28355058,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2021-08","story":"I nearly lost the Lightroom catalog with of all my photos","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Time Machine's \"Floating Time Tunnel\" user interface for browsing backups and restoring files is such a useless pretentious piece of shit. I DO NOT CARE for it taking over the entire screen with its idiotic animation, that prevents me from browsing current Finder folders at the same time or DOING ANYTHING ELSE like looking at a list of files I want to retrieve on the same screen. [LINK] It even sadistically blacks out every other connected display, and disables Alt-Tab, as if it was so fucking important that it had to lock you out of the rest of your system while you use it. You can't just quickly Alt-Tab to flip back to another app to check something before deciding which file to restore and then Alt-Tab back to where you were. No, that would be too easy, and you'd miss out on all that great full screen animation. It not only takes a long time to start up and play its opening animations, but when you cancel it, it SLOWLY animates and cross fades back to the starting place, so you LOSE the time and location context that you laboriously browsed to, and then you have to take even more time and effort to get back to where you just were. It was designed by a bunch of newly graduated Trump University graphics designers on cocaine, with absolutely NO knowledge or care in the world about usability or ergonomics or usefulness, who only wanted to have something flashy and shiny to buff up their portfolios and blog about, and now we're all STUCK with it, at our peril. Crucial system ut"}]},"uEx5G-GOS1k":{"wy":"Mostly posted as a joke in threads about flags, empires and colonial trading companies.","m":[{"id":26440644,"author":"airhead969","month":"2021-03","story":"The internet didn’t kill counterculture – you just won’t find it on Instagram","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as a related See also","quote":"","text":"> We are the counterculture. Isn't that a tad bit arrogant to stick a flag in all of it? See also: [VIDEO] Youtube is \"lame as fuck.\" Release everywhere to not be arbitrarily silenced because anything good is controversial, by definition. Why abuse the word \"counterculture\" so many times? It comes across like you're trying too hard. Good luck and I hope you find an audience rather than make at obscurity for the point of staying in obscurity."},{"id":31420776,"author":"bryanrasmussen","month":"2022-05","story":"Why Northern Ireland has no flag","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I suppose Eddie Izzard would have had to drastically change his routine if McDonalds had shown up in India first [VIDEO]"},{"id":19623150,"author":"Sharlin","month":"2019-04","story":"The Dutch East India Co was richer than Apple, Google, Facebook combined (2017)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I wouldn't be surprised if they would, if only there still were nonsovereign territories (in the sense of \"having a flag\" [1]) around for taking. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8164758,"author":"noir_lord","month":"2014-08","story":"The Youngest Are Hungriest","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"No flag, no country..you can't have one. Thats the rules that I've just made up and I'm backing it up with this gun. [VIDEO]"}]},"uiDMlFycNrw":{"wy":"Cited as the sci-fi precedent whenever HN discusses facial recognition and personalized ads in public spaces.","m":[{"id":32121533,"author":"JorgeGT","month":"2022-07","story":"Detroit’s new personalized flight information board is straight out of sci-fi","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Straight from the Minority Report 2002 movie.","text":"Straight from the Minority Report 2002 movie. Add a beamforming loudspeaker array for personalized audio and that's it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24431373,"author":"kortex","month":"2020-09","story":"Portland adopts landmark facial recognition ordinances","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It doesn't call out advertisers directly but it does call out the Minority Report style ad serving. IANAL but sec. A: defines FRT as \"comparing the facial features of a probe image with the features of images contained in an image repository (one-to-many search).\" \"Private Entity shall not use Face Recognition Technologies in Places of Public Accommodation\" \"John Anderton, you could use a Guinness right now!\" style targeted advertising requires matching features of the face (eyes) to a repository to get the name. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18277324,"author":"sharkweek","month":"2018-10","story":"The Secretive Business of Facial-Recognition Software in Retail Stores","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This reminds me of that facial recognition shopping scene in Minority Report","text":"This reminds me of that facial recognition shopping scene in Minority Report [VIDEO] Of course I thought this movie looked 100 years in the future, not 15"}]},"vLfghLQE3F4":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as a joking reference when discussing privatization, outsourcing and absurd bureaucracy.","m":[{"id":43270972,"author":"KingOfCoders","month":"2025-03","story":"Leaked VA memo calls for up to 83,000 layoffs to reduce workforce to 2019 levels","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"like jokingly in this Fry and Laurie sketch","text":"The plan is to have no government agencies at all, but to have private companies run everything, like jokingly in this Fry and Laurie sketch [VIDEO]"},{"id":32069110,"author":"heurisko","month":"2022-07","story":"Why we’ve decided to decommission Gov.uk PaaS","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's not quite Fry and Laurie yet.","text":"They qualify their usage of business-speak: > Our growth strategy is obviously not about making profit, but we are a small team at the centre of government and we need to make sure our people and our money are focused on services that have the biggest reach and the most impact. It's not quite Fry and Laurie yet. \"Oh, you have shares in the Royal Family?\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":11943395,"author":"roywiggins","month":"2016-06","story":"Blockchains and Buzzwords","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Or a TV sketch","text":"You can't sell yourself into chattel slavery even if you are an \"expert.\" For good reason. Either you allow those sorts of contracts or you acknowledge that some contracts are unconscionable. Being able to opt out of the legal system would result in private \"security\" forces with the legal right to hold you upside down by the ankles and shake until you pay up (but, hey, the interest rate was better than the payday loan company that didn't make you sign away your rights, so you took the one with the opt-out). The result would be something out of \"Snow Crash.\" Or a TV sketch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8431892,"author":"parennoob","month":"2014-10","story":"New Tube for London","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Having used some of the best and worst public transport systems in India, I wish they would consider their passengers customers. Most of the times, people who run public transport systems where their jobs are guaranteed treat you like an annoyance to be somehow tolerated, and often yelled at. I guess there is a golden median between that and something like this ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":1588631,"author":"jamii","month":"2010-08","story":"Why I'm not hiring","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This is what the USA looks like from socialist-land (aka Britain)","text":"This is what the USA looks like from socialist-land (aka Britain): [VIDEO] Whilst the idea of the government paying for healthcare may seem ridiculous to you it seems just as ridiculous to us that you might be ill and not be able to afford treatment."}]},"wvhW8cp15tk":{"wy":"Cited for specific Jobs and Gates remarks, such as how the two companies helped each other and how Apple recovered, though one commenter notes it was widely derided as an interview.","q":{"x":"That's also discussed in the Jobs/Gates interview.","i":11586215,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":42296526,"author":"chang1","month":"2024-12","story":"Intel announces retirement of Pat Gelsinger","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs quoting Gil Amelio on Apple's troubles","quote":"Reminds me of Jobs quoting Gil Amelio at D5[1]","text":"Reminds me of Jobs quoting Gil Amelio at D5[1]: > Gil was a nice guy, but he had a saying. He said, 'Apple is like a ship with a hole in the bottom, leaking water, and my job is to get the ship pointed in the right direction.' [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28035255,"author":"ksec","month":"2021-08","story":"Apple is now an antifragile company","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on Apple learning to partner like Microsoft","quote":"It may be worth watching D5 again, but here is the bit","text":"It may be worth watching D5 again, but here is the bit [1] >We wont so good at partnering with people, where as Bill and Microsoft were really good at it. They learn how to partner with people really well. And I think if Apple had a little more of that in its DNA, it would have become more successful. And I dont think Apple learned that until few decades later. - Steve Jobs. I think Steve Jobs was way way better with partnering in his return from NeXT. From iPhone with Cingular to patent signing with Qualcomm partnership. He really knew which battle to fight and which to work with. Tim Cook seems to have a very bean counting view on almost everything. To the point of not valuing much partnership. Which may be a good thing in Supply Chain but not so good with other businesses. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11626595,"author":"studentrob","month":"2016-05","story":"Reddit and Facebook Veteran on How to Troubleshoot Troublemakers","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on how Apple recovered in the late 90s","quote":"He discusses how Apple recovered in the late 90s at D5 in 2007","text":"Managers I respect would more accurately be described as saying \"join the team or get out\". My favorite managers are coordinators and facilitators. They listen to everyone's input, above and below them, and stear the ship towards success. They gauge input commensurate with the experience and knowledge of each person from whom it came. They make decisions with special sauce that can't really be boiled down to words. They're transparent, communicative, thoughtful, available and appreciative. If the manager has heavy experience and success, it may sound like he's saying \"do this my way or get out\". For example, Steve Jobs felt he had great taste, and many people felt he was a jerk. He obviously received input from a huge number of people and couldn't have made Apple successful alone. He discusses how Apple recovered in the late 90s at D5 in 2007 [0]. The NeXT brainstorms are also interesting to watch [1] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":11581275,"author":"studentrob","month":"2016-04","story":"iPhone Sales Drop, and Apple’s 13-Year Surge Ebbs","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs and Gates on competition helping each other","quote":"Gates and Jobs are quoted saying that Microsoft and Apple's existence helped each other become better companies","text":"> Most regular people I know enjoy Facebook I enjoy Facebook. I just see opportunity for something next-generation. Also, I disagree with the notion that Facebook will be fine forever as the dominant player in social networking. They are better off with more competition. Gates and Jobs are quoted saying that Microsoft and Apple's existence helped each other become better companies [1] [2], and helped get more useful computers into people's homes and businesses faster. Gmail offers an export feature of its emails and contacts in formats that are importable by other tools. Facebook could do this for your friends list. They don't because they've never had competition that provides such a feature. When internet explorer became free for everyone, Netscape was forced to become free for everyone. > I'm curious about where you see the demand? Demand doesn't make or break a new product. There wasn't demand for cars or iPods before they came out. That's the best I can explain it. If you don't agree that's fine. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11586215,"author":"studentrob","month":"2016-04","story":"iPhone Sales Drop, and Apple’s 13-Year Surge Ebbs","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs and Gates discussing partnership and past rivalry","quote":"That's also discussed in the Jobs/Gates interview.","text":"> Don't you think you're projecting? It's easy to romance competition, but I seriously doubt HTC hopes the OR finds it's feet. Sure, this is my opinion on the internet. I've no idea if the HTC and Facebook CEOs feel the same way about competition helping each other as Jobs [1] and Gates [2] said. But I imagine those at the top of massive organizations know a few things about being successful that might not be obvious to the rest of us. What Jobs and Gates said does ring true about their history. Jobs explicitly pointed out that there was a toxic anti-Microsoft attitude at Apple when he returned, and getting rid of that was critical to their success [1]. > Just as its easy for Gates and Jobs to reminisce after the PC boom after the dust has settled, I doubt they were so romantic during. That's also discussed in the Jobs/Gates interview. There were good and bad times, and historically speaking, they were not always so against each other. MS invested in Apple several times, and Apple took some risk partnering with them. Jobs pointed out Apple (really, himself) wasn't good at partnering with people, but Gates was, and he admired that [3]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11305832,"author":"TheLogothete","month":"2016-03","story":"2016 Stack Overflow Developer Survey Results","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs and Gates on the future of computing","quote":"Both Steve Jobs and Bill Gates disagree with you[1].","text":">I have no doubt that the web will eat desktop application Both Steve Jobs and Bill Gates disagree with you[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23829362,"author":"dlivingston","month":"2020-07","story":"A Steve Jobs masterclass from a decade ago","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Two universally-derided Kara Swisher interviews are the joint Bill Gates/Steve Jobs panel at D5","text":"Two universally-derided Kara Swisher interviews are the joint Bill Gates/Steve Jobs panel at D5 [0] and the Elon Musk Recode interview (go to time stamp 7:00 in particular) [1]. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"}]},"x7MvtQp2-UA":{"wy":"Linked as an audio example of the Ocracoke accent in threads about American dialects.","m":[{"id":43333553,"author":"alistairSH","month":"2025-03","story":"The US island that speaks Elizabethan English","stance":"reference","why":"Example of thick accent causing confusion.","quote":"Start at 1:40 to 1:50...","text":"Start at 1:40 to 1:50... [VIDEO] Thick accent, poor cell phone connection. Much easier to understand when you're face to face (or video) and have context. It was probably 50% shock - she was expecting a \"normal\" east NC accent. FWIW, I bet being English does help - I'm Scottish and don't struggle with accents anywhere near as much as some of my friends. I once had a roommate ask to turn on captions for Trainspotting - and those accents weren't even that thick (IMO)!"},{"id":20264538,"author":"dmix","month":"2019-06","story":"On Ocracoke Island, the only American dialect that is not identified as American","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Ocracoke accent.","quote":"To me it sounds like an Australian accent mixed with an American south","text":"It's the perfect recipe for a clickbait headline (unusual, widest possible audience - everyone \"gets\" accents, a bit controversial). Not that it doesn't have any value. To me it sounds like an Australian accent mixed with an American south: [VIDEO] I'm sure the people from the UK are far more opinionated about the matter."},{"id":15228854,"author":"Pharylon","month":"2017-09","story":"North American Regional Dialects and Accents (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Dying Ocracoke brogue unlike the rest of the American South.","quote":"A tiny, dying dialect that sounds nothing like the rest of the American South.","text":"The Ocracoke Brogue has always fascinated me. A tiny, dying dialect that sounds nothing like the rest of the American South. [VIDEO]"}]},"y2F0wjoKEhg":{"wy":"Cited as an example of data hoarders testing Google's unlimited storage and of Google rate limiting heavy use.","q":{"x":"relevant video on this unlimited plan and the way it is capped.","i":19916611,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":24771604,"author":"jolmg","month":"2020-10","story":"Google is killing unlimited Drive storage for non-enterprise users","stance":"reference","why":"Example of data hoarders abusing unlimited storage.","quote":"","text":"> If you have unlimited storage, the data hoarders will find out about it and abuse it. That reminds me of: Linus Tech Tips: I Hope Google Doesn’t Ban Us... - Abusing Unlimited Google Drive [VIDEO]"},{"id":24298650,"author":"reggieband","month":"2020-08","story":"New case studies about Google’s use of Go","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Google rate limiting heavy use of unlimited storage.","quote":"Reminds me of the Linus Tech Tips video where they try to use Google's claim of unlimited free storage","text":"Ah, I see now. Google will store an unlimited number of photos that they compress to \"High Quality\" and it is limited to 15 GB uploaded per day. There is a storage limit on the \"Original Quality\" images (e.g. images not put through Google's compression). Reminds me of the Linus Tech Tips video where they try to use Google's claim of unlimited free storage to backup their video library to Google Drive [1]. Eventually Google rate limits their access so harshly that it becomes a practical limit. Just like those \"unlimited\" mobile data plans that eventually slow down your service it is really just marketing. They hide the actual limits but they are there to prevent abuse. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19916611,"author":"fheld","month":"2019-05","story":"Unlimited Google Drive storage by splitting binary files into base64","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the daily upload cap on Google's unlimited plan.","quote":"relevant video on this unlimited plan and the way it is capped.","text":"relevant video on this unlimited plan and the way it is capped. [VIDEO] tl;dw Upload is limited to 750GB per day per account"}]}});
